= Classical mechanics
{wiki}

<Classical mechanics> describes the motion of bodies by assigning <positions> and <velocities> and specifying how their interactions change those quantities. A <point mass> is the simplest model: its size and internal structure are ignored, leaving a <mass> and a moving <position>. A <rigid body> retains orientation and <rotational kinetic energy>. Choosing a model means deciding which features affect the question being asked; a sliding block may be treated as a <point mass>, whereas predicting its <rolling without slipping> requires its <moment of inertia>.

Three complementary questions organize the subject. <Newton's second law> asks how the net <force> determines the instantaneous <acceleration>. The <work-energy theorem> asks how that <force>, acting through a <displacement>, changes <kinetic energy>. <Conservation of mechanical energy> asks which combinations of <position> and <velocity> are accessible when the interactions admit a time-independent <potential energy> and no other <force> supplies net <power>. These descriptions are compatible, but answer different questions: an <energy> calculation may find a maximum <speed> without first solving for <position> at every <time>.

The <harmonic oscillator> below makes this unity visible. A restoring <force> produces a repeating <position> trace; the corresponding <phase plane> has a closed <ellipse>; <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> exchange while their sum stays fixed. <Lagrangian mechanics> and <Hamiltonian mechanics> provide further ways to organize the same dynamics, especially when coordinates or constraints make individual <forces> awkward to track.

The scope of <classical mechanics> also matters. <Newtonian mechanics> approximates motion at <speeds> small compared with the <speed of light>; <special relativity> changes that description when this approximation fails. <Quantum mechanics> is needed for phenomena for which a classical assignment of simultaneous <position> and <momentum> is inadequate. These limitations do not prevent <classical mechanics> from being a useful predictive model within its regime. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter1.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 1: scope and limitations]{ref}

= Trajectory
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf r(t)$}

= Inclined plane
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Variable-mass system
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf F+(\mathbf u-\mathbf v)\dot m$}
{wiki}

A body exchanging <mass> with material of inertial <velocity> $\mathbf u$ satisfies the displayed <momentum conservation> law for one stream, with signed $\dot m$ positive for entry and negative for exit. The <force> $\mathbf F$ excludes the incoming or outgoing momentum flux. A short time interval gives $d(m\mathbf v)/dt=\mathbf F+\mathbf u\dot m$, proving the formula. For stationary incoming matter, $d(m\mathbf v)/dt=\mathbf F$; for matter leaving at the body's own <velocity>, $m\dot{\mathbf v}=\mathbf F$. The <rocket equation> and accreting-drop motion are applications.

= Accreting raindrop with quadratic drag
{parent=Variable-mass system}
{title2=$\dot a=cv/4,\quad\dot v=g-3(c+k)v^2/(4a)$}

For a spherical drop of constant <mass density> $\rho$, radius $a>0$ and downward <velocity> $v>0$, suppose its volume grows at rate $c\pi a^2v$ by collecting stationary mist and its upward <quadratic drag> is $k\rho\pi a^2v^2$, with $c,k>0$. The drop <mass> is $4\pi\rho a^3/3$. Its incoming <mass accretion rate> and the <variable-mass system> momentum law give the displayed equations. The accreted matter must be accelerated from rest, producing the additional $c$ term alongside drag.

= Speed-radius attractor of an accreting raindrop
{parent=Accreting raindrop with quadratic drag}
{title2=$v^2/a\longrightarrow8g/(7c+6k),\quad\dot v\longrightarrow cg/(7c+6k)$}

For the <accreting raindrop with quadratic drag>, set $q=v^2/a$ and $B=7c/8+3k/4$. Its exact equation is $\dot q=2(v/a)(g-Bq)$. Dividing by $\dot a=cv/4$ gives $q(a)=g/B+(q(a_0)-g/B)(a_0/a)^{7+6k/c}$. Thus $q$ stays between its positive initial value and positive equilibrium. Since $d\sqrt a/dt=c\sqrt q/8$, the radius grows without finite-time divergence and tends to infinity. The power correction then vanishes, proving both limits. Positive drag makes the limiting <acceleration> strictly less than $g/7$.

= Inertia
{parent=Classical mechanics}

<Inertia> is a body's persistence in its state of rest or constant <velocity> when the net <external force> vanishes in an <inertial frame>. It is not an extra <force> that pushes a moving body forward. <Newton's first law> describes this persistence; for a <point mass>, <Newton's second law> quantifies how a nonzero net <external force> changes its <momentum>. At constant <mass>, $\mathbf F_{\mathrm{net}}=m\mathbf a$, so a larger <mass> gives a smaller <acceleration> for the same net <force>.

A moving cart does not need a forward net <force> to maintain its <velocity>. Ordinary carts slow because <friction> and <drag force> produce an opposing net <external force>; reducing those interactions reveals <Newton's first law> more clearly. The <air-track test of inertial motion> separates this persistence from the initial push. In <overdamped particle dynamics>, the inertial <acceleration> term is small compared with <drag force> over the <time> scales considered; that approximation does not mean that <inertia> has disappeared.

= Mechanical configuration space
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$Q$}

= Configuration space
{synonym}

The <configuration space> of a mechanical system is the space of its allowed positions or generalized coordinates. Its tangent bundle describes configurations and velocities, and its cotangent bundle describes configurations and conjugate momenta. For smooth holonomic constraints it is a manifold on which geometric Lagrangian and Hamiltonian descriptions can be built. Singular constraints or symmetry quotients can require strata rather than one global smooth manifold.

= Relational shape space
{parent=Classical mechanics}

The <relational shape space> of a particle configuration is obtained by removing collective translations and rotations; here scale is retained. For generic collision-free, noncollinear configurations of $N\ge3$ labelled particles in three dimensions, the resulting configuration dimension is $3N-6$. Collinear configurations have nontrivial rotational stabilizers and require singular strata. Removing position and orientation does not automatically remove conserved angular momentum or its centrifugal effect; a dynamically faithful reduction must retain the appropriate momentum-level data.

= Simple pendulum
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$\ddot\theta+(g/\ell)\sin\theta=0$}

A point mass constrained to a fixed-length massless arm under uniform gravitational <acceleration> is a simple pendulum. With no drag its angular equation is $\ddot\theta+(g/\ell)\sin\theta=0$. The <small-angle approximation> yields <simple harmonic motion>, whereas the full restoring force is nonlinear. Adding drag gives a <damped pendulum>.

For an ideal point mass $m$ swinging in a vertical plane, the <torque> about the pivot is $-mg\ell\sin\theta$. Dividing by its <moment of inertia> $m\ell^2$ gives the displayed equation. Both the inertia and the gravitational <torque> scale with $m$, explaining why the point mass cancels. This derivation and the small-amplitude period are given in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-4-pendulums[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics, pendulums].

Released from rest at a small angular <amplitude> $\theta_0$, the linearized solution is $\theta(t)\simeq\theta_0\cos(\sqrt{g/\ell}\,t)$, with <oscillation period> $T_0=2\pi\sqrt{\ell/g}$. For $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, lengths one and four metres give $T_0\approx2.0061\,\mathrm s$ and $4.0121\,\mathrm s$. This same scaling follows from <dimensional analysis>; the dynamical calculation supplies the coefficient $2\pi$.

Finite angular <amplitude> changes the exact ideal period. <Conservation of energy> gives $\dot\theta^2=(2g/\ell)(\cos\theta-\cos\theta_0)$ for release from rest. Integrating a quarter oscillation and substituting $\sin(\theta/2)=\sin(\theta_0/2)\sin\phi$ gives
$$
T(\theta_0)=4\sqrt{\frac\ell g}\,K\!\left(\sin\frac{\theta_0}2\right),\qquad
\frac{T(\theta_0)}{T_0}=\frac2\pi\int_0^{\pi/2}\frac{d\phi}{\sqrt{1-\sin^2(\theta_0/2)\sin^2\phi}},\quad 0<\theta_0<\pi.
$$
Here $K$ is the <complete elliptic integral of the first kind>. The <small-angle approximation> gives a ratio tending to one; at $10^\circ,60^\circ,90^\circ$, the ratios are approximately $1.00191,1.07318,1.18034$. Thus almost constant period at small <amplitude> has a quantitative limit. As $\theta_0\to\pi$, the period diverges, connecting to the <pendulum separatrix approach takes infinite time>.

\Image[pendulum-period-scaling.png]
{title=Length scaling and finite-amplitude period corrections for an ideal simple pendulum}

These predictions assume the specified ideal system. Drag changes the <mechanical energy>; a massive support changes the <moment of inertia>. The distinction is useful in experiments: a change in period can test an approximation rather than merely indicate an arithmetic mistake.

= Conical pendulum
{parent=Simple pendulum}
{wiki}

A <conical pendulum> is a pendulum whose bob executes uniform horizontal <circular motion> beneath a fixed pivot, with its string at a constant <angle> $\theta$ to the downward vertical. The string sweeps a <right circular conical surface>; the bob follows its circular rim, without any physical cone supporting it. The ideal model has a <point mass> $m>0$, a taut, massless, inextensible string of length $\ell>0$, uniform <gravitational acceleration> of magnitude $g>0$, and negligible <drag force> and pivot <friction>. A nonzero circular motion requires $0<\theta<\pi/2$. This geometry and the corresponding <forces> are described in https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/301/lectures/node88.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, The conical pendulum, equations 262–267].

\Image[conical-pendulum-geometry.png]
{title=Conical pendulum geometry, horizontal orbit, and the two forces on the bob}

The <right triangle> formed by the pivot, the orbit centre, and the bob gives $r=\ell\sin\theta$ and $h=\ell\cos\theta$. Here $r$ is the orbit radius and $h$ is the vertical drop from the pivot to the bob's horizontal plane, not the bob's height above a table. Write $T$ for <tension>, $P$ for the <oscillation period> of one revolution, and $\omega=2\pi/P$ for the <angular speed>; then the bob's <speed> is $v=\omega r$. A side view compresses the horizontal circle to a line segment; the top view reveals the circle and its tangent <velocity>.

The two actual <forces> on the bob are <tension> toward the pivot and <gravitational weight> vertically downward. The vertical <acceleration> is zero, while the horizontal <centripetal acceleration> is $v^2/r=\omega^2r$. Thus <Newton's second law> gives
$$
T\cos\theta=mg,\qquad T\sin\theta=m\omega^2r=\frac{mv^2}{r}.
$$
The inward net <force> is the horizontal component of <tension>, rather than an extra interaction to add to these two <forces>. Constant <speed> still requires <acceleration> because the <velocity> changes direction. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-3-centripetal-force[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics 1, §6.3: centripetal acceleration and net force]{ref}

Dividing the two component equations and using $r=\ell\sin\theta$ gives the useful collection of predictions
$$
\omega^2\cos\theta=\frac g\ell,\qquad T=\frac{mg}{\cos\theta},\qquad v^2=g\ell\frac{\sin^2\theta}{\cos\theta},\qquad P=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{\ell\cos\theta}{g}}=2\pi\sqrt{\frac hg}.
$$
If $\phi$ is the azimuthal <angle>, then $\dot\phi=\pm\omega$; either direction of rotation satisfies $\dot\phi^2\cos\theta=g/\ell$. The <mass> cancels from the <angular speed> and <oscillation period>, but not from the <tension>. The motion is a <relative equilibrium>: in a <rotating frame> following the bob at <angular speed> $\omega$, its <position> is fixed. This is a concrete way to see how rotational <symmetry> can turn an apparently moving solution into a stationary one.

= Rotating bob experiment for a conical pendulum
{parent=Conical pendulum}

The motivating observation is a bob going around at almost constant height while its string traces a cone. The University of Iowa's demonstration places a camera above a short pendulum to reveal this motion. https://instructional-resources.physics.uiowa.edu/1d5025-conical-pendulum[University of Iowa, demonstration 1D50.25: short pendulum and overhead camera]{ref} The following proposed measurement procedure develops that observation into a test of the <conical pendulum> model; it is not a report of collected data.

Suspend a compact bob from a rigid, fixed support using a light string, and measure $\ell$ from the pivot to the bob's centre. Put a stationary marker beside the orbit as a timing reference. Displace the bob sideways and give it a horizontal push tangent to the intended circle, then let go. For a chosen starting <angle> $\theta$, the predicted initial <speed> is $v=\sqrt{g\ell\sin^2\theta/\cos\theta}$. Adjust the launch between trials until a short observation interval has nearly constant orbit radius and height. Keep the pivot fixed while recording: circling the support by hand changes the problem, and a maintained drive would introduce another <force>.

Record from above with the camera looking approximately along the vertical axis. Check that the bob's centre follows a circle and that successive returns to the marker take nearly equal times. A separate side recording checks whether the bob's height stays constant. A markedly elliptical orbit or visibly rising and falling bob is not the uniform <circular motion> assumed by the <conical pendulum> equations. Measure the orbit diameter with a scale at the orbit's height; in a level side view, use the two extreme horizontal positions, not one arbitrary frame. Halve the diameter to obtain $r$, or measure the vertical drop $h$ directly in a calibrated side view. An oblique camera view and a scale at a different depth distort apparent lengths, so arrange the geometry before interpreting the images.

Time $N$ complete returns to the same marker in the same direction and estimate $P=\Delta t/N$. Counting ten revolutions, when the orbit stays sufficiently steady, spreads an endpoint timing error over ten periods. Repeat the timing and compare the first and last few revolutions to detect drift; using a longer interval helps only if the motion remains close to uniform. From independently measured geometry, compute $\sin\theta=r/\ell$ or $\cos\theta=h/\ell$, and check $r^2+h^2\simeq\ell^2$ if both were measured. Then compare the measured <oscillation period> with $P^2=4\pi^2h/g$. MIT's 8.01X experimental assignment explicitly asks students to compare the <conical pendulum>'s period, length, and orbit radius with theory. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01x-physics-i-classical-mechanics-with-an-experimental-focus-fall-2002/a056f6a3e071767d4f7620bca6a59d0c_expt_vs_problem.pdf[MIT 8.01X, Fall 2002, Vibrating Systems experimental problem, question 8]{ref}

Repeat at several launch radii with the same $\ell$. Larger $r$ means smaller $h=\sqrt{\ell^2-r^2}$, so the ideal <oscillation period> decreases. A plot of measured $P^2$ against independently measured $h$ should follow a straight line through the origin with slope $4\pi^2/g$; conversely, $g=4\pi^2h/P^2$ estimates <gravitational acceleration>. Do not infer $h$ from the timing equation and then use that same equation as a test. Compare discrepancies with the spread of repeated timings and the resolution of the length measurements. String stretch, a moving pivot, and appreciable bob size challenge the stated geometry; <drag force> and pivot <friction> challenge the assumption of undriven uniform motion. Observe a short interval and record any shrinking or drifting orbit rather than silently treating it as an exact <conical pendulum>.

For an illustrative calculation, assume $\ell=1.00\,\mathrm m$, $m=0.050\,\mathrm{kg}$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, and $\theta=40^\circ$. The model gives $r\approx0.643\,\mathrm m$, $h\approx0.766\,\mathrm m$, $P\approx1.756\,\mathrm s$, $v\approx2.300\,\mathrm{m/s}$, and $T\approx0.640\,\mathrm N$. Its vertical <tension> component is $0.4905\,\mathrm N$, equal to the <gravitational weight>; its inward component is approximately $0.412\,\mathrm N$. These are predictions from chosen parameters, not measured data or measurements of local $g$. The next figure uses the same stipulated $\ell$ and $g$, including illustrative <angles> $15^\circ$, $40^\circ$, and $60^\circ$.

= Period and small-angle limit of a conical pendulum
{parent=Conical pendulum}
{title2=$P/P_0=\sqrt{\cos\theta},\quad P_0=2\pi\sqrt{\ell/g}$}

\Image[conical-pendulum-period.png]
{title=Ideal conical pendulum period decreases with cone angle and its square is proportional to the vertical drop}

For fixed $\ell$ and $g$, the exact <conical pendulum> <oscillation period> satisfies $P/P_0=\sqrt{\cos\theta}$. In the <small-angle approximation>, with $\theta$ in <radians>,
$$
\frac{P}{P_0}=1-\frac{\theta^2}{4}+O(\theta^4),\qquad P_0=2\pi\sqrt{\frac\ell g}.
$$
Thus the limiting revolution period equals the small-amplitude <oscillation period> of a planar <simple pendulum>. This equality has a geometric explanation: to first order, $x(t)=r\cos(\omega t)$ and $y(t)=r\sin(\omega t)$ are perpendicular <simple harmonic motions> of equal <amplitude>, a quarter period apart, with $\omega\to\sqrt{g/\ell}$. At finite <angle>, however, the <conical pendulum>'s <oscillation period> decreases with $\theta$, whereas a planar <simple pendulum>'s <oscillation period> increases with its angular <amplitude>.

The endpoint $\theta=0$ itself is a stationary bob with $r=0$ and no observable revolution; $P_0$ is a limit of nonzero circular motions, not a measured revolution period at rest. For fixed $\ell$, nonzero <conical pendulum> motion also requires $\omega>\sqrt{g/\ell}$, because $\cos\theta=g/(\ell\omega^2)<1$. At the other endpoint, $\theta\to\pi/2$ gives $h\to0$, $P\to0$, and unbounded <tension> and <speed>. These mathematical limits explain why the ideal string cannot sustain a horizontal orbit at finite <tension>; a real experiment ceases to satisfy the ideal assumptions before that limit.

= Damped pendulum
{parent=Simple pendulum}
{title2=$\ddot\theta+c\dot\theta+\sin\theta=0$}

A <damped pendulum> is a <simple pendulum> whose <mechanical energy> is removed by a resisting <torque>. Here the model is undriven, with a fixed pivot, a <point mass> $m>0$ at distance $\ell>0$, uniform <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, and resisting <torque> $-b\,d\theta/dt$ with constant $b>0$. The <angle> $\theta$ is measured from the downward vertical in <radians>. An ideal viscous rotary <dashpot> realizes this torque law; tangential <linear drag> $-\alpha v$ on the bob gives the same equation with $b=\alpha\ell^2$. The <moment of inertia> is $m\ell^2$, so
$$
m\ell^2\frac{d^2\theta}{dt^2}+b\frac{d\theta}{dt}+mg\ell\sin\theta=0,\qquad
\omega_0=\sqrt{\frac g\ell},\quad \gamma=\frac{b}{m\ell^2},\quad \tau=\omega_0t,\quad c=\frac\gamma{\omega_0}>0.
$$
Here $t$ is physical <time>, $\omega_0$ is the undamped small-amplitude <angular frequency>, and $\gamma$ has units of inverse <time>. In the normalized equation $\ddot\theta+c\dot\theta+\sin\theta=0$, dots mean differentiation with respect to $\tau$, not $t$. The parameter $c$ is dimensionless. This separates the chosen damping law from the <small-angle approximation>: retaining $\sin\theta$ allows finite <angles> even when the resisting <torque> is linear in <angular velocity>.

\Image[damped-pendulum-decay-observation.png]
{title=Proposed viscous-damper experiment and illustrative small-angle decay: angle versus time and successive peak ratios, not measured data}

To motivate the model, release a pendulum without a push and record how its swing shrinks. A published example by Gintautas and Hubler uses a rotary sensor built from a computer mouse to measure the frequency and damping coefficient of a <physical pendulum>. https://arxiv.org/abs/0901.4319[Gintautas and Hubler, A simple, low-cost, data-logging pendulum built from a computer mouse, 2009]{ref} The following is a proposed experiment with a chosen viscous rotary <dashpot>; the plotted observations are predictions, not a reproduction of their measurements.

Mount a compact bob on a light rigid rod with a fixed pivot, couple the pivot to an adjustable viscous rotary <dashpot>, and measure $\ell$ from the pivot to the bob's centre. First record decay with the <dashpot> disconnected, then repeat with it connected at several settings; this establishes how much decay is already due to other losses. Keep the bob, rod, camera, and release <angle> unchanged between these trials. The simple model requires rod and damper-rotor <moments of inertia> to be negligible compared with $m\ell^2$. Otherwise measure the total <moment of inertia> $I$ and the distance $d$ to the assembly's centre of mass: the <physical pendulum> equation becomes $I\,d^2\theta/dt^2+b\,d\theta/dt+Mgd\sin\theta=0$, where $M$ is the total moving <mass>. A damper that also exerts an elastic restoring <torque> would require another term.

Place a camera perpendicular to the swing plane, include the pivot and bob centre in every frame, and determine their coordinates relative to the resting vertical. For horizontal and downward separations $x,y$, use $\theta=\operatorname{atan2}(x,y)$; record the frame timestamps rather than assuming a frame rate. Release from rest at about $10^\circ$, record through several complete swings, and repeat the release. For every same-side turning point, tabulate its <time> $t_n$ and angular <amplitude> $A_n=|\theta(t_n)|$. Measure the <oscillation period> from differences $t_{n+1}-t_n$; comparing several periods exposes drift. Interpolating around each observed extremum reduces frame discretization error. Check that late peaks still exceed the angular resolution; taking the <natural logarithm> of tiny, uncertain peaks amplifies their relative errors.

In the <small-angle approximation>, the <damped pendulum> becomes a <damped harmonic oscillator>. For $0<\gamma<2\omega_0$ and release from rest at $\theta_0$, its exact linear-model solution is
$$
\theta_{\mathrm{lin}}(t)=\theta_0e^{-\gamma t/2}\left(\cos\omega_dt+\frac{\gamma}{2\omega_d}\sin\omega_dt\right),\qquad
\omega_d=\sqrt{\omega_0^2-\frac{\gamma^2}{4}},\qquad T_d=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_d}.
$$
The decaying sinusoidal envelope has coefficient $|\theta_0|\omega_0/\omega_d$; because release is from rest, its successive same-side extrema occur at $t_n=nT_d$ and have $A_n=|\theta_0|e^{-\gamma nT_d/2}$. Thus the envelope and the sequence of actual peak heights are different descriptions of the same trace. The linear damping regimes and this solution are derived in https://farside.ph.utexas.edu/teaching/336k/Newton/node19.html[Richard Fitzpatrick, Damped Oscillatory Motion, equations 82–93].

The experiment tests two predictions independently: nearly equal same-side peak spacing and a straight line when plotting $\ln(A_n/A_0)$ against $t_n-t_0$. Its predicted slope is $-\gamma/2$, giving
$$
\gamma=\frac{2}{t_{n+1}-t_n}\ln\frac{A_n}{A_{n+1}},\qquad b=m\ell^2\gamma,\qquad
\omega_d^2+\frac{\gamma^2}{4}=\frac g\ell.
$$
Use several peaks and repeated trials rather than a single ratio, and compare the frequency identity with independently measured $\ell$ and $g$. For the illustrative choices $\ell=1.00\,\mathrm m$, $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, $c=0.20$, and $\theta_0=10^\circ$, the linear model gives $\gamma\approx0.6264\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $T_d\approx2.0162\,\mathrm s$, and $A_{n+1}/A_n\approx0.5318$. The first two positive peaks after release are therefore approximately $5.318^\circ$ and $2.828^\circ$. The undamped small-amplitude <oscillation period> is about $2.0061\,\mathrm s$: substantial loss per swing need not imply a large change in period.

Repeat at a larger release <angle> to distinguish two limitations. The relative error in replacing $\sin\theta$ by $\theta$ is about $\theta^2/6$ for small <angles>: about $0.51\%$ at $10^\circ$, but about $17.3\%$ at $60^\circ$ when compared with $\theta$. Finite-amplitude swing spacing can therefore change as the swing shrinks even if $b$ is constant. Separately, <friction> at the pivot or <quadratic drag> can invalidate the assumed linear resisting <torque>; exponential peak decay should be checked, not imposed on all recorded motion. Peters discusses why real pendulum damping can require nonlinear terms. https://arxiv.org/abs/physics/0306081[Randall D. Peters, Nonlinear Damping of the 'Linear' Pendulum, 2003]{ref} Repeatability, residual pivot motion, changes in damper <temperature>, and finite camera resolution are useful checks on this proposed measurement.

The normalized <equilibrium points> are $(\theta,\dot\theta)=(k\pi,0)$. Even multiples of $\pi$ are attracting: their <linearization> has <eigenvalues> $(-c\pm\sqrt{c^2-4})/2$, giving a <stable focus> for $0<c<2$, repeated negative <eigenvalues> at $c=2$, and a <stable node> for $c>2$. These are the underdamped, critically damped, and overdamped small-displacement regimes. Odd multiples of $\pi$ have <eigenvalues> $(-c\pm\sqrt{c^2+4})/2$ of opposite signs and are <saddle equilibria>. The <energy dissipation of a damped pendulum> explains why trajectories in the <phase portrait> spiral inward near a <stable focus> instead of closing into the recurring orbit of an undamped <simple pendulum>.

= Energy dissipation of a damped pendulum
{parent=Damped pendulum}
{title2=$\dot E=-c\dot\theta^2$}

For the normalized <damped pendulum>, the sum of <kinetic energy> and gravitational <potential energy>, divided by $mg\ell$, is
$$
E(\tau)=\frac12\dot\theta^2+1-\cos\theta,\qquad
\dot E=\dot\theta(\ddot\theta+\sin\theta)=-c\dot\theta^2\leq0.
$$
The <chain rule> and the full nonlinear equation give this identity exactly, without a <small-angle approximation>. Here $\dot\theta=d\theta/d\tau$ as in <damped pendulum>. In physical units the <mechanical energy> and the <power> of the resisting <torque> are
$$
\mathcal E(t)=\frac12m\ell^2\left(\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right)^2+mg\ell(1-\cos\theta),\qquad
\frac{d\mathcal E}{dt}=-b\left(\frac{d\theta}{dt}\right)^2.
$$
The pendulum's lost <mechanical energy> is transferred to the damper and surroundings; it does not violate <conservation of energy>. In an ideal viscous <dashpot> this loss is <frictional heating>. The exact cumulative balance is particularly useful for checking a numerical solution:
$$
Q(\tau)=c\int_0^\tau\dot\theta(s)^2\,ds,\qquad E(\tau)+Q(\tau)=E(0).
$$

\Image[damped-pendulum-nonlinear-energy.png]
{title=Illustrative nonlinear and linear damped pendulum solutions at 10 and 60 degrees, with exact mechanical energy transfer and loss; no measured data}

The figure uses $c=0.20$ and release from rest. Over $0\leq\tau\leq25$, the largest angular discrepancy between the nonlinear solution and the <damped harmonic oscillator> solution is approximately $0.000702\,\mathrm{rad}$ ($0.0402^\circ$) for a $10^\circ$ release, but $0.14995\,\mathrm{rad}$ ($8.592^\circ$) for a $60^\circ$ release. Their nonlinear initial energies are $1-\cos10^\circ\approx0.015192$ and $1-\cos60^\circ=0.5$, respectively. At $\tau=25$ the remaining fractions are approximately $0.0064080$ and $0.0063757$. These are illustrative calculations from specified initial conditions, not observations. The generator uses a <Runge-Kutta method> and integrates $Q$ alongside the motion; halving its step and independently integrating $c\dot\theta^2$ checks the result.

For $\ell=1\,\mathrm m$ and $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the first three nonlinear same-side swing intervals after a $60^\circ$ release are approximately $2.1008$, $2.0378$, and $2.0221\,\mathrm s$. They approach the linear-model <oscillation period> $2.0162\,\mathrm s$ as the <amplitude> shrinks. After a $10^\circ$ release the corresponding intervals are already about $2.0185$, $2.0168$, and $2.0164\,\mathrm s$. This makes the experiment's period drift a quantitative test of the <small-angle approximation>, rather than an automatic reason to change the damping coefficient.

At a turning point, $\dot\theta=0$ and $\dot E=0$ even though the swing is losing <mechanical energy> over the cycle. Its <potential energy> there is $E_n=1-\cos A_n$, so measured turning <angles> give successive energies without estimating <angular velocity>. In the proposed decay experiment, multiply these values by $mg\ell$ to obtain energies in <joules>, then subtract adjacent same-side energies to estimate the loss over each full swing. Only for small <amplitudes> is $E_n\simeq A_n^2/2$. In the linear underdamped model, same-side peak energies consequently satisfy $E_{n+1}/E_n=e^{-\gamma T_d}$, the square of the angular <amplitude> ratio. For the illustrative parameters above this energy ratio is approximately $0.2828$. Between turning points, <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> continue to exchange while their sum decreases; the energy slope vanishes briefly at each extremum and is negative whenever the bob moves.

It is therefore inaccurate to replace the exact identity by $\dot E=-cE$. Even for the linear model, $E_{\mathrm{lin}}=(\dot\theta^2+\theta^2)/2$ has $\dot E_{\mathrm{lin}}=-c\dot\theta^2$. For weak damping and small <angles>, cycle averaging gives $\langle\dot\theta^2\rangle\simeq\langle E_{\mathrm{lin}}\rangle$, so the averaged energy approximately follows $e^{-c\tau}$ while angular <amplitude> follows $e^{-c\tau/2}$. This <exponential decay> is an approximation to the energy trace; the same-side peak ratio is exact within the linear model. The figure distinguishes the actual energy curves from this averaging approximation.

The energy identity also gives a global conclusion without solving the nonlinear equation. If $E$ were constant on a positive-length interval, then $\dot\theta$ would vanish throughout it; the equation would force $\sin\theta=0$, making the entire trajectory an <equilibrium>. Every nonstationary trajectory therefore loses <mechanical energy> over every positive-length interval, even though its instantaneous <derivative> can vanish at a turning point. A nonconstant periodic motion would have to return to its initial <mechanical energy>, so it is impossible for this undriven <damped pendulum>. Oscillations near a <stable focus> are successive, shrinking swings, not a repeating cycle. Moreover, if $E(0)<2$, the <angle> cannot reach an upright <equilibrium>, whose <potential energy> is $2$; such motion stays within its original potential well. This is the usefulness of the energy description: one scalar balance controls both the observed decay and which nonlinear motions remain accessible.

= Eisenhart-Duval lift
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$g=d\mathbf x^2+2dt(du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt)$}

= Eisenhart lift
{c}
{synonym}

= Eisenhart-Duval metric lift
{c}
{synonym}

A mechanical Lagrangian quadratic in velocities can be represented by <geodesics> of a <Lorentzian metric> with an extra null coordinate. For $L=\tfrac12|\dot{\mathbf x}|^2-2\mathbf A\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}-U$, use $g=d\mathbf x^2+2dt(du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt)$. The null <Killing vector field> $\partial_u$ gives the conserved momentum $p_u=t\prime$. Fixing $p_u=1$ projects the <geodesic equation> to the mechanical <Euler-Lagrange equations>. This is useful for relating mechanical forces to spacetime geometry.

= Gauge transformations of an Eisenhart-Duval lift
{parent=Eisenhart-Duval lift}
{title2=$\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi,\quad u\mapsto u+2\chi$}

A time-independent <gauge transformation> $\mathbf A\mapsto\mathbf A+\nabla\chi$ in an <Eisenhart-Duval lift> is the coordinate change $u\mapsto u+2\chi$. It preserves the metric one-form $du-2\mathbf A\cdot d\mathbf x-Udt$ and adds only $-2d\chi/dt$ to the reduced Lagrangian.

= Null Kaluza-Klein reduction of a stationary force
{parent=Eisenhart-Duval lift}
{title2=$p_u=t\prime\ne0$}

For an <Eisenhart-Duval lift>, reduction along the null isometry fixes a nonzero conserved $p_u=t\prime$ and projects <geodesics> to $(t,\mathbf x)$. One never divides by $g_{uu}=0$. When $\mathbf E=-\nabla U$ and $\nabla\times\mathbf A=\mathbf B$, the reduced equation is $\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf E+2\mathbf B\times\dot{\mathbf x}$. Every trajectory admits a null lift by choosing $\dot u=U+2\mathbf A\cdot\dot{\mathbf x}-\tfrac12|\dot{\mathbf x}|^2$.

= Newtonian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=m\mathbf a$}

Newtonian mechanics describes motion using forces, masses and acceleration in <inertial frames> with absolute time. It is the low-speed limit of <special relativity>; for constant <proper acceleration> with $|at|\ll c$, relativistic <hyperbolic motion> reduces to $x\simeq at^2/2$.

= Small-slope particle motion on a height graph
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{title2=$\ddot{\mathbf r}\simeq-g\nabla h$}

A particle constrained to the graph $z=h(x,y)$ has <kinetic energy> $m[|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2+(\nabla h\cdot\dot{\mathbf r})^2]/2$ and <potential energy> $mgh$. For a gently varying graph and velocities for which its geometric kinetic correction is higher order, the leading <Euler-Lagrange equations> are the displayed planar gradient-force equations. Small slope alone at arbitrarily large speed or curvature does not justify discarding the normal inertial acceleration. For a radial height, the leading planar <angular momentum> is conserved, allowing an <effective potential> reduction.

= Radial turning points for an inverse-square height well
{parent=Small-slope particle motion on a height graph}
{title2=$\dot r^2=2\mathcal E+2g/r-(L^2+2g)/r^2$}

For the height profile $h(r)=r^{-2}-r^{-1}$, set $A=L^2+2g$ and use the planar small-slope model. Its <effective potential> per unit mass is $V_{\rm eff}=A/(2r^2)-g/r$. Release with negligible radial speed at $r_0$ gives $\mathcal E=A/(2r_0^2)-g/r_0$ and the displayed radial-speed law. A bounded trajectory requires $\mathcal E<0$, equivalently $r_0>A/(2g)$. The two positive turning radii are $r_0$ and $Ar_0/(2gr_0-A)$; they coincide at the circular solution $r_0=A/g$. Zero energy is marginal escape, not bounded motion.

= Tangentially driven motion on an axisymmetric surface
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{title2=$\dot\phi^2=\frac{g h'(r)}{r+2r_\phi^2/r-r_{\phi\phi}}$}

Neglecting vertical acceleration and the vertical driving-force component, the radial normal reaction is $-Mg h'(r)$. A drive parallel to the horizontal velocity cancels when the radial acceleration equation is combined with $r_\phi/r$ times the azimuthal equation. For a prescribed path $r(\phi)$ this gives the displayed speed constraint. It describes the projected approximation, not arbitrary trajectories with substantial vertical motion.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Friction
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}

<Friction> is a contact <force> tangent to the surfaces. <Kinetic friction> opposes <contact slip>; <static friction> maintains contact without <contact slip>, including <rolling without slipping>. The perpendicular contact component is the <normal force>. These two components answer different questions: how strongly the surfaces press together, and how strongly they resist sliding past one another.

The ideal Coulomb dry-contact model combines a bound on <static friction> with a sliding law for <kinetic friction>. Its parameters are the <coefficient of static friction> and the <coefficient of kinetic friction>; constant coefficients are an empirical approximation, rather than a consequence of <Newton's laws of motion>. Surface condition matters, and the static coefficient need not universally exceed the kinetic coefficient. https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_12.html#Ch12-S2[Feynman, Leighton and Sands, The Feynman Lectures on Physics I, §12-2: friction and the limits of the empirical law]{ref}

The model's historical setting was practical machinery: Coulomb's \i[Théorie des machines simples] investigated <friction> and the stiffness of ropes. The Académie's bibliography records its 1781 prize and its appearance in volume 10, pages 161–332, of the \i[Recueil des savants étrangers]. https://www.academie-sciences.fr/pdf/dossiers/Coulomb/Coulomb_pubd1.htm[Académie des sciences: Coulomb's published works]{ref}

= Kinetic friction
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$|F_{\mathrm{fr}}|=\mu N$}

<Kinetic friction> is <friction> during nonzero <contact slip>. For one-dimensional motion along a surface, choose right as positive and write $v$ for the body's contact-point <velocity> and $U$ for the surface's contact-point <velocity>, both measured in the same <inertial frame>. The signed <relative velocity> is $w=v-U$. The ideal Coulomb law for the tangential <force> on the body is
$$
f_k=-\mu_kN\operatorname{sgn}(v-U),\qquad v-U\ne0.
$$
Here $N\ge0$ is the magnitude of the <normal force>, $\mu_k\ge0$ is the <coefficient of kinetic friction>, and $\operatorname{sgn}$ is the <sign function>. Thus <kinetic friction> opposes <contact slip>, which can differ from the body's direction of motion. In several tangential directions the same law reads $\mathbf f_k=-\mu_kN\mathbf w/|\mathbf w|$ for a nonzero tangential <relative velocity> $\mathbf w$. The magnitude law is documented in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-2-friction[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, OpenStax University Physics 1, §6.2: static and kinetic friction].

For a stipulated toy model, take a nonrotating box with <mass> $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, <normal force> $N=10\,\mathrm N$, <coefficient of static friction> $\mu_s=0.5$, and <coefficient of kinetic friction> $\mu_k=0.3$. These are assumed model parameters, rather than measured properties of any particular pair of materials; the prescribed <normal force> is consistent with horizontal support under $g=10\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ and no other vertical <forces>. On stationary ground, a box already sliding right has $f_k=-3\,\mathrm N$. With an applied horizontal <force> $F=6\,\mathrm N$ to the right, <Newton's second law> gives $a=(F+f_k)/m=3\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, for as long as rightward <contact slip> persists.

Direction is clearer by comparing two instantaneous states of this same model. If the box has $v=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ on stationary ground, $U=0$ and $w=+1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, so <kinetic friction> acts left with magnitude $3\,\mathrm N$. If the box still has $v=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ but a belt beneath it has $U=2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, then $w=-1\,\mathrm{m/s}$: <kinetic friction> acts right with magnitude $3\,\mathrm N$, even while the box moves right.

\Image[kinetic-friction-relative-motion.png]
{title=The same rightward box velocity gives opposite friction directions on stationary ground and a faster moving belt}

In the belt case, the <power> delivered to the box is $P_{\mathrm{box}}=f_kv=+3\,\mathrm W$. With no other horizontal <force>, its <kinetic energy> therefore increases by the <work-energy theorem>. By <Newton's third law>, the box exerts $-f_k$ on the belt, so the contact <power> delivered to the belt is $P_{\mathrm{belt}}=-f_kU=-6\,\mathrm W$. Their sum is $-3\,\mathrm W$: <frictional heating> produces thermal <internal energy> at $3\,\mathrm W$. An ideal motor maintaining the prescribed belt <velocity> supplies $+6\,\mathrm W$ against this contact load, neglecting other losses, so its input divides into $3\,\mathrm W$ of box <kinetic energy> and $3\,\mathrm W$ of <frictional heating>. These are instantaneous rates, since the box's <velocity> subsequently changes. This balance follows directly from <power> as <force> times <velocity> and <conservation of energy>; the power law is given in https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/7-4-power[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, OpenStax University Physics 1, §7.4: power].

When <contact slip> vanishes, including a transition to <rolling without slipping>, the nonzero-slip law no longer applies. Determine the required <static friction> from the no-slip condition and the <equations of motion>, then test its bound. Substituting $\operatorname{sgn}(0)=0$ into the sliding law would incorrectly exclude a nonzero <static friction>.

= Coefficient of kinetic friction
{parent=Kinetic friction}
{title2=$\mu_k$}

The <coefficient of kinetic friction> is the <dimensionless quantity> $\mu_k=|f_k|/N$ for sliding contact with positive <normal force> $N$ in the ideal <kinetic friction> model. It characterizes the pair of contacting surfaces and their condition. A constant $\mu_k$ means the model neglects dependence on <contact slip> speed; unlike the <coefficient of static friction>, it specifies a sliding <force> magnitude rather than a no-slip upper bound.

The ratio compares the tangential <kinetic friction> magnitude with the perpendicular <normal force>:
$$
\mu_k=\frac{|f_k|}{N},\qquad N>0,\quad |\mathbf w|>0.
$$
Here $\mathbf w$ is the tangential <relative velocity> defining <contact slip>. Both numerator and denominator have the same <physical dimension>, so the <coefficient of kinetic friction> has no units. It belongs to a contact pair under specified conditions, rather than to one material alone. The <coefficient of static friction> instead describes the threshold $|f_s|_{\max}/N$ before <contact slip>; a pull required to start motion cannot be substituted for a sliding measurement.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[§6.2, “Magnitude of Static Friction” and “Magnitude of Kinetic Friction”, equations 6.1–6.2; unitless coefficient and dependence on the two surfaces]{ref}

As an original model example, stipulate $\mu_k=0.30$ and <gravitational acceleration> $g=9.8\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. An already sliding, nonrotating block of total <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$ is pulled horizontally across a stationary horizontal surface at constant <velocity>. With no other vertical <force>, <Newton's second law> gives $N=mg=19.6\,\mathrm N$. Zero horizontal <acceleration> then requires a pulling <force> $F=|f_k|=\mu_kmg=5.88\,\mathrm N$. Doubling the total <mass> to $4\,\mathrm{kg}$ gives $N=39.2\,\mathrm N$ and $F=11.76\,\mathrm N$. The <force> doubles while the <coefficient of kinetic friction> stays $0.30$ by assumption. These numbers are model predictions, not observations or a calibration for a particular material pair.

\Image[kinetic-friction-constant-speed-force-balance.png]
{title=Original horizontal sliding model: a 2 kg block has balanced forces at constant speed with a stipulated kinetic friction coefficient of 0.30}

A constant <coefficient of kinetic friction> is useful because one ratio can summarize many loads, as tested by <load dependence of kinetic friction>. It is an empirical approximation over a stated range of <normal forces>, <speeds> and surface conditions. It implies no universal independence from <speed> or apparent contact <area>: a change of face or sliding regime can change the result. <Temperature>, wear and lubrication are further conditions to report. <Newton's second law> still applies when the constant-coefficient approximation fails.<University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others>[§6.2, approximate empirical model; small nonzero speeds; exceptions for lubricated surfaces and high speeds; “Atomic-Scale Explanations of Friction”, apparent versus actual contact area]{ref}

= Constant-speed measurement of kinetic friction
{parent=Coefficient of kinetic friction}
{title2=$\mu_k=\overline F/(mg)$}

A force gauge reads the pulling <force>. For horizontal straight-line sliding at constant <velocity>, its reading equals the opposing <kinetic friction> magnitude, provided these are the only horizontal <forces>. For total supported <mass> $m$, including added loads, $N=mg$, so a mean sliding reading $\overline F$ gives $\mu_k=\overline F/(mg)$. The important readout is the sustained sliding force after the starting peak; the peak belongs to the <static friction> threshold.

The University of Mississippi's Experiment 6 uses a wooden block on a wooden plane, a triple-beam balance, added masses, and a dual-range 50 N force probe connected through LabPro to Logger Pro. Its procedure calibrates the probe with a $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ load on a hanger, entering the corresponding <force> in newtons, and measures the block's <mass> separately. With the plane horizontal, it starts with $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ on the block and pulls horizontally at constant <speed>. The recorded <force> trace has a starting peak; the procedure selects the subsequent constant-force interval and takes its <mean>. It repeats with $0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$ increments up to $3\,\mathrm{kg}$ added, then plots sliding <force> against <normal force> to estimate the <coefficient of kinetic friction> from the <slope>.<Experiment 6: The Coefficient of Friction (University of Mississippi teaching laboratory)>[Experiment 6, The Coefficient of Friction, PDF pp. 1–2: Equipment, Objectives, Procedure steps 1–7]{ref}

For a replication, check the gauge's zero with the string slack and its response to known <gravitational weights>; zeroing alone does not check its scale. Keep the plane level, the pull parallel to it, and the block face and track region consistent. Specify the contacting materials and whether the surfaces are dry, cleaned or lubricated; repeat runs to see whether debris or wear changes the sliding <force>. Verify constant <velocity> independently from <position> versus <time>, rather than assuming that a flat force trace proves zero <acceleration>. These are proposed measurement controls, not additional observations from the laboratory writeup. Record the selected interval, variation between runs and gauge resolution as sources of <measurement error>.

= Load dependence of kinetic friction
{parent=Coefficient of kinetic friction}
{title2=$|f_k|=\mu_kN$}

Changing the added <mass> while retaining the same contact pair and sliding <speed> tests whether a constant <coefficient of kinetic friction> is a useful model. Compute $N=(m_{\mathrm{block}}+m_{\mathrm{added}})g$ for each level horizontal run, and plot the mean sliding <force> vertically against <normal force> horizontally. The constant-coefficient prediction is a straight line through the origin; its <slope> is $\mu_k$. This extracts a ratio from several loads rather than relying on one reading.

\Image[kinetic-friction-load-model-and-apparatus.png]
{title=Horizontal wooden-block force-probe apparatus and original load model: 19.6 N and 39.2 N normal loads predict 5.88 N and 11.76 N sliding forces}

The apparatus sketch identifies the contact pair and horizontal pull used by the cited laboratory procedure. The plotted line and points are the original stipulated $\mu_k=0.30$ model from <coefficient of kinetic friction>, not laboratory data. The two model points give
$$
\frac{\Delta |f_k|}{\Delta N}=\frac{11.76-5.88}{39.2-19.6}=0.30.
$$
In actual measurements, a <linear regression> $\overline F=b+\mu_kN$ can expose an unexplained <intercept> $b$. A nonzero <intercept> calls for checking the gauge zero, other resisting <forces> and the contact model; forcing the line through zero can hide those problems. Conversely, constant ratios within <measurement error> support the approximation only over the loads, <speeds> and surface conditions tested. A load sweep alone cannot establish independence from contact <area> or sliding <speed>; those require separate controlled variations.

= Corrections to a pulling measurement of kinetic friction
{parent=Coefficient of kinetic friction}
{title2=$\mu_k=(F\cos\alpha-ma)/(mg-F\sin\alpha)$}

For a block sliding right on a stationary horizontal plane, take rightward <acceleration> $a$ as positive. A gauge reading $F$ for a pull at <angle> $\alpha$ above horizontal has horizontal component $F\cos\alpha$ and upward component $F\sin\alpha$. With no vertical <acceleration> or other <forces>, <Newton's second law> gives
$$
N=mg-F\sin\alpha,\qquad F\cos\alpha-|f_k|=ma,
$$
and therefore
$$
\mu_k=\frac{F\cos\alpha-ma}{mg-F\sin\alpha}.
$$
This inference requires continued contact with $N>0$ and rightward <contact slip>, so its numerator must be nonnegative. An upward pull reduces <normal force>; a downward pull, represented by $\alpha<0$, increases it. The horizontal constant-<velocity> formula is the special case $\alpha=0$, $a=0$.

For the original $2\,\mathrm{kg}$, $\mu_k=0.30$ model, an upward pull at $\alpha=30^\circ$ maintaining constant <velocity> needs
$$
F=\frac{\mu_kmg}{\cos\alpha+\mu_k\sin\alpha}\simeq5.7873\,\mathrm N,\qquad N\simeq16.7064\,\mathrm N.
$$
Dividing this gauge reading by $mg$ would give about $0.2953$, rather than $0.30$: the gauge reads the full inclined <force>, and the pull has changed the <normal force>.

Even with a horizontal pull, an accelerating measurement needs the $ma$ correction: $\mu_k=(F-ma)/(mg)$. In the same model, a horizontal $7.88\,\mathrm N$ pull produces $a=1\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ while the block slides right; subtracting $ma=2\,\mathrm N$ recovers the $5.88\,\mathrm N$ <kinetic friction>. Dividing the uncorrected reading by $19.6\,\mathrm N$ instead gives about $0.4020$. Measure <acceleration> in an <inertial frame>, along the sliding direction, over the same interval as the gauge reading. These corrections follow from the <force> balance, independently of whether the resulting <coefficient of kinetic friction> is constant across runs.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Two-stage stopping under linear drag and dry friction
{parent=Kinetic friction}
{title2=$t=\frac M\lambda\log\frac U{u_*}+\frac{u_*}{\mu g},\quad d=\frac M\lambda(U-u_*)+\frac{u_*^2}{2\mu g}$}

For a body of mass $M$ initially at speed $U$, suppose the total resistance is <linear drag> $\lambda u$ until speed $u_*>0$, followed by <kinetic friction> $\mu Mg$ until rest. The first stage solves $M\dot u=-\lambda u$, giving duration $(M/\lambda)\log(U/u_*)$ and distance $(M/\lambda)(U-u_*)$. Constant deceleration in the second stage gives duration $u_*/(\mu g)$ and distance $u_*^2/(2\mu g)$. The stopping distance is finite because the switch removes the infinite exponential stopping time of pure linear drag.

= Contact slip
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$\mathbf w$}

<Contact slip> is the tangential <relative velocity> between the two material points instantaneously in contact. For a nonrotating box on a belt it is $w=v-U$; for a <rigid body>, use the <velocity> of its contact point, including the contribution from <angular velocity>. Zero <contact slip> defines <rolling without slipping> at a rolling contact, even when the <center of mass> moves.

= Frictional heating
{parent=Friction}
{title2=$\dot E_{\mathrm{thermal}}=\mu_kN|w|$}

In the ideal <kinetic friction> model, the loss of combined <mechanical energy> of the two contacting bodies becomes thermal <internal energy> at rate
$$
\dot E_{\mathrm{thermal}}=-f_k(v-U)=\mu_kN|v-U|\ge0.
$$
This follows by adding the contact <powers> $f_kv$ and $-f_kU$ from <Newton's third law>. It depends on <contact slip>, whereas the contact <power> received by either body separately depends on its <velocity> in the chosen <inertial frame>. Thus positive <frictional heating> is compatible with increasing <kinetic energy> of one body, as the moving-belt example in <kinetic friction> shows. Heating need not cause a <temperature> rise if thermal <energy> escapes by <heat transfer>.

= Newton–Cartan theory
{parent=Newtonian mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Newton–Cartan theory describes <Newtonian mechanics> geometrically using absolute time, a degenerate spatial inverse metric, and a compatible torsion-free <affine connection>. It is not a theory with a nondegenerate Lorentzian metric. Gravitationally accelerated particles are geodesics of that connection, whose curvature measures tidal gravity.

= Newton–Cartan gravitational connection
{parent=Newton–Cartan theory}
{c}
{title2=$\Gamma^i{}_{00}=\partial_i\Phi$}

In Cartesian spatial coordinates and absolute time $t$, the <gravitational potential> $\Phi(t,x)$ determines an <affine connection> by the displayed coefficients, all others zero. The <geodesic equation> with affine time is $d^2x^i/dt^2=-\partial_i\Phi$. It preserves $dt$ and the spatial inverse metric $h^{ij}=\delta^{ij}$, reproducing freely falling <Newtonian mechanics> in a curved spacetime connection.

= Newtonian tidal curvature
{parent=Newton–Cartan gravitational connection}
{title2=$R^i{}_{0j0}=\partial_i\partial_j\Phi,\quad R_{00}=\nabla^2\Phi$}

Using $[\nabla_c,\nabla_d]V^a=R^a{}_{bcd}V^b$, the <Newton–Cartan gravitational connection> has only the displayed Hessian components and their antisymmetric partners in the last two indices. The <geodesic deviation> equation gives relative acceleration $-\partial_i\partial_j\Phi\,\delta x^j$. The <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> is therefore $R_{ab}=4\pi G\rho\,(dt)_a(dt)_b$. A spatially uniform gravitational acceleration has zero tidal curvature even if the connection coefficients do not vanish in the original coordinates.

= Equation of motion
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Equations of motion
{synonym}

An equation of motion determines how a physical state evolves. A variational action gives <Euler-Lagrange equations>; a <phase-space action> instead gives canonical <Hamiltonian mechanics> equations together with any imposed <constraints>.

= Time reversal in classical mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{title2=$x(t)\mapsto x(-t)$}

<Time reversal in classical mechanics> reverses the order of a trajectory's states and the sign of its velocities. A coordinate is time-even, while its velocity is time-odd. <Microscopic reversibility> compares a path with this physical reversal, including the correct equilibrium initial weights.

= Newton's law of universal gravitation
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf F=-GMm\mathbf r/|\mathbf r|^3$}
{wiki}

<Newton's law of universal gravitation> gives the attractive <force> between two <point masses>. With source <mass> $M$ at the origin and relative <position> $\mathbf r$ of <mass> $m$, write $r=|\mathbf r|>0$:
$$
\mathbf F_{M\to m}=-\frac{GMm}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad |\mathbf F_{M\to m}|=\frac{GMm}{r^2}.
$$
Here $G$ is the <gravitational constant>. The minus sign points toward $M$; the magnitude follows an <inverse-square law>, so doubling the separation quarters the <force>. The opposite <force> on $M$ satisfies <Newton's third law>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation[OpenStax: mutual gravitational forces]{ref}

Both <masses> accelerate. Applying <Newton's second law> to each in the isolated <two-body problem> gives
$$
\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\frac{\mu}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad \mu=G(M+m).
$$
The relative <standard gravitational parameter> includes both <masses>; replacing it by $GM$ requires the <test particle> approximation $m\ll M$.

Historically, Newton's Book III, Proposition VII argues that gravitational attraction applies to all bodies and scales with their <masses>; its second corollary states the <inverse-square law> for individual particles. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Proposition_7:_Gravity_tending_to_all_Bodies[Newton, Principia, Book III, Proposition VII, Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

= Free fall
{parent=Newton's law of universal gravitation}
{wiki}

Free fall is motion under <gravity> alone: there is no supporting <normal force>, engine thrust or appreciable <drag force>. In the <Newtonian mechanics> model, a <test particle> of <mass> $m$ obeys $m\ddot{\mathbf r}=m\mathbf g(\mathbf r)$, hence $\ddot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf g(\mathbf r)$. The <mass> cancels. Bodies released at the same <position> with the same <velocity> follow the same path in this model, even if their <gravitational weights> differ. https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/free-falling-objects/[NASA Glenn: free-falling objects and the cancellation of mass]{ref}

A dropped stone is one example; a coasting satellite is another. Free fall does not require motion straight down or decreasing distance to the attracting body. An ideal <circular orbit> has constant radius while its <velocity> continually turns under <centripetal acceleration>. The <circular orbit as continuous free fall> connects the familiar drop with orbital motion.

Near a surface, treating the <gravitational acceleration> as constant gives a useful first prediction. Over larger heights, the <inverse-square law> changes the acceleration during the fall. <Radial free fall from rest> uses <conservation of mechanical energy> to account for that change exactly, within the specified <Newtonian gravitational field>. The contrast matters: the simplest formula is an approximation to a physical model, rather than a law with the same numerical $g$ everywhere.

History also makes the idealization tangible. Galileo's *Two New Sciences* (1638), Third Day, Theorem II, derives distance proportional to the square of time for uniformly accelerated motion from rest; its first corollary gives successive distances in the ratios $1,3,5,7,\ldots$ over equal time intervals. This is a kinematic statement, predating the gravitational <inverse-square law>. https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/tns_draft/tns_160to243.html[Galileo, Two New Sciences, Third Day, Theorem II and Corollary I; Crew and de Salvio's 1914 translation, pp. 174–176]{ref}

On Apollo 15's final lunar surface excursion, David Scott released a hammer and a feather together. The near-<vacuum> removed the large difference in <drag force> familiar on Earth. NASA's report describes their arrival together within the accuracy of the release; this demonstration illustrates the <free fall> prediction without establishing infinite experimental precision. https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-apollo-15-hammer-feather-drop/[NASA's original footage and Joe Allen's account in the Apollo 15 Preliminary Science Report, p. 2-11]{ref}

\Video[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oYEgdZ3iEKA]
{title=NASA footage of David Scott's Apollo 15 hammer-and-feather free-fall demonstration}

= Free fall in a constant gravitational field
{parent=Free fall}
{title2=$s(t)=\tfrac12gt^2,\quad v(t)=gt$}

Take downward <displacement> $s$ as positive, assume a constant <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, and release a <point mass> from rest at $s=0$. Integrating $\ddot s=g$ twice gives
$$
v(t)=gt,\qquad s(t)=\frac12gt^2.
$$
For a downward distance $h>0$, the arrival time and <speed> are
$$
t_{\mathrm{fall}}=\sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}},\qquad v_{\mathrm{arrival}}=\sqrt{2gh}.
$$
For the model values $h=20\,\mathrm m$ and $g=9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, these are approximately $2.0193\,\mathrm s$ and $19.8091\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. Doubling the height multiplies both quantities by $\sqrt2$; it does not double them. The same <speed> follows from the <work-energy theorem>, $mgh=mv^2/2$.

For intervals of length $\Delta t$, the successive downward <displacements> are
$$
s((j+1)\Delta t)-s(j\Delta t)=\frac12g(\Delta t)^2(2j+1),\qquad j=0,1,2,\ldots.
$$
Thus equal spacing in time produces increasingly separated positions. This is the odd-number pattern in Galileo's result. It assumes constant <acceleration> and release from rest; a nonzero initial <velocity> adds a linear term to $s(t)$.

The approximation can be estimated for a spherical source of radius $R$ and <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu$. Writing $g_0=\mu/R^2$, the gravitational magnitude at height $h$ is
$$
\frac{g(R+h)}{g_0}=\left(1+\frac hR\right)^{-2}.
$$
For $h/R\ll1$, a <Taylor expansion> gives $g(R+h)/g_0=1-2h/R+O((h/R)^2)$. Small height relative to the source's radius makes nearly constant gravity plausible; negligible <drag force> remains a separate requirement.

= Radial free fall from rest
{parent=Free fall}
{title2=$v(r)=\sqrt{2\mu(1/r-1/r_0)}$}

Let a <test particle> start at rest at radius $r_0$ outside a fixed spherically symmetric source. The <spherical shell theorem> gives the exterior <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi(r)=-\mu/r$, where $\mu=GM$ is the source's <standard gravitational parameter>. Zero initial transverse <velocity> makes the motion radial. <Conservation of mechanical energy> per unit <mass> gives
$$
\frac{\dot r^2}{2}-\frac{\mu}{r}=-\frac{\mu}{r_0},\qquad
\dot r=-\sqrt{2\mu\left(\frac1r-\frac1{r_0}\right)}.
$$
The minus sign selects inward motion; the positive square root is its <speed>. For a fall from $r_0=2R$ to the surface $r=R$, that <speed> is $\sqrt{\mu/R}$. In the limit of release from rest infinitely far away, the familiar <free-fall speed> is $\sqrt{2\mu/r}$, equal in magnitude to the local <escape velocity>. A finite starting radius gives a smaller speed.

The arrival time follows from the same first integral. Set $r=r_0\cos^2\eta$. Then
$$
t(\eta)=\sqrt{\frac{r_0^3}{2\mu}}\left(\eta+\sin\eta\cos\eta\right),\qquad
\eta=\arccos\sqrt{\frac r{r_0}},\qquad 0\leq\eta<\frac\pi2.
$$
For arrival at $R>0$ this is finite, and the field formula is used only outside the source. For $r_0=2R$, $\eta=\pi/4$ gives
$$
t_{\mathrm{arrival}}=\left(1+\frac\pi2\right)\sqrt{\frac{R^3}{\mu}}\approx2.570796\sqrt{\frac{R^3}{\mu}}.
$$
Keeping the smaller initial gravitational magnitude $g(r_0)=\mu/(4R^2)$ constant would predict $\sqrt8\,\sqrt{R^3/\mu}$, about $10.0\%$ longer. The actual <acceleration> increases during the fall.

\Image[radial-free-fall-gravity-variation.png]
{title=Radial free-fall height and arrival time compared with constant gravity at the release point}

The curves compare a shallow fall, $h/R=0.02$, with a fall from $r_0=2R$, so $h/R=1$. Each comparison holds the release height and initial gravitational magnitude fixed. The constant-gravity model is close for the shallow fall and increasingly inaccurate for the larger one. This is also a practical use of <nondimensionalization>: the normalized trajectories depend on $h/R$, while $\sqrt{R^3/\mu}$ supplies the dimensional time scale.

= Rotational deflection of a falling body
{parent=Free fall}

In an Earth-fixed <rotating reference frame>, the horizontal displacement of a freely falling body receives contributions from <Coriolis acceleration> and <centrifugal acceleration>. A short-fall approximation substitutes the unperturbed downward <velocity> into the Coriolis term and the Earth's surface <position> into the centrifugal term. If <gravitational acceleration> excludes centrifugal acceleration, both contributions must be included explicitly.

= Newtonian gravitational field
{parent=Newton's law of universal gravitation}
{c}
{title2=$\mathbf g$}

The <Newtonian gravitational field> is the gravitational <force> per unit <mass> of a <test particle>. For a <point mass> $M$ at the origin,
$$
\mathbf g(\mathbf r)=\frac{\mathbf F}{m}=-\frac{GM}{r^3}\mathbf r,\qquad g(r)=|\mathbf g|=\frac{GM}{r^2}.
$$
The <test particle>'s <mass> cancels: different small <masses> have the same <gravitational acceleration> at the same <position>. In <SI>, the <Newtonian gravitational field> has units $\mathrm{N\,kg^{-1}}=\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The same exterior expression holds for a spherically symmetric source, by the <spherical shell theorem>.

Choose a reference radius $r_0>0$, set $g_0=GM/r_0^2$, and use <nondimensionalization> with $z=r/r_0$. Then $g/g_0=z^{-2}$: at $z=1,2,4$, the magnitudes are $1,1/4,1/16$. This makes the spatial variation visible without specifying a planet. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/13-1-newtons-law-of-universal-gravitation[OpenStax: inverse-square attraction and spherical sources]{ref}

\Image[gravity-force-and-potential.png]
{title=Normalized Newtonian gravitational field and point-mass potential}
{description=Doubling the radius quarters the magnitude of the <Newtonian gravitational field>, but halves the magnitude of the negative <Newtonian gravitational potential>. Both curves approach zero as radius increases; the inward <gravitational acceleration> is minus the <gradient> of the <Newtonian gravitational potential>.}

= Uniform gravitational field in an off-center spherical cavity
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{title2=$g=-4\pi G\rho c/3$}

Remove a spherical void centered at $c$ from a uniform sphere centered at the origin. At points inside the void, <superposition> combines the sphere's interior field $-4\pi G\rho x/3$ with the removed sphere's field $+4\pi G\rho(x-c)/3$. Their sum is the displayed constant <Newtonian gravitational field>. A second removed mass outside this void adds a nonuniform contribution.

= Cavity equilibrium destabilized by a second void
{parent=Uniform gravitational field in an off-center spherical cavity}

A uniform <Newtonian gravitational field> inside one spherical void can be cancelled at a point by the repulsive field representing another removed sphere. The latter field has a <Jacobian matrix> proportional to $I-3\hat r\hat r^T$: its axial eigenvalue is negative and both transverse eigenvalues positive. The equilibrium is restoring along the axis but unstable transversely. A zero force is thus not enough for stable rest.

= Newtonian gravitational stress tensor
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}
{title2=$T_{ij}$}

For a <Newtonian gravitational potential> obeying the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>, define $T_{ij}=[\partial_i\Phi\partial_j\Phi-\delta_{ij}|\nabla\Phi|^2/2]/(4\pi G)$. Commutation of mixed <partial derivatives> gives $\partial_jT_{ij}=\rho\partial_i\Phi$. Thus the gravitational force density can be put in conservative form as $-\partial_jT_{ij}$. The sign is chosen so that $T_{ij}$ is added to the momentum flux; reversing the stress convention reverses the divergence sign.

= Gravitational stress contribution to angular-momentum transport
{parent=Newtonian gravitational stress tensor}
{title2=$rT_{r\phi}$}

In cylindrical coordinates, the <Newtonian gravitational stress tensor> gives radial <angular-momentum flux> $rT_{r\phi}=r(\partial_r\Phi)(r^{-1}\partial_\phi\Phi)/(4\pi G)$. Nonaxisymmetric structure can correlate the field components and transport <angular momentum>. The isotropic negative-pressure part has zero off-diagonal component, so it gives no direct radial transport. This separates a shear flux from a contribution to normal force balance.

= Self-gravity
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

Self-gravity is the <gravitational acceleration> produced by a system's own <mass density>. It is obtained by solving the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> for that density, rather than treating the <Newtonian gravitational potential> as an externally fixed field. It competes with <pressure> and rotational support in <gravitational instability of an astrophysical disk>.

= Standard gravitational parameter
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{title2=$\mu$}
{wiki}

= Gravitational parameter
{synonym}

The product $\mu=GM$ of the <gravitational constant> and a central <mass> sets the strength of the exterior <Newtonian gravitational field>. Its dimensions are length cubed divided by time squared. In the exact relative <two-body problem>, the parameter is $G(M_1+M_2)$; using $GM$ for one source assumes the <test particle> approximation.

The combination is useful because orbital and falling-body observations constrain $\mu$ directly. A gravitational <circular orbit> of radius $a$ has <oscillation period> $T=2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}$, so measuring $a$ and $T$ gives $\mu=4\pi^2a^3/T^2$. That measurement alone does not separately determine $G$ and $M$. Similarly, <radial free fall from rest> has a natural time scale $\sqrt{r_0^3/\mu}$.

Other interactions require a different model. Inverse-square <radiation pressure> can reduce the effective attraction for a dust <Kepler orbit>; calling that effective coefficient a gravitational parameter must not hide the added <force>.

= Tidal tensor
{title2=$T_{jk}$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

The acceleration tidal tensor is $T_{jk}=\partial_k g_j=-\partial_j\partial_k\Phi$ for <gravitational acceleration> $\mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi$. It describes differential acceleration $\Delta\mathbf g\simeq T\Delta\mathbf r$. Some authors instead call the positive <Hessian matrix> $\partial_j\partial_k\Phi$ the tidal tensor, reversing this sign convention. For a point mass and the positive potential convention $\mathbf g=\nabla\psi$, the tensor is $Gm(3\mathbf R\mathbf R^{\mathsf T}/R^5-\mathbf1/R^3)$.

The <tidal tensor> is the <Jacobian matrix> of the <Newtonian gravitational field>, rather than the <gravitational acceleration> itself. Its entries have units $\mathrm{s^{-2}}$: multiplying by a <displacement> produces an <acceleration>. For a smooth <Newtonian gravitational potential>, the <tidal tensor> is a <symmetric matrix>, since mixed <partial derivatives> commute. A spatially uniform <gravitational acceleration> has zero <tidal tensor>, however large that <acceleration> is.

= Point-mass tidal stretching and squeezing
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{title2=$T=\mu(3\mathbf n\mathbf n^{\mathsf T}-I)/R^3$}

For a source <point mass> $M$ at the origin, let $\mathbf R=R\mathbf n$, where $\mathbf n$ is an outward <unit vector>, and let $\mu=GM$ be the source's <standard gravitational parameter>. Differentiating $g_i=-\mu R_i/R^3$ gives the <tidal tensor>
$$
T_{ij}=\frac{\mu}{R^3}(3n_i n_j-\delta_{ij}),\qquad T=\frac{\mu}{R^3}(3\mathbf n\mathbf n^{\mathsf T}-I),
$$
where $\delta_{ij}$ is the <Kronecker delta> and $I$ is the <identity matrix>. In an <orthonormal basis> with first axis radial, the <tidal tensor> is
$$
T=\frac{\mu}{R^3}\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\0&-1&0\\0&0&-1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The radial <eigenvalue> $2\mu/R^3$ separates nearby freely falling <test particles>; the two transverse <eigenvalues> $-\mu/R^3$ bring them together. The farther radial <test particle> falls inward less rapidly, so its <acceleration> relative to the nearer one points outward. Transverse <gravitational accelerations> converge toward the source. Thus an initially resting small cloud stretches radially and squeezes in both transverse directions. These are instantaneous relative <accelerations>; the subsequent shape also depends on initial <velocities> and the changing <tidal tensor> along the cloud's <free fall>.

Outside the source, the <matrix trace> is $2\mu/R^3-\mu/R^3-\mu/R^3=0$. This matches $\operatorname{tr}T=-\nabla^2\Phi=0$ in a region with zero <mass density>, by the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. Zero <matrix trace> means no leading change of a small initially resting cloud's <volume> at order $t^2$, not that each separation stays fixed. Inside matter, the same sign convention gives $\operatorname{tr}T=-4\pi G\rho$ instead.

= Quadrupolar point-mass tidal potential
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{title2=$\psi_{\mathrm{tid}}=\tfrac12r_iT_{ij}r_j$}

Subtracting the constant and uniform-acceleration terms from the point-mass <relative potential> gives $\psi_{\mathrm{tid}}=Gm_p[3(\mathbf r\cdot\widehat{\mathbf r}_p)^2-r^2]/(2r_p^3)$, with force convention $\nabla\psi$. Its <tidal tensor> is $Gm_p(3\widehat{\mathbf r}_p\widehat{\mathbf r}_p^T-I)/r_p^3$. This is the leading term for target size small compared with separation.

= Tidal disruption
{parent=Tidal tensor}

Disruption of a self-gravitating body when differential gravitational <acceleration> across it exceeds its binding <acceleration>. For a body of radius $R$, <mass> $m$ at distance $r$ from a much more massive body $M$, the competing scales are $GMR/r^3$ and $Gm/R^2$; their equality gives $r\sim R(M/m)^{1/3}$, up to geometry and internal-structure factors. The <tidal tensor> gives the directional differential <acceleration>. A <tidal disruption event> near a black hole is a specific astronomical application.

= Tidal force
{parent=Tidal tensor}
{wiki}

Differential gravitational <acceleration>, multiplied by the affected <mass>, across a spatially extended body. In the linear approximation the <acceleration> difference is $T\Delta\boldsymbol r$, where $T$ is the <tidal tensor>. Stellar <tidal forces> limit the domain of a <circumplanetary orbit>; sufficiently strong ones can produce <tidal disruption>.

To isolate a <tidal force>, subtract the <gravitational acceleration> shared by the body as a whole. Let a reference <test particle> follow $\ddot{\mathbf R}=\mathbf g(\mathbf R)$, and let another have <position> $\mathbf R+\mathbf x$. In a <translating accelerated reference frame> with parallel, nonrotating axes,
$$
\ddot{\mathbf x}=\mathbf g(\mathbf R+\mathbf x)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)=T(\mathbf R)\mathbf x+O\!\left(\frac{\mu|\mathbf x|^2}{R^4}\right)
$$
for a source <point mass>, when $|\mathbf x|/R\ll1$. Here $\mu=GM$ is its <standard gravitational parameter> and the last term uses <Big O notation>. This <Taylor expansion> removes the common inward pull and keeps the spatial variation. The near side has an excess pull toward the source; the far side has a deficit of inward pull, which is an outward relative <acceleration>. Both sides can stretch apart while both still accelerate toward the source.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I by Richard Feynman and others/Chapter 7: The Theory of Gravitation>[§7–4, discussion of stronger near-side and weaker far-side attraction]{ref}

\Image[tidal-field-difference-and-stretching.png]
{title=Subtracting common gravitational acceleration reveals radial stretching and transverse squeezing}
{description=Blue arrows show the exact <Newtonian gravitational field> at several <positions> near a reference point. Orange arrows show its linear residual, calculated with the <tidal tensor>. Arrow lengths use separate scales in the two panels; this is a comparison of direction and spatial variation, not two equal-magnitude fields.}

For a material element of <mass> $m$, the residual external <force> in this <translating accelerated reference frame> is $m[\mathbf g(\mathbf R+\mathbf x)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)]$. Actual deformation also depends on <elasticity>, <pressure> and <self-gravity>. A <rigid body> approximation suppresses deformation but can still have internal <forces> that resist the <tidal force>. The <point-mass tidal stretching and squeezing> pattern describes freely falling, noninteracting <test particles>, rather than promising the same shape for every solid, star or ocean. A <rotating reference frame> introduces <centrifugal acceleration> and <Coriolis acceleration>, which must be included separately.

= Exact tidal acceleration between separated masses
{parent=Tidal force}
{title2=$\Delta a=2\mu L/[R^3(1-L^2/(4R^2))^2]$}

Stipulate a fixed source <point mass> $M=10^{12}\,\mathrm{kg}$, two equal <test particles> of <mass> $m=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, and $G=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$. This is a chosen model, not a measurement of a particular object. Place the <test particles> on the outward radial axis at $R-L/2$ and $R+L/2$, with midpoint distance $R=100\,\mathrm m$ and separation $L=2\,\mathrm m$. Neglect their mutual attraction, other sources and all supporting <forces>.

Each signed radial <gravitational acceleration> is inward:
$$
a_-=-\frac{\mu}{(R-L/2)^2}\approx-0.0068098153\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}},\qquad a_+=-\frac{\mu}{(R+L/2)^2}\approx-0.0065427899\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}.
$$
Their separation has outward relative <acceleration>
$$
\ddot L=a_+-a_-=\mu\left[\frac{1}{(R-L/2)^2}-\frac{1}{(R+L/2)^2}\right]=\frac{2\mu L}{R^3}\left(1-\frac{L^2}{4R^2}\right)^{-2}.
$$
The exact value is $2.6702540\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$. The midpoint <tidal tensor> predicts $2\mu L/R^3=2.6697200\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$, an underestimate of $0.019999\%$ relative to the exact value. For these equal $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <masses>, the difference of external <forces> has the same numerical value in the <SI> unit of <force>, the <newton (unit)>. It is not an additional mutual <force> between the <test particles>.

The exact formula requires $L<2R$ and describes the instantaneous relative <acceleration>. Because the two sample <positions> are symmetric about the midpoint, the <Taylor expansion> error relative to the linear result starts at order $(L/R)^2$; a one-sided comparison with a reference point generally has relative error of order $L/R$. If either <test particle> enters an extended source, use that source's interior <Newtonian gravitational field> instead of extrapolating the singular <point-mass> model.

= Gravity magnitude versus tidal gradient
{parent=Tidal force}
{title2=$g=\mu/R^2,\quad \lambda_r=2\mu/R^3$}

For a fixed source <point mass>, the magnitude $g=\mu/R^2$ of the <gravitational acceleration> measures the local pull; the radial <eigenvalue> $\lambda_r=2\mu/R^3$ of the <tidal tensor> measures how that pull changes across space. Doubling $R$ divides $g$ by four but $\lambda_r$ by eight. For a fixed small radial separation $L$, the linear relative <acceleration> is $\Delta a\simeq\lambda_r L$, so
$$
\frac{\Delta a}{g}\simeq\frac{2L}{R}.
$$
A small body can therefore experience strong common <gravitational acceleration> with a comparatively small <tidal force>. A measurement of $g$ alone does not determine the <tidal tensor>: sources at different distances can have the same $g$ but different spatial variation.

\Image[tidal-gravity-distance-scaling.png]
{title=Gravity falls as inverse distance squared, while point-mass tidal stretching falls as inverse distance cubed}
{description=The left panel compares independently normalized <gravitational acceleration> and radial <tidal tensor> <eigenvalue> for a fixed source. The right panel compares exact and linear relative <accelerations> for two radially separated <test particles> at a fixed midpoint, showing the finite-separation correction as their separation grows.}

The local stretching time scale is $\lambda_r^{-1/2}=\sqrt{R^3/(2\mu)}$: it marks when an initially resting separation could change by order itself if the <tidal tensor> stayed fixed. During <free fall>, $R$ changes, so this scale is not a complete trajectory or a universal disruption time. <Tidal disruption> additionally compares the <tidal force> with binding by <self-gravity> and material <forces>; $g$ by itself supplies no such comparison.

= Love number
{c}
{title2=$k_2$}
{wiki}
{parent=Tidal force}

A Love number measures a body's tidal response to an applied potential. The degree-two potential response $k_2$ is sensitive to radial density structure and central concentration, and can complement mass and radius constraints on an <exoplanet interior>. Inferring it from orbital precession requires separating other contributions and assumptions about rotation and the tidal response.

= Gravity gradiometry
{parent=Tidal tensor}

<Gravity gradiometry> measures spatial differences of <gravitational acceleration>. Two simultaneous measurements along a <unit vector> $\mathbf e$, separated by $L\mathbf e$, give
$$
\frac{\mathbf e\cdot[\mathbf g(\mathbf R+L\mathbf e)-\mathbf g(\mathbf R)]}{L}\simeq\mathbf e^{\mathsf T}T\mathbf e.
$$
This measures one directional component of the <tidal tensor>, not its whole <matrix>. Any identical platform <acceleration> cancels in the difference. Unequal instrument responses, changing geometry and <rotating reference frames> can spoil that cancellation. For a large baseline, the quotient is an average of the directional <tidal tensor> along the baseline, rather than its exact value at one point.

= Atom-cloud gravity-gradient measurement
{parent=Gravity gradiometry}

A laboratory <gravity gradiometry> experiment used two vertically separated clouds of <chemical atoms> in <vacuum>, launched into <free fall>. Shared <laser> beams applied three pulses ($\pi/2,\pi,\pi/2$), splitting and recombining <matter waves>. Balanced <photodiodes> measured probe-light absorption to infer internal-state populations; scanning the last pulse's <wave phase> produced an <interference> fringe.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§I, pp. 033608-1–2; §II E 1, p. 033608-6; §II F 1, p. 033608-7]{ref}

The difference of fringe <wave phases> obeys $\delta\phi=\mathbf k_{\mathrm{eff}}\cdot\Delta\mathbf g\,\tau^2$, with effective <wave vector> $\mathbf k_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and pulse spacing $\tau$: shared reference motion cancels, leaving differential <gravitational acceleration>. Eight lead bricks, each approximately $12.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, were placed symmetrically around the lower chamber, about $0.2\,\mathrm m$ from the fountain apex. The measured signal was $(8.1\pm2.1)\times10^{-9}g$, versus a calculated $8.2\times10^{-9}g$, where $g$ denotes terrestrial <gravitational acceleration>.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§I, p. 033608-2; §III A 2, p. 033608-10, mass detection]{ref}

Controls deliberately shook the shared reference reflector and compared actively isolated and unisolated operation. Tilting tests exposed sensitivity to <Coriolis acceleration> and <centrifugal acceleration>. Thus shared translation is rejectable, while rotation and finite measurement uncertainty remain limitations. The brick signal detects spatially varying <gravity>; it does not establish a precision value of the <gravitational constant>.<Sensitive absolute-gravity gradiometry using atom interferometry by J. M. McGuirk and others>[§III A, p. 033608-9; §III B, pp. 033608-10–11]{ref}

= Quadrupolar tidal forcing
{parent=Tidal tensor}

For a point-mass companion at separation $a$, subtract the constant and linear terms of its <Newtonian gravitational potential> in a frame accelerating with the target's center. The leading residual is
$$
\Phi_{\mathrm{tide}}=-\frac{GM_2}{2a^3}\left[3(\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf r)^2-r^2\right].
$$
It is degree two in <spherical harmonics>. In a circular orbit with angular frequency $\omega_o$, its time-dependent $m=\pm2$ components force at $2\omega_o$ in a frame with no spin; there is also a static $m=0$ component. This is the leading approximation in $r/a$, and the tidal amplitude may be absorbed into a harmonic-dependent constant.

= Gravitational acceleration
{title2=$g$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{wiki}

= Newtonian gravity
{synonym}
{c}

Gravitational acceleration is the <acceleration> of a <test particle> in <free fall> in a <Newtonian gravitational field>. It is a vector pointing along that field; $g$ commonly denotes its positive magnitude. Near Earth's surface, the magnitude is approximately constant over shallow vertical scales. For a fixed spherical source it instead varies as $g(r)=\mu/r^2$, where $\mu$ is the <standard gravitational parameter>.

This is distinct from <gravitational weight>, the <force> $m\mathbf g$, and from <apparent weight>, the support force a scale measures. An orbiting body can have substantial <gravitational acceleration> while its <apparent weight> is zero.

= Newtonian central configuration
{c}
{parent=Gravitational acceleration}
{title2=$\nabla_iW=\Lambda m_i\mathbf a_i$}

A collision-free positive-mass configuration satisfies the displayed equation when every gravitational acceleration points proportionally toward a common centre. With $W=-\sum_{i<j}Gm_im_j/|\mathbf a_i-\mathbf a_j|$, homogeneity gives $\Lambda I=-W>0$, $I=\sum_i m_i|\mathbf a_i|^2$. Pairwise force cancellation implies $\sum_i m_i\mathbf a_i=0$. Such a configuration generates a nonrotating <homothetic gravitational motion> by multiplying all position vectors by one positive scale factor.

= Homothetic gravitational motion
{parent=Newtonian central configuration}
{title2=$\ddot a=-\Lambda/a^2$}

For a <Newtonian central configuration>, set $\mathbf x_i(t)=a(t)\mathbf a_i$ with $a>0$. The gravitational force scales as $a^{-2}$, reducing all equations to the displayed scalar <ordinary differential equation>. Its first integral is $\dot a^2/2-\Lambda/a=k/2$. The total <energy> is $kI/2$, so the scalar energy constant exactly tracks the many-body energy for the fixed shape.

= Gravitational constant
{title2=$G$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{wiki}

The <gravitational constant> $G$ sets the strength of <Newton's law of universal gravitation>. Two <point masses> $M$ and $m$ a center-to-center distance $r$ apart attract with <force> magnitude
$$
F=\frac{GMm}{r^2}.
$$
For fixed <masses> and separation, doubling $G$ would double the <force>. For bodies with <spherical symmetry>, the same exterior formula follows from the <spherical shell theorem>; for arbitrary extended bodies, add the attractions of their <mass density> elements instead. The <gravitational constant> connects a laboratory attraction to the <Newtonian gravitational field> controlling a <Kepler orbit>.

The <gravitational constant> $G$ and local <gravitational acceleration> $g$ are different quantities. Applying <Newton's second law> to a <test particle> gives $g=F/m=GM/r^2$ for one spherical source. The source's <mass> and the observer's <position> change $g$; $G$ remains the same parameter of the law. A local falling-body experiment therefore measures a field, not $G$ alone. On rotating <Earth>, an instrument fixed to the surface also responds to <centrifugal acceleration>, while surrounding <mass density> and altitude change the gravitational contribution.

<Dimensional analysis> explains the units. Denote the base <physical dimensions> of <length>, <mass> and <time> by $\mathsf L,\mathsf M,\mathsf T$. Since <force> has dimension $\mathsf M\mathsf L\mathsf T^{-2}$,
$$
[G]=\frac{[F][r]^2}{[M][m]}=\mathsf L^3\mathsf M^{-1}\mathsf T^{-2},\qquad G\text{ has SI unit }\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}.
$$
By contrast, $[g]=\mathsf L\mathsf T^{-2}$ and the <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu=GM$ has dimension $\mathsf L^3\mathsf T^{-2}$. A small numerical value of a dimensional constant depends on the units chosen: it is the resulting <force> for specified physical <masses> and separation that conveys the laboratory scale.

NIST's current complete listing gives
$$
G=6.67430(15)\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}
$$
for the \i[2022 CODATA adjustment], the latest adjustment listed when checked on 14 October 2026. Parentheses indicate a <standard uncertainty> $u(G)=0.00015\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$, approximately $22$ parts per million relative to $G$. The adjustment year is 2022; NIST's May 2024 data update is a different date.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[2022 CODATA adjustment, Newtonian constant of gravitation row: value, uncertainty and units]{ref} <Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>[Current adjustment heading, next-adjustment notice and data-content update]{ref}

For an original scale calculation, stipulate two $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point masses> separated by $1\,\mathrm m$ and insert this recommended $G$: $F=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm N$. The corresponding <acceleration> of either <mass> is $6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}$ at that instant. Reducing the separation to $0.10\,\mathrm m$ multiplies both by $100$. These are ideal model predictions, not observations; their tiny scale explains why stray <forces> matter.

Being a constant of a physical law does not make the numerical value exact. The <SI> fixes the numerical values of seven defining constants, including the <speed of light> and <Planck constant>; $G$ is determined experimentally. Exact unit definitions still require physical realizations with <measurement uncertainty>: calibrating a <length> or a <mass> does not become error-free.<The International System of Units (SI), ninth edition, SI Brochure>[English §§1.3 and 2.2–2.2.1, printed pp. 122–124: realization, seven defining constants and exact numerical values]{ref}

= Gravitational mass degeneracy
{parent=Gravitational constant}
{title2=$\mu=GM$}

An <orbit> around an isolated source constrains its <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu=GM$ rather than independently measuring the source's <mass> and the <gravitational constant>. In the <test particle> approximation, a <circular orbit> of radius $a$ and <orbital period> $T$ satisfies
$$
\frac{4\pi^2a}{T^2}=\frac{GM}{a^2},\qquad \mu=\frac{4\pi^2a^3}{T^2}.
$$
The left equation equates the required <centripetal acceleration> to the <gravitational acceleration>. More generally, a <Kepler orbit> depends on the same $\mu$. In the exact relative <two-body problem>, replace $GM$ by $G(M+m)$.

For any positive dimensionless number $q$, replacing $G$ by $qG$ and $M$ by $M/q$ leaves $GM$ and every predicted <test particle> <trajectory> unchanged. This is the <gravitational mass degeneracy>: more observations of the same <orbit> can refine $GM$ without separating its factors. A laboratory source whose <mass> is calibrated independently supplies the missing information.

Consequently an astronomical <mass> expressed in kilograms as $M=\mu/G$ inherits <measurement uncertainty> from $G$, even if $\mu$ is known much more precisely. To first order,
$$
\frac{\delta M}{M}=\frac{\delta\mu}{\mu}-\frac{\delta G}{G}.
$$
When the relative <standard uncertainty> of $\mu$ is negligible, that of $M$ is approximately $u(G)/G$. Conversely, the ratio of two source <masses> inferred using the same $G$ is $M_1/M_2=\mu_1/\mu_2$, so this common factor cancels. That distinction explains how excellent <orbit determination> can coexist with less precise absolute astronomical <masses>.

= Gravitational constant measurement sensitivity
{parent=Gravitational constant}
{title2=$G_{\mathrm{inferred}}=Fr^2/(Mm)$}

A laboratory determination of the <gravitational constant> must connect a sensor's reading to a gravitational signal from independently characterized <masses>. For an ideal two-<point-mass> <force> measurement, invert the model:
$$
G_{\mathrm{inferred}}=\frac{Fr^2}{Mm},\qquad \frac{\delta G}{G}\simeq\frac{\delta F}{F}+2\frac{\delta r}{r}-\frac{\delta M}{M}-\frac{\delta m}{m}.
$$
These are signed small changes obtained by taking the <logarithmic derivative>, not an instruction to add <standard uncertainties> with signs. For independent small input uncertainties, linear <propagation of uncertainty> instead gives
$$
\left(\frac{u(G)}G\right)^2\simeq\left(\frac{u(F)}F\right)^2+4\left(\frac{u(r)}r\right)^2+\left(\frac{u(M)}M\right)^2+\left(\frac{u(m)}m\right)^2.
$$
Correlated inputs require their <covariances> too. In particular, common geometric calibration errors must not be treated as independent simply because several distances were recorded.

For a concrete model check, hold the actual <force> and <masses> fixed but overestimate a true $r=0.100\,\mathrm m$ by $1\,\mathrm{\mu m}$. The exact inferred ratio is
$$
\frac{G_{\mathrm{inferred}}}{G_{\mathrm{true}}}=(1+10^{-5})^2=1.0000200001.
$$
The resulting bias is $20.0001$ parts per million, already comparable with the relative <standard uncertainty> of the recommended <gravitational constant>. A sensor can resolve very small changes while its inferred answer remains limited by source geometry. The example concerns one idealized separation; it is not an uncertainty estimate for any actual experiment.

\Image[gravitational-constant-scale-and-sensitivity.png]
{title=Weak laboratory attraction and the geometric sensitivity of an inferred gravitational constant}
{description=Left: ideal <force> between two stipulated $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <point masses> using the 2022 recommended <gravitational constant>. Right: exact model bias when only the separation entered in the inference changes. These curves are calculations, not measurement data.}

Making the source <mass> larger or bringing it closer strengthens the signal, but also makes detailed <mass density> and geometry important. Nearby apparatus supplies its own <Newtonian gravitational field>; electromagnetic shielding does not remove that attraction. Sensor offsets, changing <temperature>, vibration and source motion can imitate or alter a signal. The <Cavendish experiment> illustrates calibration of a <torsion constant>; a different sensor requires its own calibrated response and a full source model.

Repeating readings can reduce independent <measurement noise>, but a fixed scale error survives averaging. A useful experiment deliberately changes the source configuration, checks whether the signal follows the predicted change, and characterizes what moves with the source. A <systematic error> that changes with that configuration also survives background subtraction. Comparing methods with different sensors and different dominant errors is therefore valuable.

= Cold-atom measurement of the gravitational constant
{parent=Gravitational constant}

In 2014, <Gabriele Rosi> and colleagues used <atom interferometry>: two <rubidium>-87 clouds prepared by <laser cooling> in <vacuum>, $328\,\mathrm{mm}$ apart, received shared three-pulse <Raman transitions> spaced by $160\,\mathrm{ms}$. <Fluorescence> readout and paired-fringe <ellipse> fitting yielded differential <wave phase>. Twenty-four <tungsten> alloy cylinders, about $516\,\mathrm{kg}$ total, moved between C and F. Cloud subtraction rejected shared vibration; configuration subtraction isolated the source change.<Precision Measurement of the Newtonian Gravitational Constant Using Cold Atoms by Gabriele Rosi and others>[Main text pp. 3–5; Methods pp. 13–14; Fig. 1 caption p. 21]{ref}

\Image[gravitational-constant-atom-interferometer.png]
{title=Cold-atom measurement: two clouds and two source configurations}
{description=Original conceptual scheme; selected cylinders represent surrounding arrays. Geometry is schematic.<Precision Measurement of the Newtonian Gravitational Constant Using Cold Atoms by Gabriele Rosi and others>[Main text pp. 3–5 and Fig. 1 caption p. 21]{ref}}

Calibration used timed flight and light-sheet crossings; mirror tilt compensated <Earth> rotation. <Wave vector> reversal rejected unwanted shifts. Modeling included source <Newtonian gravitational potential>, atom <trajectories>, cloud distributions and moving supports. Source positioning, <mass density> variation and cloud geometry limited the reported combined relative <standard uncertainty> to $150$ parts per million.<Precision Measurement of the Newtonian Gravitational Constant Using Cold Atoms by Gabriele Rosi and others>[Main text pp. 5–6; Methods pp. 14–18; Table 1, p. 27]{ref}

To understand the subtraction without reproducing that full analysis, consider an ideal <atom interferometer> in uniform vertical <acceleration> during each pulse sequence. Let $k_{\mathrm{eff}}$ be its signed effective vertical <wave vector> component and $T$ the pulse spacing. Its acceleration-dependent <wave phase> is $\phi\simeq k_{\mathrm{eff}}gT^2$; labels U and L denote upper and lower clouds. The double difference is then
$$
S=(\phi_U-\phi_L)_C-(\phi_U-\phi_L)_F\simeq k_{\mathrm{eff}}T^2[(g_U-g_L)_C-(g_U-g_L)_F].
$$
A background field identical in C and F cancels even if it differs between the two clouds. In a fixed-geometry, linear-response model, write $S=G\mathcal K$, where $\mathcal K$ is calculated from the source <mass density>, calibrated pulse sequence and atom <trajectories>. Thus $G_{\mathrm{inferred}}=S/\mathcal K$. Measuring <wave phase> accurately is one part of the task; calculating $\mathcal K$ accurately is another. The real nonuniform field and finite clouds require the trajectory-dependent analysis described above rather than this uniform-field approximation.

= Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity
{c}
{title2=$\nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

For <mass density> $\rho$, the <Newtonian gravitational potential> satisfies the <Poisson equation> $\nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho$. The gravitational acceleration is $-\nabla\Phi$. An isolated system also requires the exterior vacuum solution and decay at infinity; solving the interior equation alone does not fix a globally physical field.

= Dyadic spherical shells with inverse-radius density scaling
{parent=Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity}
{title2=$M(2^{-N})=M(1)\,4^{-N}$}

Suppose the shell $2^{-n}<r<2^{1-n}$ has <mass density> $2^{n-1}\rho_0$. Its mass is $(14\pi\rho_0/3)4^{-n}$, by integrating $4\pi r^2\rho\,dr$. Summing the <geometric series> gives total mass $14\pi\rho_0/9$. At $r=2^{-N}$ the enclosed shells have indices $n\geq N+1$, so the enclosed mass is the total mass times $r^2$. The magnitude of the <Newtonian gravitational field> at every such boundary is therefore the same, $14\pi G\rho_0/9$. This does not assert that the field is constant throughout each shell.

= Newtonian gravitational potential
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}

The <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi$ is <potential energy> per unit <mass> of a <test particle>. Its <gradient> determines the <Newtonian gravitational field>:
$$
U=m\Phi,\qquad \mathbf g=-\nabla\Phi,\qquad \mathbf F=-\nabla U=-m\nabla\Phi.
$$
In <SI>, $U$ has units $\mathrm J$, while $\Phi$ has units $\mathrm{J\,kg^{-1}}=\mathrm{m^2\,s^{-2}}$; its <gradient> has units of <acceleration>, not <force>. Adding a constant to $\Phi$ leaves the <Newtonian gravitational field> unchanged. For an isolated <point mass>, the convenient reference is $\Phi(\infty)=0$. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/16-07-dynamics-fall-2009/0bb19205a6316ba88f88284bf7257ec6_MIT16_07F09_Lec16.pdf[Peraire and Widnall, MIT Dynamics, Lecture 16: potential per unit mass]{ref}

Increasing the radius from $r_0$ to $r_1>r_0$ raises the <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>:
$$
\Delta U=GMm\left(\frac1{r_0}-\frac1{r_1}\right)>0.
$$
An external agent supplies this positive <work> if the initial and final <kinetic energies> agree; gravity does <work> $-\Delta U$. For $r_1=2r_0$, the required <work> is $GMm/(2r_0)$. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>{ref}

= Planetary quadrupole coefficient
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$J_2$}

The dimensionless coefficient $J_2$ describes the leading axisymmetric departure of a planet's external <Newtonian gravitational potential> from a monopole: $\Phi=-GM_p/r+[GM_pJ_2R_p^2/r^3]P_2(\cos\vartheta)$, where $\vartheta$ is colatitude from the spin axis. An oblate mass distribution has positive $J_2$. The <disturbing function> is minus the nonspherical potential term. On a circular inclined <Kepler orbit>, $\cos\vartheta=\sin I\sin M$, so <orbit averaging> gives $\overline{\mathcal R}_{J_2}=GM_pJ_2R_p^2a^{-3}(1/2-3\sin^2I/4)$. The exact circular nodal rate is $-3nJ_2(R_p/a)^2\cos I/2$; its small-tilt limit is $-3nJ_2(R_p/a)^2/2$.

= Oblate-quadrupole epicyclic precession
{parent=Planetary quadrupole coefficient}
{title2=$\kappa^2=n^2(1-3\alpha^2/R^2),\quad\nu^2=n^2(1+9\alpha^2/R^2)$}

For $\Phi=-\mu/r+\alpha^2\mu(3z^2-r^2)/r^5$, a nearly circular equatorial orbit has $n^2=\mu/R^3$ and azimuthal frequency $\Omega_c^2=n^2(1+3\alpha^2/R^2)$. The <radial epicyclic frequency> is $\kappa^2=R\,d\Omega_c^2/dR+4\Omega_c^2$, and the <vertical epicyclic frequency> is $\nu^2=\partial_z^2\Phi|_{z=0}$. Radial stability requires $R^2>3\alpha^2$. The <nodal precession> rate $\Omega_c-\nu$ is negative and the <apsidal precession> rate $\Omega_c-\kappa$ positive. These sign statements concern small eccentricity and inclination. For an arbitrary inclined orbit, the averaged <argument of periapsis> instead evolves as $(3/2)n\alpha^2[5\cos^2i-1]/[a^2(1-e^2)^2]$ and can regress.

= Axisymmetric logarithmic gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=\tfrac12V^2\log(R^2+z^2/q^2)$}

An axisymmetric logarithmic <Newtonian gravitational potential> has spheroidal equipotential surfaces and a <flat galaxy rotation curve>: $R\Phi_R(R,0)=V^2$. The singular model is defined away from the origin; a fixed reference length makes the logarithm dimensionless. Its central $r^{-2}$ <mass density> cusp is locally integrable, but its total <mass> diverges at large radius.

= Density positivity for an axisymmetric logarithmic potential
{parent=Axisymmetric logarithmic gravitational potential}
{title2=$q^2\geq1/2$}

The <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> gives
$$
\rho=\frac{V^2}{4\pi Gq^2}\frac{R^2+(2-q^{-2})z^2}{(R^2+q^{-2}z^2)^2}.
$$
For $V>0$, nonnegative <mass density> away from the origin is equivalent to $q^2\geq1/2$. Strict positivity requires $q^2>1/2$. This condition does not by itself guarantee a nonnegative <galactic distribution function>.

= Power-law gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=-Ar^{-\beta}$}

A radial <Newtonian gravitational potential> $\Phi=-Ar^{-\beta}$ produces inward acceleration $A\beta r^{-\beta-1}$. The point-mass case has $\beta=1$ and $A=GM$. Treating the <power-law gravitational potential> as prescribed does not require it to be sourced by the flowing gas.

= Gravitational quadrupole potential of a point-mass binary
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi_2=-G\mu[3(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf r)^2-x^2r^2]/(2x^5)$}

For a <binary star> viewed far from its <centre of mass>, let $\mathbf r$ be the separation and $\mu$ the <reduced mass>, $M=M_1+M_2$ the total <mass>, $r=|\mathbf r|$ and $x=|\mathbf x|$. The <Taylor expansion> of the point-mass <Newtonian gravitational potential> has zero dipole because $\sum_A M_A\mathbf x_A=0$. Its second moment is $\sum_A M_Ax_{Ai}x_{Aj}=\mu r_i r_j$, so
$$
\Phi=-\frac{GM}{x}-\frac{G\mu}{2x^5}\left[3(\mathbf x\cdot\mathbf r)^2-x^2r^2\right]+O(GMr^3/x^4).
$$
This holds for an instantaneous separation in any orbit, not only a <circular orbit>. The <mass quadrupole moment> convention $Q_{ij}=\mu(r_ir_j-r^2\delta_{ij}/3)$ gives $\Phi_2=-3GQ_{ij}x_ix_j/(2x^5)$.

= Flattened power-law gravitational potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{title2=$\Phi=\Phi_0[R_0/(R^2+\lambda^2z^2)^{1/2}]^\beta$}

A flattened power-law <Newtonian gravitational potential> has equipotential spheroids $R^2+\lambda^2z^2=\text{constant}$. For $\Phi_0<0$ and $0<\beta<1$, its midplane <circular orbit> angular frequency satisfies $\Omega^2=-\beta\Phi_0R_0^\beta R^{-\beta-2}$.

= Density positivity for a flattened power-law potential
{parent=Flattened power-law gravitational potential}

Applying the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity> to a <flattened power-law gravitational potential> yields
$$
\rho=-\frac{\beta\Phi_0R_0^\beta}{4\pi G}\frac{(\lambda^2-\beta)R^2+\lambda^2[2-(\beta+1)\lambda^2]z^2}{(R^2+\lambda^2z^2)^{(\beta+4)/2}}.
$$
For $0<\beta<1$, nonnegative <mass density> everywhere outside the origin requires and is ensured by $\beta\leq\lambda^2\leq2/(\beta+1)$. The central density singularity is locally integrable and carries no point mass, although the total mass diverges at large radius in this scale-free model.

= Newtonian potential of a point mass
{title2=$\Phi(r)=-GM/r$}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}
{c}

= Gravitational potential
{synonym}

The <Newtonian potential of a point mass> $M$ at the origin is
$$
\Phi(r)=-\frac{GM}{r},\qquad r>0,\qquad \Phi(\infty)=0.
$$
It is the free-space <Green function of the Laplacian> with the normalization of the <Poisson equation for Newtonian gravity>. Differentiating gives inward <gravitational acceleration> $-GM/r^2$. With $z=r/r_0$, the normalized <Newtonian gravitational potential> is $\Phi/(GM/r_0)=-1/z$; at $z=1,2,4$ it equals $-1,-1/2,-1/4$.

For a radially outward launch from $r_0$, <conservation of mechanical energy> in the fixed-source <test particle> approximation gives the minimum <escape velocity>:
$$
\frac{E}{m}=\frac{v_0^2}{2}-\frac{GM}{r_0}=0
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{r_0}}.
$$
At this threshold the <speed> tends to zero only at infinite separation. The model neglects air resistance, external <tidal forces>, and <general relativity>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.3 Gravitational Potential Energy and Total Energy>{ref}

A <circular orbit> at $r_0$ instead requires tangential <speed> $v_c=\sqrt{GM/r_0}$. Choosing units with $G=M=r_0=1$ gives $g_0=1$, $v_c=1$, and $v_{\mathrm{esc}}=\sqrt2\simeq1.41421$. A radial launch at <speed> $1$ reaches $r=2$ and returns; the same <speed> directed tangentially gives a <circular orbit>. <University Physics Volume 1 by William Moebs and others/13.4 Satellite Orbits and Energy>{ref}

\Image[gravitational-escape-energy.png]
{title=Energy balance and outward radial speeds below, at, and above escape}
{description=Left: at the <escape velocity>, positive <kinetic energy> exactly balances negative <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>. Right: radial launches at normalized <speeds> $1$, $\sqrt2$, and $1.6$ respectively turn at $r/r_0=2$, approach zero <speed> at infinity, and retain a positive limiting <speed>. The plotted trajectories use a fixed source and the <test particle> approximation.}

= Spherically symmetric potential
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential}

A spherically symmetric potential depends only on distance $r$ from one point. Its force is central, so angular momentum is conserved.

= Newtonian gravitational potential energy
{c}
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}

Two point masses $m_1,m_2$ separated by distance $r$ have <potential energy>
$$
V(r)=-\frac{Gm_1m_2}{r}.
$$
The corresponding attractive force is $-\nabla V$.

This is the Newtonian point-mass specialization of <gravitational energy>, with the reference value chosen at infinite separation.

= Spherical binding-energy mass integral
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential energy}
{title2=$W=-\dfrac G2\int_0^\infty M(r)^2r^{-2}\,dr$}

Assembling a spherical mass distribution in shells gives <gravitational potential energy> $W=-G\int M(r)\,dM(r)/r$. Integration by parts gives the displayed identity when $M(r)^2/r$ vanishes at both endpoints. It includes each interacting pair once; no extra factor of one half belongs in the original shell-assembly integral.

= Minimum-separation binding-energy bounds
{parent=Newtonian gravitational potential energy}
{title2=$B_0\leq rU\leq A_0$}

For positive <masses>, let $r$ be the actual minimum pairwise separation and use positive binding <energy> $U=\sum_{j<k}Gm_jm_k/r_{jk}$. If $m_{(1)},m_{(2)}$ are the smallest <masses>, then $Gm_{(1)}m_{(2)}\leq rU\leq G\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k$. Thus $U^{-1}$ is comparable to $r$ with constants depending only on the masses.

= Negative-energy minimum-separation upper bound
{parent=Minimum-separation binding-energy bounds}
{title2=$r\leq A_0/|E|$}

With $E=T-U<0$ and $T\geq0$, the positive binding <energy> satisfies $U\geq|E|$. The <minimum-separation binding-energy bounds> imply $r\leq A_0/|E|$. At least one pair stays close; this does not bound every particle's distance or exclude a <negative-energy gravitational collision>.

= Cavendish experiment
{parent=Newtonian gravitational field}
{c}
{wiki}

The <Cavendish experiment> makes the attraction predicted by <Newton's law of universal gravitation> measurable between laboratory <masses>. Two small test <masses> hang at the ends of a horizontal rod in a <Cavendish torsion balance>; nearby larger source <masses> exert a tiny <torque>. A thin suspension fiber supplies an opposing <torque> as it twists. Measuring its twist and calibrating its <torsion constant> lets an experimenter infer the gravitational <force> and, in a modern analysis, the <gravitational constant>. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, Physics 258/259, The Cavendish Experiment: apparatus and equations of motion, pp. 1–3]{ref}

The beautiful feature is that the two horizontal <forces> can cancel as vectors while their <torques> add. The instrument converts weak attraction into a rotation, with sensitivity set by the fiber's <torsion constant>. Earth's much larger downward <gravitational weight> is supported by the suspension rather than supplying the intended <torque> about its vertical axis. A laboratory measurement can therefore probe the same <Newtonian gravitational field> that governs the <two-body problem>.

Henry Cavendish's original paper was \i[Experiments to determine the Density of the Earth], published in \i[Philosophical Transactions], volume 88 (1798), pp. 469–526, and read on 21 June 1798. On p. 469 he credits John Michell with the apparatus idea and explains that Michell died before using it; the instrument reached Cavendish through Francis John Hyde Wollaston. Cavendish's stated aim was Earth's mean <mass density>. Calling the experiment a determination of the <gravitational constant> is a modern interpretation, not Cavendish's own formulation of his result. https://www.famaf.unc.edu.ar/~wolf/FisicaI/Phil.%20Trans.%20R.%20Soc.%20Lond.-1798-Cavendish-rstl.1798.0022.pdf[Cavendish (1798), original paper, opening p. 469; DOI 10.1098/rstl.1798.0022]{ref}

The connection between Earth's mean <mass density> and the <gravitational constant> follows in a spherical, nonrotating model of <Earth>. With Earth <mass> $M_E$, radius $R_E$, and surface <gravitational acceleration> $g$, the <spherical shell theorem> and <Newton's law of universal gravitation> give
$$
g=\frac{GM_E}{R_E^2},\qquad \overline\rho_E=\frac{3M_E}{4\pi R_E^3},\qquad G=\frac{3g}{4\pi R_E\overline\rho_E}.
$$
Thus a local comparison of gravitational attractions, together with knowledge of Earth's size and surface <gravitational acceleration>, reaches a property of the entire planet. These are modern equations explaining the connection, not equations quoted from Cavendish's paper.

For a worked example, use an explicitly simplified two-near-pair model. Each test <point mass> is $m$, each source <point mass> is $M$, and each <lever arm> is $L$, so the rod's full <length> is $2L$. Neglect the rod's <mass>. At zero twist put the test <masses> at $(-L,0)$ and $(L,0)$, and the sources at $(-L,-r)$ and $(L,r)$. Here $r$ is the near-pair center separation. Ignore each source's attraction on the test <mass> at the opposite end. Evaluate the two near-pair <forces> at zero twist and treat their <torque> as constant for a small twist, requiring $L|\theta|\ll r$. This is a teaching model, not a precision model of Cavendish's actual apparatus.

By <Newton's law of universal gravitation>, each retained <force> has magnitude $F=GmM/r^2$. The left <force> points down and the right <force> points up. Using <torque> about the suspension axis, both contributions are counterclockwise when viewed from above:
$$
\tau_{z,\mathrm{left}}=(-L)(-F)=LF,\qquad \tau_{z,\mathrm{right}}=LF,\qquad |\tau_g|=2L\frac{GmM}{r^2}.
$$
The net retained horizontal <force> is zero, yet the net <torque> is positive. Moving the sources to $(-L,r)$ and $(L,-r)$ reverses both <forces> and hence the <torque>. In a real apparatus, the opposite source also attracts each test <mass>; that correction is explicitly included in the university derivation. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, p. 2, equation (1): opposite-source correction]{ref}

\Image[cavendish-torsion-balance-schematic.png]
{title=Two near-pair forces give the same counterclockwise torque about a vertical suspension fiber}

For illustrative parameters, take $m=0.010\,\mathrm{kg}$, $M=1.0\,\mathrm{kg}$, $r=0.050\,\mathrm m$, $L=0.10\,\mathrm m$, and <torsion constant> $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. Use $G=6.67430\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$, the central value in NIST's 2022 CODATA table; that table gives standard uncertainty $0.00015\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{m^3\,kg^{-1}\,s^{-2}}$. This is an input for the example, not a measurement made with these assumed parameters. <Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>{ref}
$$
F=\frac{(6.67430\times10^{-11})(0.010)(1.0)}{(0.050)^2}=2.66972\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm N,\qquad |\tau_g|=2(0.10)F=5.33944\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm{N\,m}.
$$
This sub-nanonewton <force> produces a measurable twist in the <Cavendish torsion balance>. The displayed digits make the toy calculation reproducible; they do not describe experimental precision.

= Cavendish torsion balance
{parent=Cavendish experiment}
{c}

A <Cavendish torsion balance> is a <torsion balance> arranged to measure gravitational <torque> from nearby source <masses>. Its suspended horizontal rod rotates about a vertical fiber. In the linear elastic regime, a twist <angle> $\theta$, measured from the fiber's untwisted orientation, gives a restoring <torque> $\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta$, where $\kappa>0$ is the <torsion constant>. The minus sign is essential: the fiber resists the twist. Under an approximately constant gravitational <torque>, the rotational equation using <moment of inertia> $I$ and <angular acceleration> $\ddot\theta$ is
$$
I\ddot\theta=\tau_g-\kappa\theta,\qquad \theta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{\tau_g}{\kappa}.
$$
At <equilibrium>, the two <torques> balance. The <angle> is a displacement from the fiber's reference orientation, not the largest excursion during an <oscillation>. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, pp. 2–3: restoring torque and equilibrium deflection]{ref}

For the toy parameters in the <Cavendish experiment>, the predicted <equilibrium> twist is
$$
\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\frac{5.33944\times10^{-11}}{1.0\times10^{-8}}=0.00533944\,\mathrm{rad}\simeq0.3059^\circ.
$$
Here $L|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|/r\simeq0.0107$, consistent with using a small geometric displacement in the toy model, but not an estimate of its total <measurement error>. Reversing the source positions ideally takes the <equilibrium> <angle> from $+\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$ to $-\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$: the magnitude of the change is $2|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|\simeq0.6119^\circ$. A stable zero offset then cancels in the difference. A real reading may track a reflected light spot; the <law of reflection> doubles a mirror's rotation in the outgoing beam, so the spot's angular change must not be confused with the rod's <angle>. https://sciencedemonstrations.fas.harvard.edu/presentations/cavendish-experiment[Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations, Cavendish Experiment: mirror readout and source reversal]{ref}

How can the fiber's <torsion constant> be found without already knowing the gravitational <force>? Measure the <oscillation period> and calculate the <moment of inertia>. For the same toy rod, neglecting its <mass> and treating both test <masses> as <point masses>, $I=mL^2+mL^2=2mL^2$. With the source <torque> absent or treated as constant, let $\phi=\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}$ be a small departure from <equilibrium>. Neglect <damping> and any change of gravitational <torque> with <angle>. The rotational equation becomes <simple harmonic motion>:
$$
I\ddot\phi+\kappa\phi=0,\qquad \phi(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\delta),\qquad \omega=\sqrt{\frac{\kappa}{I}},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac I\kappa},\qquad \kappa=\frac{4\pi^2I}{T^2}.
$$
Here $A$ is the angular amplitude, $\delta$ specifies the initial phase, and $\omega$ is the <angular frequency>. A softer fiber gives a larger <equilibrium> twist but also a longer <oscillation period>: greater sensitivity costs observation time. An undamped <oscillation> does not settle at <equilibrium>; <damping> is needed for settling. https://www.phys.uconn.edu/~hamilton/phys258/N/cav.pdf[University of Connecticut, The Cavendish Experiment, p. 3, equation (7): period calibration neglecting damping]{ref}

The toy <moment of inertia> and undamped <oscillation period> are
$$
I=2(0.010)(0.10)^2=2.0\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2},\qquad T=2\pi\sqrt{\frac{2.0\times10^{-4}}{1.0\times10^{-8}}}\simeq888.5766\,\mathrm s\simeq14.81\,\mathrm{min}.
$$
Timing this <oscillation period> would recover the assumed <torsion constant>. The units check explains the result: <torque> divided by <torsion constant> is an <angle> in <radians>, and $I/\kappa$ has units of <time> squared when <radians> are treated as dimensionless in <SI>.

\Image[torsion-balance-period-calibration.png]
{title=Undamped small torsional oscillations calibrate the restoring torque: a softer fiber has a longer period}

Combining the <equilibrium> twist with the period calibration, the two-near-pair model would infer the <gravitational constant> as
$$
G=\frac{\kappa|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|r^2}{2LmM}=\frac{4\pi^2Lr^2|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|}{MT^2}.
$$
The test <mass> cancels in the second expression because it enters both the gravitational <torque> and the toy <moment of inertia>. If the measured quantity is the magnitude $|\Delta\theta|$ of the change between opposite source positions, use $|\theta_{\mathrm{eq}}|=|\Delta\theta|/2$. Substituting the toy twist and <oscillation period> returns the input $G$; it is a consistency check, not independent experimental evidence.

The sensitivity of a <Cavendish torsion balance> also explains its practical difficulty. Air motion can exert unwanted <torque>; unequal <temperatures> can drive that motion. Cavendish discusses this effect on p. 471 and describes observing from outside a closed room to avoid disturbing the instrument. https://www.infinitoteatrodelcosmo.it/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/cavendish-1997-xxi-experiments-to-determine-the-density-of-the-earth.pdf[Cavendish (1798), original paper, p. 471: temperature-driven air currents and remote observation]{ref} Mechanical vibrations can excite the same slow <oscillations> used to calibrate the <torsion constant>, while changes in the fiber or source separations alter the inferred <force>. Precision therefore depends on resolving the <equilibrium> <angle>, calibrating the full suspended <moment of inertia> and <torsion constant>, and modeling the actual source geometry. The two-near-pair model omits opposite-source attraction, rod contributions, finite-size effects, <damping>, and the variation of gravitational <torque> as the rod turns. https://sciencedemonstrations.fas.harvard.edu/presentations/cavendish-experiment[Harvard Cavendish demonstration: sensitivity, settling, and separation-dependent measurement]{ref}

= Torsion balance
{parent=Classical mechanics}

A <torsion balance> measures a weak <torque> by suspending a body from a thin fiber and reading its twist <angle>. The fiber supports the body's <gravitational weight> while permitting rotation about the suspension axis. In the range of <linear elasticity>, a twist $\theta$ in <radians>, measured from the untwisted reference orientation, produces a restoring <torque>
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta,\qquad \kappa>0.
$$
The <torsion constant> $\kappa$ sets the sensitivity. At static <equilibrium> with an applied <torque> $\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}$ and no other axial <torque>, $\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}=\kappa\theta$. A perpendicular <force> $F$ at <lever arm> $a$ therefore gives $\theta=aF/\kappa$. The <torsion balance> measures a turning effect: a <force> whose line of action passes through the suspension axis gives no axial <torque>. The <Cavendish torsion balance> is the gravitational application; <Coulomb's law> supplies an electrical one. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 Coulomb Balance manual, pp. 1 and 7: twist measurement of electric force]{ref}

\Image[torsion-balance-readout.png]
{title=Torsion balance readout: fiber twist, force geometry, and returning the body to a fixed angle}

The original schematic separates two ways of reading the same <torque>. In deflection operation the top attachment stays fixed and the body rotates. In null operation the experimenter rotates the top attachment by $\alpha$ while returning the body's laboratory <angle> $\beta$ to its original value. If the initially untwisted configuration has $\alpha=\beta=0$, the fiber twist is $\theta=\beta-\alpha$, not $\beta$ alone. Thus
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa(\beta-\alpha),\qquad \tau_{\mathrm{ext}}=\kappa(\beta-\alpha).
$$
For a positive applied <torque>, holding $\beta=0$ requires $\alpha=-\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. Keeping the body at the same <angle> keeps the <lever arm> and source geometry fixed, making comparison of different applied <forces> easier. Either readout still requires a calibrated <torsion constant> and a stable reference orientation.

An illustrative calculation shows the amplification: with $a=0.050\,\mathrm m$, $F=2.0\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm N$, and $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, the applied <torque> is $1.0\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ and the <equilibrium> twist is $0.010\,\mathrm{rad}\simeq0.573^\circ$. These are stipulated values, not measured data. Reducing the <torsion constant> tenfold increases this twist tenfold, provided the fiber remains in its calibrated <linear elasticity> range. The cost is a slower response, explained by the <oscillation period> in <torsion constant>.

A concrete laboratory experiment is PASCO's Coulomb balance: level its counterbalanced rod, discharge both conducting spheres, and set the unloaded zero. Charge the spheres at maximum separation; switch off the charging supply. Move the second sphere to a known center separation $R$, then turn the suspension dial until the rod returns to its index mark. Record the dial change and $R$, recharge, and repeat at several separations. The manual recommends a draft-free room, clean insulating supports, and recharging before each reading: leakage, especially in humid conditions, otherwise changes the <electric charges> during the sequence. At close separations the conducting spheres redistribute charge, so the <point charge> approximation needs correction. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 manual, pp. 4–8: setup, force-versus-distance procedure, and corrections]{ref}

For constant <electric charges> and a fixed perpendicular <lever arm>, the <Coulomb's law> prediction is $|\theta|\propto R^{-2}$. Plotting the <logarithm> of twist magnitude against the <logarithm> of separation gives $\log|\theta|=\text{constant}-2\log R$, so the predicted slope is $-2$. Absolute <force> measurement additionally needs the <torsion constant>; comparing ratios can eliminate it if it remains stable. Repeating a reference separation between other settings distinguishes a separation effect from a changing signal. A declining reading at the reference separation would invalidate the assumption of unchanged <electric charges>, even if a distance plot looks smooth.

The historical electrical example is Coulomb's first memoir of 1785. His apparatus enclosed a suspended needle with a small ball and a second ball; an adjustable head twisted the suspension. After charging the balls by contact, he reported angular separations $36^\circ$, $18^\circ$, and $8.5^\circ$ with head settings $0^\circ$, $126^\circ$, and $567^\circ$. The total twists were approximately $36^\circ$, $144^\circ$, and $576^\circ$: fourfold restoring <torque> accompanied approximately halved separation. These are his reported observations, not data from our diagrams. https://cnum.cnam.fr/pgi/redir.php?ident=8CA121-1&onglet=c[Coulomb, Premier mémoire (1785), reprinted in Mémoires de Coulomb, pp. 104–109, CNAM digitization]{ref} A precise reconstruction must use center distances and the changing <lever arm>, rather than identify angular separation exactly with distance; Alberto A. Martínez's replication explicitly analyzes that geometry. https://martinezwritings.com/m/Coulomb_files/CoulombExperiment-1.pdf[Martínez, Replication of Coulomb's Torsion Balance Experiment (2006), pp. 522–525]{ref}

Sensitivity also exposes changes of the apparatus itself. Fleischer and colleagues' cryogenic <torsion balance> used a temperature-controlled enclosure to reduce vacuum-vessel top-plate <temperature> fluctuations to approximately $\pm0.01\,\mathrm K$, limiting changes in suspension geometry from <thermal expansion>. They used a mild bake-out near $40^\circ\mathrm C$ to speed initial fiber relaxation and reduce slow zero motion. Their optical readout compared the pendulum mirror with an internal reference mirror to reject shared optical motion. Room-temperature <thermal radiation> entering through the optical opening heated the pendulum; a cold filter reduced that load. The authors could not directly measure the pendulum's <temperature> without disturbing it, and explicitly distinguished the cold shield's <temperature> from the body's. Cooling therefore requires checking the actual noise and thermal environment, rather than assuming that the fiber and body share the shield's <temperature>. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.02890[Fleischer et al., A Cryogenic Torsion Balance Using a Liquid-Cryogen Free, Ultra-Low Vibration Cryostat (2022), §§II B–C and III A]{ref}

A useful control strategy is to record unloaded readings and <temperature> before and after each signal sequence, recalibrate the <torsion constant> under the same conditions, and compare forward and reverse settings. The algebra of <torsion balance zero drift> shows precisely which offsets such comparisons can cancel. A fine angular scale alone does not establish a small <measurement error>.

= Torsion constant
{parent=Torsion balance}
{title2=$\kappa$}

= Torsional spring constant
{synonym}

The <torsion constant>, or <torsional spring constant>, is the positive restoring <torque> per unit twist <angle> of a complete suspension in its <linear elasticity> range:
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}=-\kappa\theta,\qquad \kappa=-\frac{d\tau_{\mathrm{fiber}}}{d\theta}>0.
$$
Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. The minus sign describes restoration toward the untwisted orientation. The derivative specifies a local stiffness if the response is not globally linear; using one constant over a finite range additionally requires a straight calibration curve. Return to the same zero after unloading tests recoverable <elastic deformation>; repeat loading in both directions to check whether the response depends on its previous loading. Staying below permanent deformation is necessary, but does not alone prove proportionality between <torque> and twist.

For an ideal uniform circular wire of <length> $\ell$, radius $r$, and <shear modulus> $G_s$, the <filament torsional stiffness> is $C=G_sJ_p$, with polar <second moment of area> $J_p=\pi r^4/2$. Uniform twist gives $\theta/\ell$ as the twist per unit <length>, so
$$
\kappa=\frac C\ell=\frac{\pi G_s r^4}{2\ell},\qquad U=\frac12\kappa\theta^2.
$$
Here $U$ is stored <elastic energy>. Doubling the wire's radius multiplies the ideal <torsion constant> by $16$; doubling its <length> halves it. This explains why a long thin fiber is useful for weak <torque> measurement. The formula assumes <linear elasticity> with uniform material and circular section; it does not calibrate arbitrary ribbons, clamps, or a compound suspension. Within this ideal model, the <equipartition theorem> predicts an equilibrium thermal mean-square angular displacement $\langle\theta^2\rangle=k_B\mathcal T/\kappa$, where $k_B$ is the <Boltzmann constant> and $\mathcal T$ the uniform absolute <temperature>. A softer suspension magnifies both the signal and its <thermal fluctuations>.

Static calibration supplies known <torques> without assuming the unknown interaction being measured. Apply a known perpendicular <force> $F$ at <lever arm> $a$, record the change from the unloaded twist, and use
$$
\tau_{\mathrm{cal}}=aF,\qquad \kappa=\frac{\tau_{\mathrm{cal}}}{\Delta\theta}.
$$
Several positive and negative settings test proportionality; fit the slope of applied <torque> against twist and retain a possible intercept. A nonzero intercept must not be silently absorbed into the slope. Convert degrees to <radians> before reporting $\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$. PASCO's procedure turns the Coulomb balance on its side and adds small known <masses> to generate a <gravitational weight> at the sphere's <lever arm>, returning the rod to its index mark by turning the dial. Its tabulated coefficient is <force> per degree: multiply by the <lever arm> and $180/\pi$ to obtain our <torsion constant>. https://cdn.pasco.com/product_document/Coulombs-Law-Apparatus-Manual-ES-9070.pdf[Bruce Lee, PASCO ES-9070 manual, pp. 9–10: mass calibration and force-per-degree convention]{ref}

\Image[torsion-balance-calibration.png]
{title=Toy torsion calibrations: static slope and a period corrected for viscous damping}

The figure's ideal static calibration uses $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$: an applied <torque> of $2.0\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm{N\,m}$ gives $0.020\,\mathrm{rad}$. Both signs lie on the same straight line. The plotted points are calculated examples, not experimental observations; they cannot demonstrate a real fiber's linearity.

Dynamic calibration instead measures the <oscillation period> $T_0$ with known total suspended <moment of inertia> $I$. For negligible <damping>, small free <oscillations> about a fixed reference obey <simple harmonic motion>:
$$
I\ddot\theta+\kappa\theta=0,\qquad T_0=2\pi\sqrt{\frac I\kappa},\qquad \kappa=\frac{4\pi^2I}{T_0^2}.
$$
Include the rod, mirrors, and any attachments in $I$ unless their contributions are demonstrably negligible. Keep the applied <torque> absent or independent of <angle>. If an external <torque> has derivative $g_\tau=d\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/d\theta$ at <equilibrium>, linearization gives effective stiffness $\kappa-g_\tau$: timing alone then measures that combination, and stability requires $\kappa-g_\tau>0$. A constant applied <torque> merely shifts <equilibrium>. Non-negligible <damping> changes the measured period; <damped torsional oscillation> gives a correction under a specified damping model. Agreement between static and dynamic calibrations is a useful check on the assumed <moment of inertia>, reference stability, and restoring law, rather than a reason to ignore their assumptions.

= Damped torsional oscillation
{parent=Torsion constant}
{title2=$I\ddot\theta+b\dot\theta+\kappa\theta=0$}

A <damped torsional oscillation> is rotation about a fiber's fixed reference orientation with a restoring <torque> and dissipative <torque>. In the ideal viscous model, <damping> contributes $-b\dot\theta$ with constant $b\geq0$. This is a rotational <damped harmonic oscillator>. For known constant <moment of inertia> $I$ and <torsion constant> $\kappa$, define $\gamma=b/(2I)$. In the oscillatory regime $\gamma^2<\kappa/I$,
$$
\theta(t)=A e^{-\gamma t}\cos(\omega_dt+\varphi),\qquad \omega_d^2=\frac\kappa I-\gamma^2,\qquad T_d=\frac{2\pi}{\omega_d}.
$$
$A$ and $\varphi$ are set by the initial state. Successive positive peaks measured from the same <equilibrium> have magnitudes $p_n$ and $p_{n+1}$; their ratio gives $\delta=\ln(p_n/p_{n+1})=\gamma T_d$. Consequently a measured damped <oscillation period> and decay together recover
$$
\boxed{\kappa=I\left[\left(\frac{2\pi}{T_d}\right)^2+\gamma^2\right]=\frac I{T_d^2}(4\pi^2+\delta^2).}
$$
Using only $4\pi^2I/T_d^2$ underestimates the <torsion constant> in this model. The toy curve in <torsion constant> sets $I=2.0\times10^{-6}\,\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$, $\kappa=1.0\times10^{-8}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, and $\gamma=0.020\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. It gives $T_0\simeq88.86\,\mathrm s$, $T_d\simeq92.64\,\mathrm s$, and $\delta\simeq1.853$. Ignoring the decay recovers $9.2\times10^{-9}\,\mathrm{N\,m/rad}$, an $8\%$ underestimate; including it recovers the stipulated <torsion constant>.

These equations assume a single rotational mode, a stationary zero, <linear elasticity>, and viscous <damping>. Internal fiber losses need not behave as a constant viscous coefficient: Fleischer and colleagues model their precision apparatus using a loss angle. Their response is evidence for specifying a damping model, not for applying the viscous correction universally. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2206.02890[Fleischer et al. (2022), §I, equation (1): thermal torque noise and internal damping represented by a loss angle]{ref}

= Torsion balance zero drift
{parent=Torsion balance}
{title2=$\beta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\beta_0(t)+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$}

<Torsion balance zero drift> is slow motion of the unloaded reference <angle> $\beta_0(t)$ during a measurement. If the <torsion constant> remains fixed, a constant applied <torque> gives $\beta_{\mathrm{eq}}=\beta_0(t)+\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. For two equal and opposite applied <torques> measured at the same reference zero, their <equilibrium> <angles> differ by $2\tau_{\mathrm{ext}}/\kappa$. A stable offset cancels; a changing zero contributes $\beta_0(t_+)-\beta_0(t_-)$ and does not cancel automatically.

A simple symmetric sequence makes the limitation explicit. Let $b_1,b_2,b_3$ be settled <equilibrium> <angles> for applied <torques> $+\tau,-\tau,+\tau$ at equally spaced times $t_0-h,t_0,t_0+h$. If the unloaded zero is linear in <time>, $\beta_0(t)=u+vt$, and $\kappa$ and $\tau$ remain fixed, then
$$
\widehat\tau=\frac\kappa2\left(\frac{b_1+b_3}{2}-b_2\right)=\tau.
$$
The arithmetic average of the outer zero values equals the middle zero, so both the constant offset and linear drift cancel. Curved zero motion, unequal time spacing, changing <torsion constant>, changing signal strength, or unsettled <oscillations> violate these assumptions. This is an algebraic control example, not a claim that every <torsion balance> follows a linear drift law.

= Degree of freedom
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Degrees of freedom
{synonym}

A degree of freedom is an independent coordinate needed to specify a mechanical configuration after all <mechanical constraints> have been imposed.

= Constraint
{disambiguate=mechanics}
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Mechanical constraint
{synonym}

= Constraint
{synonym}

A mechanical constraint restricts the allowed configurations or velocities of a system and reduces its <degrees of freedom>.

= Bead on a parabolic wire
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$T=2\sqrt{2a/g}\int_{-1}^1\sqrt{\frac{1+(h/a)u^2}{1-u^2}}\,du$}

For a smooth stationary wire $z=x^2/(4a)$ and release from rest at height $h$, the <normal reaction> does no work. The energy equation is $[1+x^2/(4a^2)]\dot x^2/2+gx^2/(4a)=gh$. Integrating the travel time between turning points gives the displayed period. Its small-amplitude expansion is $T=2\pi\sqrt{2a/g}[1+h/(4a)+O((h/a)^2)]$. The mass cancels because both gravitational and inertial forces are proportional to it.

= Constraint surface
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$C_a=0$}

The common zero locus of constraint functions $C_a$ in <phase space> is the constraint surface. A physical trajectory lies on this surface, but off-surface identities are often needed when analyzing a <constraint algebra>.

= Holonomic constraint
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{wiki}

A <holonomic constraint> is a relation $f(q,t)=0$ involving generalized coordinates and possibly time, rather than a genuinely nonintegrable velocity relation. Independent smooth constraints of full row rank define a local configuration submanifold. Ideal constraint forces are normal to its tangent virtual displacements and can be represented by <Lagrange multipliers>. A time-dependent <holonomic constraint> need not conserve mechanical <energy>.

= First-class constraint
{parent=Constraint (mechanics)}
{title2=$\varphi_i\approx0$}

= First class
{synonym}

A first-class constraint has <Poisson brackets> with all constraints that vanish on the constraint surface. For a regular constrained <Hamiltonian> system, first-class constraints generate <gauge transformations>; each independent constraint and its gauge direction remove one <canonical pair>.

= Regular first-class phase-space reduction
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\dim\Gamma_{\mathrm{phys}}=2N-2n$}

For $n$ independent regular <first-class constraints> in a $2N$-dimensional phase space, imposing the equations removes $n$ dimensions and quotienting independent gauge orbits removes another $n$. Reducibility, singular strata or vanishing gauge directions can invalidate that elementary count. Closure of the <constraint algebra> is necessary but does not by itself establish regularity.

= Canonical gauge transformation
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\delta F=\{F,\epsilon^i\varphi_i\}$}

A <first-class constraint> generates an infinitesimal <canonical transformation> $\delta F=\{F,\epsilon^i\varphi_i\}$. For the <phase-space action> $I=\int(p\dot q-\lambda^i\varphi_i)dt$, the multiplier transformation $\delta\lambda^k=\dot\epsilon^k-\lambda^i\epsilon^jC_{ij}{}^k$ cancels the bulk variation when $\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=C_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$. The remaining temporal boundary term vanishes for parameters that vanish at the endpoints.

= Constraint algebra
{parent=First-class constraint}
{title2=$\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=f_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$}

The <Poisson brackets> of <first-class constraints> close on the constraints. When the constraints are independent and the coefficients are constant, the <Jacobi identity for the Poisson bracket> makes the coefficients <structure constants> of a <Lie algebra>.

= Structure functions of a constraint algebra
{parent=Constraint algebra}
{title2=$C_{ij}{}^k(q,p)$}

The coefficients $C_{ij}{}^k(q,p)$ in $\{\varphi_i,\varphi_j\}=C_{ij}{}^k\varphi_k$ are structure functions. A finite real span of <first-class constraints> is a <Lie algebra> when it closes with constant coefficients. General structure functions instead describe closure with phase-space-dependent coefficients, while still permitting <canonical gauge transformations>.

= Position
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Position_(geometry)}

Position specifies the location of a particle relative to a coordinate system.

= Plane polar coordinates
{title2=$(r,\theta)$}
{parent=Position}

Plane polar coordinates describe a point by its radial distance $r$ and polar angle $\theta$.

These are the planar <polar coordinates>; the general coordinate-system definition and transformations belong to that canonical concept.

= Acceleration in polar coordinates
{parent=Plane polar coordinates}
{title2=$\mathbf a=(\ddot r-r\dot\theta^2)\widehat{\mathbf r}+(2\dot r\dot\theta+r\ddot\theta)\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$}

Differentiating $\mathbf r=r\widehat{\mathbf r}$ twice and using $\dot{\widehat{\mathbf r}}=\dot\theta\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}$ and $\dot{\widehat{\boldsymbol\theta}}=-\dot\theta\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives the displayed <acceleration>. For a <central force>, its angular component vanishes, so $r^2\dot\theta$ is constant and expresses <conservation of angular momentum>.

= Velocity
{parent=Position}
{title2=$\mathbf v=d\mathbf r/dt$}
{wiki}

<Velocity> is the <vector> giving the rate of change of <position> with <time>, measured in a specified <reference frame>. For a differentiable path $\mathbf r(t)$, its instantaneous <velocity> and its average <velocity> between two <times> are
$$
\mathbf v(t)=\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt},\qquad \mathbf v_{\mathrm{avg}}=\frac{\mathbf r(t_2)-\mathbf r(t_1)}{t_2-t_1},\qquad t_2>t_1.
$$
The <displacement> in the numerator retains direction. In <Cartesian coordinates>, each <vector component> is a <derivative>, for example $v_x=dx/dt$; its sign tells whether the corresponding coordinate is increasing or decreasing. The <SI> unit of <velocity> is $\mathrm{m/s}$. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-2-instantaneous-velocity-and-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics Volume 1, section 3.2: instantaneous velocity and speed]{ref}

For a concrete reversal, let $x(t)=(1\,\mathrm m)+(1\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2-(2\,\mathrm{m/s})t$ for $0\le t\le2\,\mathrm s$. Its signed <velocity> is $v_x(t)=(2\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$: at $t=0$ it is $-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, at $t=1\,\mathrm s$ it is zero, and at $t=2\,\mathrm s$ it is $+2\,\mathrm{m/s}$. The opposite endpoint <velocities> describe opposite directions, although the endpoint <speeds> agree. The <position> moves from $1\,\mathrm m$ to $0$ and back to $1\,\mathrm m$.

Recovering <displacement> from a measured <velocity> is an <integration> problem: $\mathbf r(t_2)-\mathbf r(t_1)=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\mathbf v(t)\,dt$, with the <integral> taken component by component. In the reversal example, $\int_0^{2\,\mathrm s}v_x(t)\,dt=0$: the negative and positive contributions cancel. This distinction is useful in navigation, where the final <position> depends on direction as well as <speed>.

= Normal velocity
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$v_n=v\cdot n$}

The <normal velocity> is the scalar component of a <velocity> in a specified unit-normal direction. Its sign depends on the orientation of the normal. At a material interface, no penetration equates the fluid and interface <normal velocities>; it does not require their tangential components to agree. <Surface acoustic impedance> uses a fixed inward normal-velocity convention to determine the sign of energy absorbed by a boundary.

= Speed
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$v=|\mathbf v|$}
{wiki}

<Speed> is the nonnegative magnitude of <velocity>, a <scalar> rather than a <vector>. In orthonormal <Cartesian coordinates>,
$$
v(t)=|\mathbf v(t)|=\sqrt{v_x(t)^2+v_y(t)^2+v_z(t)^2}.
$$
Its <SI> unit is $\mathrm{m/s}$. In the reversal example under <velocity>, the endpoint <velocities> $-2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $+2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ both have <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$, but the <speed> falls to zero between them. A <speed> reading alone therefore cannot tell which way the body is moving.

For a smooth path, the total distance traveled is its <arc length>, $L=\int_{t_1}^{t_2}v(t)\,dt$. Unlike the <integral> of a signed <velocity> component, this <integral> cannot cancel outward and return motion. The reversal example travels $2\,\mathrm m$ in $2\,\mathrm s$, giving an average <speed> of $1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, while its average <velocity> is zero. This makes <speed> useful for estimating travel duration or fuel use, while <velocity> also tracks progress toward a destination. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/3-2-instantaneous-velocity-and-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 3.2: distance traveled versus displacement]{ref}

= Acceleration
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$\mathbf a=d\mathbf v/dt$}
{wiki}

<Acceleration> is the <time> <derivative> of <velocity>, equivalently the second <derivative> of <position>:
$$
\mathbf a(t)=\frac{d\mathbf v}{dt}=\frac{d^2\mathbf r}{dt^2}.
$$
It is a <vector> with <SI> unit $\mathrm{m/s^2}$. A change in direction produces <acceleration> even if <speed> stays constant. For example, a car rounding a bend at steady <speed> needs an inward resultant <force> by <Newton's second law>. Thus <acceleration> does not mean only speeding up. When $v>0$, the <dot product> gives $dv/dt=\mathbf v\cdot\mathbf a/v$: a perpendicular <acceleration> changes direction without changing <speed> at that instant.

Take counterclockwise <circular motion> about the origin with <radius> $R=2\,\mathrm m$ and constant <angular speed> $\omega=0.5\,\mathrm{rad/s}$. The <position>, <velocity> and <acceleration> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r(t)&=(R\cos(\omega t),R\sin(\omega t)),\\
\mathbf v(t)&=(-R\omega\sin(\omega t),R\omega\cos(\omega t)),\\
\mathbf a(t)&=(-R\omega^2\cos(\omega t),-R\omega^2\sin(\omega t))=-\omega^2\mathbf r(t).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <speed> is $R\omega=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ throughout, while the inward <centripetal acceleration> has magnitude $R\omega^2=v^2/R=0.5\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. At the four cardinal points, reached at $t=0,\pi,2\pi,3\pi\,\mathrm s$, the <velocities> in $\mathrm{m/s}$ are $(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1),(1,0)$; the <accelerations> in $\mathrm{m/s^2}$ are $(-0.5,0),(0,-0.5),(0.5,0),(0,0.5)$. The <vectors> turn together, remaining perpendicular. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/4-4-uniform-and-nonuniform-circular-motion[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 4.4: uniform circular motion]{ref}

\Image[constant-speed-circular-acceleration.png]
{title=Constant speed with changing velocity and inward acceleration at four points of a circle}

The proof outline is just two applications of the <chain rule> to $\mathbf r(t)$; $\sin^2(\omega t)+\cos^2(\omega t)=1$ then fixes both magnitudes. The usefulness is geometric: turning requires a <force> even when a <speed> reading remains steady, and doubling <speed> at fixed <radius> quadruples the required <centripetal acceleration>. The same mechanism appears in a <circular orbit>.

Historically, Newton's \i[Principia], Book I, Section II, Proposition IV, relates the inward <forces> of uniform <circular motion> to the squared arcs traversed in equal <times>, divided by their <radii>. In modern notation, its dependence on <speed> and <radius> is $v^2/R$; Newton presents a geometric argument rather than the coordinate <derivatives> above. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Book_1/Section_2[Newton, Principia, Book I, Proposition IV; Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

= Jerk
{parent=Acceleration}
{disambiguate=kinematics}
{title2=$\mathbf j=d\mathbf a/dt=d^3\mathbf r/dt^3$}
{wiki=Jerk_(physics)}

= Kinematic jerk
{synonym}

The kinematic jerk is the time derivative of <acceleration>. For a <Kepler orbit> with relative position $\mathbf r$, relative velocity $\mathbf v$, $r=|\mathbf r|$, $\dot r=\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf v/r$ and total <mass> $M$, differentiation of <Newton's law of universal gravitation> gives $\mathbf j=-GM(\mathbf v-3\dot r\,\mathbf r/r)/r^3$.

= Constant speed
{parent=Velocity}

A path has <constant speed> when the magnitude of its <velocity> does not vary with <time>. Its <acceleration> may still be nonzero: uniform <circular motion> has turning <velocity> and inward <centripetal acceleration>. Constant <velocity> is stronger, requiring unchanged direction as well as unchanged <speed>.

= Terminal velocity
{parent=Velocity}
{title2=$\mathbf v_T$}
{wiki}

<Terminal velocity> is a constant limiting <velocity> approached when a <velocity>-dependent resistance balances the other applied <forces>. A falling body may keep moving while its net <force> and <acceleration> tend to zero. For downward motion in a stationary medium, its limiting <speed> is set by balancing <gravitational weight> against <drag force>, including <buoyancy> if appreciable. The balance, rather than a universal numerical value, is the defining idea. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-4-drag-force-and-terminal-speed[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, section 6.4: drag and terminal speed]{ref}

The <terminal velocity under linear drag> gives a simple example with an explicit approach to the limit. Real falling objects need an appropriate resistance model: <linear drag> is useful for slow motion of a small sphere in <Stokes flow>, whereas many faster falling objects require <quadratic drag>. Changing <mass> also changes the question: the existing <accreting raindrop with quadratic drag> has a <speed-radius attractor of an accreting raindrop>, rather than the fixed-<mass> balance used below.

= Terminal velocity under linear drag
{parent=Terminal velocity}
{title2=$v_T=Mg/b,\quad\tau=M/b$}

Consider a <point mass> of fixed <mass> $M>0$ released from rest in a stationary medium. Choose downward as positive, assume constant <gravitational acceleration> $g>0$, neglect <buoyancy>, and impose <linear drag> $F_d=-bv$ with constant <linear friction coefficient> $b>0$. Here $v$ is the signed downward <velocity> component; it equals <speed> during this descent. <Newton's second law> becomes
$$
M\frac{dv}{dt}=Mg-bv,\qquad v(0)=0,\qquad v_T=\frac{Mg}{b},\quad\tau=\frac{M}{b}.
$$
The <terminal velocity> balances the two <forces>, and the <time constant> $\tau$ controls how quickly that balance is approached:
$$
v(t)=v_T(1-e^{-t/\tau}),\qquad a(t)=\frac{dv}{dt}=ge^{-t/\tau},\qquad t\ge0.
$$
For the illustrative parameters $M=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, $b=2\,\mathrm{kg/s}$ and $g=9.8\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$, the <time constant> is $0.5\,\mathrm s$ and the <terminal velocity> is $4.9\,\mathrm{m/s}$ downward. At $t=1\,\mathrm s=2\tau$, the downward <velocity> is $4.9(1-e^{-2})\,\mathrm{m/s}\simeq4.23686\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and the downward <acceleration> is $9.8e^{-2}\,\mathrm{m/s^2}\simeq1.32629\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$. This is a toy <linear drag> model, not a claim that every $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ falling object has this resistance law.

\Image[linear-drag-terminal-velocity.png]
{title=Exponential approach to terminal velocity and decay of acceleration in an illustrative linear drag model}

For a proof outline, subtract the equilibrium value: $u=v-v_T$ obeys $du/dt=-u/\tau$. The <exponential decay> solution and the initial condition give $u=-v_Te^{-t/\tau}$; one <derivative> gives the <acceleration>. Every <time constant> multiplies the remaining gap $v_T-v$ by $e^{-1}$. The exact limit is reached only as $t\to\infty$. This makes the model useful for estimating a settling duration without mistaking vanishing <acceleration> for vanishing <velocity>.

Newton already analyzed vertical motion under uniform <gravity> and resistance proportional to <velocity> in Book II, Section I, Proposition III of the \i[Principia]. His geometric construction includes a greatest downward <velocity>, illustrating that a limiting rate can emerge from balancing a driving <force> and resistance. https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Mathematical_Principles_of_Natural_Philosophy_(1729)/Book_2/Section_1[Newton, Principia, Book II, Proposition III; Motte's 1729 translation]{ref}

\Include[energy]{parent=Classical mechanics}
\Include[momentum]{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Period of an oscillation
{title2=$T$}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Oscillation period
{synonym}

The period is the time required for a periodic motion to complete one full cycle.

\Include[force]{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Equation of motion in a rotating frame
{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Equations of motion in a rotating frame
{synonym}

For a frame rotating with constant angular velocity $\boldsymbol\omega$,
$$
\mathbf a_{\rm inertial}
=\ddot{\mathbf x}+2\boldsymbol\omega\times\dot{\mathbf x}
+\boldsymbol\omega\times(\boldsymbol\omega\times\mathbf x).
$$
The extra terms produce the Coriolis and centrifugal forces in the rotating-frame equation.

= Astroid motion of a harmonic oscillator in a rotating frame
{parent=Equation of motion in a rotating frame}
{title2=$(x',y')=(\cos^3\omega t,\sin^3\omega t)$}

For an isotropic <harmonic oscillator> with inertial frequency $2\omega$, viewed in a frame rotating at $\omega$, initial rotating coordinates $(1,0)$ and zero relative <velocity> produce the displayed <astroid> trajectory. The complex coordinate satisfies $\ddot\zeta+2i\omega\dot\zeta+3\omega^2\zeta=0$, with solution $\zeta=\tfrac34e^{i\omega t}+\tfrac14e^{-3i\omega t}$. The relative <speed> is $(3|\omega|/2)|\sin2\omega t|$, attaining its first maximum at $\pi/(4|\omega|)$ for nonzero $\omega$.

= Circular motion
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Uniform circular motion of radius $a$ and angular frequency $\omega$ has centripetal acceleration of magnitude $a\omega^2$ toward the centre.

= Centripetal acceleration
{parent=Circular motion}
{title2=$\mathbf a=-\omega^2\mathbf r$}

For uniform <circular motion> of radius $R$ and <angular speed> $\omega$, the <acceleration> is directed toward the centre and has magnitude $\omega^2R=v^2/R$. Writing $\mathbf r$ for the <position> relative to the centre gives $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\omega^2\mathbf r$. The required resultant <force> is $m\ddot{\mathbf r}$; it may be supplied by a <normal force>, gravity, or another physical interaction.

= Circular orbit
{parent=Circular motion}

A circular orbit maintains constant distance from its centre of attraction. Its <velocity> changes direction even when its <speed> stays constant. The inward <centripetal acceleration> has magnitude $v^2/r$, so the radial <force> must satisfy $F_r=-mv^2/r$. For a <Newtonian gravitational field> with <standard gravitational parameter> $\mu$, this gives $v=\sqrt{\mu/r}$.

= Circular orbit as continuous free fall
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$\mathbf r(t)=a(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t),\quad\omega^2=\mu/a^3$}

For a fixed spherical gravitating source, place a <test particle> at $(a,0)$ with initial <velocity> $(0,\sqrt{\mu/a})$, where $a$ is outside the source. Its exact <circular orbit> is
$$
\mathbf r(t)=a(\cos\omega t,\sin\omega t),\qquad \omega=\sqrt{\frac{\mu}{a^3}}.
$$
Differentiating twice gives $\ddot{\mathbf r}=-\omega^2\mathbf r=-\mu\mathbf r/a^3$, precisely the <Newtonian gravitational field> at each point of that circle. There is no outward physical <force> to cancel gravity in this <inertial frame>. Gravity supplies the inward <centripetal acceleration> that continually changes the <velocity>'s direction.

\Image[circular-orbit-continuous-free-fall.png]
{title=A circular free-fall orbit, inward acceleration, and the straight path the same initial velocity would produce without gravity}

Over a short interval, the displacement from the straight tangent path begins inward with magnitude $\tfrac12(\mu/a^2)t^2$. The <curvature> of the orbit matters at the same order: the particle moves sideways while continually falling away from its instantaneous tangent. The full circular solution remains at radius $a$ rather than striking the source.

Its <speed> is $\sqrt{\mu/a}$, its <oscillation period> is $2\pi\sqrt{a^3/\mu}$, and its <specific orbital energy> is $-\mu/(2a)$. Removing gravity while keeping the initial <velocity> would give the straight path $(a,a\omega t)$, rather than a stationary particle. This contrast explains why orbital <weightlessness> is compatible with a substantial gravitational <force>: a freely coasting spacecraft and its contents fall together. https://www.nasa.gov/centers-and-facilities/glenn/what-is-microgravity/[NASA Glenn: orbit as continuous free fall and the origin of weightlessness]{ref}

= Circular-orbit energy gradient
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$d\varepsilon=\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf h$}

In a spherically symmetric <gravitational potential> $\Phi(r)$, a <circular orbit> has $r\Omega^2=\Phi'(r)$, <specific orbital energy> $\varepsilon=\Phi+r^2\Omega^2/2$ and <specific angular momentum> $\mathbf h=r^2\Omega\mathbf n$, with $|\mathbf n|=1$. Differentiating gives $\varepsilon'=2r\Omega^2+r^2\Omega\Omega'=\Omega(r^2\Omega)'$. Changes in orbital orientation have $\mathbf n\cdot d\mathbf n=0$, so $\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf h=\Omega\,d(r^2\Omega)=d\varepsilon$. For a particle of variable <mass> $M$ and total <angular momentum> $\mathbf H=M\mathbf h$, this implies $d(M\varepsilon)=(\varepsilon-\Omega h)dM+\boldsymbol\Omega\cdot d\mathbf H$. Moreover $d(\varepsilon-\Omega h)/dr=-h\Omega'$, which is positive when the orbital <angular velocity> decreases outward.

= Circular orbits in an exponential central potential
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$l^2=r^3e^{-r}$}

For unit mass with specific <angular momentum> $l$ in $V(r)=-e^{-r}$, the <effective potential> is $-e^{-r}+l^2/(2r^2)$. A positive-radius <circular orbit> satisfies the displayed equation. Since $r^3e^{-r}$ has its unique maximum $27/e^3$ at $r=3$, exactly two radii occur for $0<l^2<27/e^3$. Their radial stability follows from $V_{\rm eff}''=e^{-r}(3/r-1)$: the inner orbit is stable at fixed <angular momentum>, the outer unstable. The case $l=0$ has no finite-radius <circular orbit> despite satisfying the upper bound.

= Circular-orbit stability for a power-law central force
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$\Omega_r^2=(n+3)ka^{n-1}$}

For the radial <acceleration> $-kr^n$ with $k>0$, a <circular orbit> of radius $a$ has squared <specific angular momentum> $h^2=ka^{n+3}$. At fixed $h$, its radial <small oscillations> have squared frequency $(n+3)ka^{n-1}$, so the orbit is stable for $n>-3$. This is a special case of the <effective potential stability criterion>.

= Circular orbit in a quadratic central potential
{parent=Circular orbit}
{title2=$r_0^4=h^2/(2km^2)$}

For $V(r)=kmr^2$, fixed nonzero <angular momentum> $h$ gives the <effective potential> $V_{\rm eff}=h^2/(2mr^2)+kmr^2$. A positive-radius circular orbit exists when $k>0$, at the displayed radius, and $V_{\rm eff}''(r_0)=8km>0$ proves radial stability by the <effective potential stability criterion>. Its angular speed has magnitude $\sqrt{2k}$ and its small radial oscillation frequency is $\sqrt{8k}$. If $k\le0$, no such orbit with nonzero angular momentum exists; for $h=0,k>0$, the sole minimum is at the origin, not a positive-radius circular orbit.

= Inverse-square force
{parent=Classical mechanics}

An inverse-square central force has magnitude proportional to the reciprocal square of the separation.

= Repulsive inverse-square force
{parent=Inverse-square force}

The repulsive inverse-square force $\mathbf F=k\mathbf r/r^3$ has <potential energy> $V(r)=k/r$ and scatters positive-energy trajectories along hyperbolic conics.

= Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector
{title2=$\mathbf A$}
{c}
{parent=Inverse-square force}
{wiki=Laplace–Runge–Lenz_vector}

= Runge-Lenz vector
{c}
{synonym}

For the signed <inverse-square force> $\boldsymbol F=-\kappa\boldsymbol r/r^3$ on a particle of <mass> $m$, with Hamiltonian $H=p^2/(2m)-\kappa/r$, the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> $\boldsymbol A=\boldsymbol p\times\boldsymbol L-m\kappa\widehat{\boldsymbol r}$ is conserved. Attraction has $\kappa>0$ and repulsion has $\kappa<0$. It obeys $\boldsymbol A\cdot\boldsymbol L=0$ and $A^2=m^2\kappa^2+2mHL^2$. For unit <mass> in a <Kepler orbit>, $\kappa=GM$.

= Kepler dynamical symmetry algebra
{c}
{parent=Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector}
{title2=$\mathfrak{so}(4),\quad\mathfrak e(3),\quad\mathfrak{so}(3,1)$}

For $H=p^2/2-\kappa/r$, the <angular momentum> and <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> obey $\{K_i,K_j\}=-2H\epsilon_{ijk}L_k$. On negative or positive energy regions use $K/\sqrt{|2H|}$ to obtain the compact or Lorentz <Lie algebra>, respectively. At zero energy the algebra on the characteristic orbit quotient is the Euclidean motion algebra. Global <group actions> require complete generators or collision regularization; simply replacing $H$ by a constant inside a Hamiltonian-vector-field derivative is not valid on the unreduced energy hypersurface.

= Polar equation of a repulsive inverse-square orbit
{parent=Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector}

Taking the dot product of the <Laplace-Runge-Lenz vector> with $\widehat{\mathbf r}$ gives $A\cos\theta=L^2/r+mk$. Thus
$$
r=\frac{\lambda}{e\cos\theta-1},
\qquad
\lambda=\frac{L^2}{mk},
\qquad
e=\frac{A}{mk}.
$$

= Displacement
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki=Displacement_(geometry)}

Displacement is the change in position between two points.

= Time in physics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Time
{synonym}

Time parametrizes physical change and orders events.

= Mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Mass measures inertia and acts as the source of Newtonian gravity.

= Point mass
{parent=Mass}
{title2=$\rho=m\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf z)$}

A point mass idealizes a localized body's mass density as $\rho(\mathbf x,t)=m\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf z(t))$, using the <Dirac delta function>. Its <second mass moment tensor> is $I_{ij}=mz_i z_j$. The approximation ignores its internal size and structure.

= Rolling acceleration with rotational inertia
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For a round body with $I=\gamma Ma^2$ rolling without slipping down an incline, static friction gives
$$
a_{\rm cm}=\frac{g\sin\alpha}{1+\gamma}.
$$

= Angular momentum about the centre of mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}

The derivative of angular momentum about the moving centre of mass equals the total external torque about that centre, because total relative momentum vanishes and internal central torques cancel.

= Angular momentum decomposition about the centre of mass
{parent=Angular momentum about the centre of mass}
{title2=$\boldsymbol L=M\boldsymbol X\times\boldsymbol V+\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol r_i'\times\boldsymbol v_i'$}

Write each position and velocity as its <centre of mass> value plus a relative value. Expanding the total <angular momentum> gives the displayed split into orbital angular momentum of the centre and internal angular momentum about it. The cross terms vanish because $\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol r_i'=\sum_i m_i\boldsymbol v_i'=0$.

= Legendre transform in mechanics
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For a regular Lagrangian, $p=\partial L/\partial\dot q$ and $H=p\dot q-L$. Regularity means the velocity Hessian is invertible.

= Regular Lagrangian
{parent=Legendre transform in mechanics}
{title2=$\det(L_{v^iv^j})\ne0$}

A <Lagrangian> is regular when its velocity <Hessian matrix> is invertible. The <inverse function theorem> then makes the fiber derivative $p_i=L_{v^i}$ locally invertible, allowing a local <Legendre transform in mechanics> to a <Hamiltonian>. If that fiber derivative is globally invertible, the <Lagrangian> is called a <hyperregular Lagrangian>. Regularity alone does not assert global invertibility, and singular <Lagrangians> may give constrained rather than ordinary unconstrained <Hamilton's equations>.

= Hyperregular Lagrangian
{parent=Regular Lagrangian}
{title2=$\mathbb FL:TQ\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}T^*Q$}

A <Lagrangian> is hyperregular when its fiber derivative, sending a velocity to its conjugate momentum, is a global <diffeomorphism> from the <tangent bundle> to the <cotangent bundle>. This gives a globally defined <Legendre transform in mechanics> and a global equivalence between the <Euler-Lagrange equations> and <Hamilton's equations>. With time dependence this requirement is imposed at each time, with a smooth inverse depending on time.

= Singular Legendre transform of a degree-one velocity Lagrangian
{parent=Legendre transform in mechanics}

For $L=mc|\dot x|-V(x)$,
$$
p=mc\frac{\dot x}{|\dot x|},
\qquad |p|=mc,
\qquad H=V(x).
$$
The momentum loses the speed, so the velocity cannot be recovered from $(x,p)$ and the ordinary inverse Legendre transform does not exist.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle
{c}
{parent=Classical mechanics}

For $L=m\dot r^2/2-e\phi+e\dot r\cdot A$, $p=m\dot r+eA$ and $H=|p-eA|^2/(2m)+e\phi$.

= Virial theorem
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0$}

The virial theorem relates the <average value of a function> along a trajectory to the <forces> that bind it. For an <isolated system> of particles interacting through <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>, it gives $2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0$: the average <kinetic energy> is half the magnitude of the average negative <potential energy>. An eccentric <Kepler orbit> makes the distinction vivid: <kinetic energy> and <potential energy> vary throughout the <orbit>, although their sum is constant. The relation concerns their <time> averages, rather than their values at every instant. It underlies the <virial mass estimate> and the <negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system>.

Use constant positive particle <masses> $m_i$, <positions> $\mathbf r_i$, mechanical <momenta> $\mathbf p_i=m_i\dot{\mathbf r}_i$, and total <kinetic energy> $K=\sum_i\mathbf p_i^2/(2m_i)$. The auxiliary quantity $G(t)$ is a sum of <dot products>, not the <gravitational constant>, which we denote here by $G_{\!N}$:
$$
G(t)=\sum_i\mathbf p_i(t)\cdot\mathbf r_i(t),\qquad
\langle A\rangle_T=\frac1T\int_0^T A(t)\,dt,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle_T+\left\langle\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i\right\rangle_T
=\frac{G(T)-G(0)}{T}.
$$
This finite-$T$ identity is exact wherever <Newton's second law> holds along a regular trajectory; $\mathbf F_i$ must include every <force>. The endpoint term is what a finite observation window leaves behind. For the infinite-<time> theorem, suppose the averages on the left exist and $(G(T)-G(0))/T\to0$. A sufficient condition is uniformly bounded <positions> and <velocities> for finitely many particles. More generally $G(T)=o(T)$ suffices, even without those individual bounds. Bounded <positions> alone do not supply a bound on <momenta>, especially near a singular <potential energy>; nor does a small endpoint term prove <thermodynamic equilibrium>. Periodic motion with $G(T)=G(0)$ gives the identity without an endpoint term at that finite $T$. For an <isolated system>, use an inertial <centre of mass> frame so translational drift does not contribute bulk <kinetic energy>.<Cosmology by David Tong/1 The Expanding Universe>[Cosmology by David Tong, §1.4.3, “The Virial Theorem and Galaxy Clusters”: bounded positions and velocities, existence of averages, equation (1.74) and endpoint limit]{ref}

For a differentiable <potential energy> that is a <homogeneous function> of degree $q$, choose its additive constant so that the scaling law really holds. With <conservative forces>, the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> gives
$$
U(\lambda\mathbf r_1,\ldots,\lambda\mathbf r_N)=\lambda^qU(\mathbf r_1,\ldots,\mathbf r_N),\qquad
\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i=-qU,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle_T-q\langle U\rangle_T=\frac{G(T)-G(0)}T.
$$
Thus a <harmonic oscillator> has $q=2$ and $\langle K\rangle=\langle U\rangle$. An <isolated system> with <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> has
$$
U=-G_{\!N}\sum_{i<j}\frac{m_im_j}{|\mathbf r_i-\mathbf r_j|},\qquad q=-1,\qquad
2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=0,\qquad
E=\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=-\langle K\rangle.
$$
Each interacting pair occurs once, and $U=0$ at infinite separation. Here the total <energy> $E=K+U$ is conserved. An external field or a mixture of different scaling powers requires its own <force> term; one cannot assign the whole <potential energy> a single degree $q$. The <virial theorem for a homogeneous pair potential> states the corresponding general <energy> relation.

= Virial theorem on a Kepler ellipse
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\langle K\rangle=\kappa/(2a),\quad\langle U\rangle=-\kappa/a$}

Consider the exact <two-body problem> with <masses> $m_1,m_2$. In the inertial <centre of mass> frame, its internal <kinetic energy> is $K=m|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2$, where $\mathbf r$ is their relative <position>, $m=m_1m_2/(m_1+m_2)$ is the <reduced mass>, and $U=-\kappa/r$ with $\kappa=G_{\!N}m_1m_2$. Take a noncollision <elliptic Kepler orbit> with <semi-major axis> $a$ and <orbital eccentricity> $0<e<1$. Write $\eta$ for <eccentric anomaly>, to avoid confusing it with total <energy> $E$. With <mean motion> $n=\sqrt{\kappa/(ma^3)}$ and <orbital period> $P=2\pi/n$, <Kepler's equation> and the <orbit> geometry give
$$
nt=\eta-e\sin\eta,\qquad r=a(1-e\cos\eta),\qquad
K=\frac{\kappa}{r}-\frac{\kappa}{2a},\qquad U=-\frac{\kappa}{r},\qquad E=-\frac{\kappa}{2a}.
$$
The <time> origin is <periapsis>. Since $dt=(1-e\cos\eta)\,d\eta/n$, the <time> average of $1/r$ over one <orbital period> is exactly $1/a$. Therefore $\langle K\rangle_P=\kappa/(2a)$ and $\langle U\rangle_P=-\kappa/a$, independently of <orbital eccentricity>. Equal steps in <eccentric anomaly> are not equal <time> steps; unweighted averaging of a drawing of the <ellipse> gives the wrong answer.

\Image[virial-kepler-ellipse-energy-time-averages.png]
{id=virial-kepler-ellipse-energy-time-averages}
{title=Instantaneous <kinetic energy> and <potential energy>, their <orbital period> averages, and the finite-window virial residual on an eccentric <Kepler orbit>}

The original dimensionless example in both diagrams has $m=a=\kappa=1$ and $e=0.6$. At <periapsis>, $r=0.4$, $K=2$, $U=-2.5$; at <apoapsis>, $r=1.6$, $K=0.125$, $U=-0.625$. Both have $E=-0.5$, but $2K+U$ is respectively $1.5$ and $-0.375$. Only the <orbital period> averages are $\langle K\rangle_P=0.5$ and $\langle U\rangle_P=-1$. The instantaneous zero of $2K+U$ occurs at $r=a$, not at every point of the <orbit>.

\Image[virial-kepler-ellipse-orbit-configuration.png]
{id=virial-kepler-ellipse-orbit-configuration}
{title=Relative <orbit> of the same <two-body problem>, with equal-<time> markers, the attracting focus, and <periapsis> and <apoapsis> <energies>}

The endpoint term can also be evaluated exactly: $G=m\mathbf r\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}=mna^2e\sin\eta$. Starting at <periapsis>, a window ending at <eccentric anomaly> $\eta$ has
$$
2\langle K\rangle_T+\langle U\rangle_T
=\frac{\kappa}{a}\frac{e\sin\eta}{\eta-e\sin\eta}.
$$
At $T=P/4$, <Kepler's equation> gives $\eta\simeq2.09133$ and a positive residual $\simeq0.3314\kappa/a$. At $T=P/2$ or $P$, $G$ returns to zero and the residual vanishes exactly. The lower panel shows the running residual and its endpoint expression coinciding; the shaded quarter-<orbital period> window explains why a short observation need not satisfy the infinite-<time> theorem.

= Virial mass estimate
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$M\simeq3R_g\sigma_{\rm los}^2/G_{\!N}$}

Motion can weigh a system whose <mass> is otherwise inaccessible. For a self-gravitating <isolated system> in <virial equilibrium>, let $K$ and $U$ denote equilibrium averages and use its <gravitational radius> $R_g=-G_{\!N}M^2/U$ and a mass-weighted mean squared <speed> relative to its <centre of mass>. Then $K=M\langle v^2\rangle/2$ gives $M=R_g\langle v^2\rangle/G_{\!N}$. If random <velocities> are <isotropic> and mean streaming has been removed, $\langle v^2\rangle=3\sigma_{\rm los}^2$, where $\sigma_{\rm los}$ is the one-dimensional line-of-sight <velocity dispersion>. Observations sample many objects at one epoch; replacing a <time> average by that sample requires a representative, approximately stationary population, beyond the trajectory identity itself.<Cosmology by David Tong/1 The Expanding Universe>[Cosmology by David Tong, §1.4.3, “The Virial Theorem and Galaxy Clusters”: self-averaging assumption and mass estimate (1.75)]{ref}

A practical measurement route uses an <optical telescope> to locate members and a <spectrograph> to measure <Doppler shifts> of identified <spectral lines>. Their shifts relative to the system's mean give radial <velocities>; angular separations plus a distance estimate constrain its size. Subtracting instrumental broadening and measurement variance, testing membership, and looking for coherent rotation or subgroups help control the inference. The projected luminous size need not equal $R_g$, and luminous tracers need not have the same distribution as all gravitating <mass>. Unknown <velocity-anisotropy parameters>, interlopers, mergers, external <tidal forces> and appreciable boundary stresses can bias a naive estimate. A large <velocity dispersion> alone does not establish <virial equilibrium>.

= Boundary-pressure correction to the virial theorem
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle+\langle U\rangle=3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle$}

An external <pressure> changes the balance even if the system remains confined. For a material boundary acted on by spatially uniform inward <pressure> $P_s$, the surface <force> contributes $-P_s\int_{\partial\mathcal V}\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf n\,dS=-3P_s\mathcal V$, by the <divergence theorem>. For <Newtonian gravitational potential energy>, the finite-window identity becomes
$$
\frac{G(T)-G(0)}T=2\langle K\rangle_T+\langle U\rangle_T-3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle_T.
$$
Here $\mathcal V$ is the enclosed <volume>, $\mathbf n$ the outward <unit normal>, and $K$ includes the microscopic translational <kinetic energy> of the confined particles. Do not also add the same random motions as a separate internal <pressure> term. A nonuniform external stress needs its surface integral; a fixed observational boundary crossed by matter also needs flux terms.<Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell>[Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell, p. 2, §1: external-pressure virial relation]{ref}

With negligible surface <pressure>, the <negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system> follows from $E=-\langle K\rangle$: after a slow loss of total <energy> and readjustment to <virial equilibrium>, the average <kinetic energy> rises. For fixed $N$ classical particles with three translational <degrees of freedom>, define kinetic <temperature> $\Theta$ by $\langle K\rangle=3Nk_B\Theta/2$. Along this isolated equilibrium sequence, $dE/d\Theta=-3Nk_B/2$. The system contracts and its binding <potential energy> becomes more negative, supplying both the outgoing <energy> and the increased <kinetic energy>. This is an endpoint comparison, not a claim that every transient contraction obeys the average relation. A <collisionless stellar system> need not have a common thermodynamic <temperature>; a pressure-confined system instead has $E=-\langle K\rangle+3\langle P_s\mathcal V\rangle$. A stable <canonical ensemble> in contact with a large <thermal reservoir> does not support this negative <heat capacity> branch.<Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell>[Negative Specific Heat in Astronomy, Physics and Chemistry by D. Lynden-Bell, pp. 2-3, §1: isolated-system energy, negative heat capacity and instability in a large heat bath]{ref}

= Virial theorem derivation and history
{parent=Virial theorem}

The proof is a single <time derivative>. Apply the <product rule> to each <dot product> and use <Newton's second law>:
$$
\dot G=\sum_i\dot{\mathbf p}_i\cdot\mathbf r_i+\sum_i\mathbf p_i\cdot\dot{\mathbf r}_i
=\sum_i\mathbf F_i\cdot\mathbf r_i+2K.
$$
Integrating from $0$ to $T$ gives the endpoint identity; the stated sublinear condition removes the endpoint term only in the infinite-<time> limit. Equivalently, for the scalar inertia $I=\tfrac12\sum_i m_i|\mathbf r_i|^2$ used in the <pairwise moment-of-inertia identity>, $\dot I=G$ and $\ddot I=2K+\sum_i\mathbf r_i\cdot\mathbf F_i$. Specifying this factor of one half avoids confusion with another common inertia convention.

In <On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius> (1870, pp. 123-124), the author defines stationary motion through bounded <positions> and <velocities>, includes planetary <orbits> and elastic vibrations, and names the average negative half of the force-position sum the “virial”. That historical convention differs from the auxiliary $G(t)$ defined above. His proof on pp. 126-127 keeps an endpoint term, then explains why it vanishes over a complete period or becomes small in a long bounded irregular motion. The same paper separates internal and external <forces> and includes uniform boundary <pressure> on pp. 125-126: confinement was already part of the formulation, rather than a reason to omit external stresses.<On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius>[On a Mechanical Theorem applicable to Heat by R. Clausius (1870), pp. 123-127; p. 122 footnote dates the lecture to 13 June 1870]{ref}

= Virial mean escape speed
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=4\langle v^2\rangle$}

For an isolated equilibrium of equal-mass points, the <virial theorem> gives $\langle v^2\rangle=-W/M$. A particle's instantaneous <escape velocity> satisfies $v_{\rm esc}^2=-2\Phi$ with zero potential at infinity. Since $W=\tfrac12\sum_i m\Phi_i$, averaging gives $\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=-4W/M$. These are three-dimensional mean-square speeds, not one-component <velocity dispersions>.

= Virial theorem for a homogeneous pair potential
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2\langle K\rangle=q\langle W\rangle$}

For a differentiable total pair <potential energy> homogeneous of degree $q$ in all particle positions, the <Euler theorem for homogeneous functions> gives $\sum_i\boldsymbol r_i\cdot\nabla_iW=qW$. Since $\boldsymbol F_i=-\nabla_iW$, the force virial is $-qW$. The bounded-motion time-averaged <virial theorem> consequently gives the displayed equation. For $q\ne0$, total energy is $E=(q+2)\langle K\rangle/q$. A positive kinetic-temperature coefficient makes its <heat capacity> negative exactly when $-2<q<0$. For a pair potential proportional to $r^{p+1}$ this means $-3<p<-1$.

= Negative heat capacity of a virialized gravitational system
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$dE/d\Theta=-3Nk_B/2$}

An isolated Newtonian system in <virial equilibrium> has total <energy> $E=K+W=-K$. Along an equilibrium sequence at fixed particle number, define the kinetic <temperature> by $K=3Nk_B\Theta/2$. Its <heat capacity> is therefore negative, as displayed: losing total energy increases the kinetic temperature. A <collisionless stellar system> need not have a thermodynamic temperature; this is a kinetic-temperature statement unless thermal equilibrium is additionally specified. Boundary pressure and external confinement can change the energy relation.

= Radiative energy of virialized gravitational collapse
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$E_{\rm rad}\simeq3M\sigma^2/2$}

For an isolated final <stellar population> with isotropic one-dimensional <velocity dispersion> $\sigma$, kinetic energy is $K=3M\sigma^2/2$. The <virial theorem> gives $W=-2K$ and total energy $E=-K$. Collapse from a diffuse nearly unbound gas reservoir therefore requires removal of approximately $K$ of energy. The radiated fraction can differ if there are outflows, appreciable initial binding energy, or boundary stresses.

= Virial equilibrium
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$2K+W=0$}

A bounded gravitational system in statistical equilibrium satisfies the time-averaged <virial theorem>. For inverse-distance gravity with negligible boundary stresses, $2K+W=0$. This relation does not require static particles or a homogeneous density profile, and reaching it does not erase the system's dynamical history.

= Positive-energy linear diameter growth
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$\liminf_{t\to\infty}R(t)/t\geq\sqrt{E/(2A_1)}$}

For an isolated Newtonian point-mass system, $\ddot I=T+E\geq E>0$. Twice integrating gives a quadratic lower bound on $I$. The <maximum-separation inertia bounds> then give at-least-linear asymptotic growth of the diameter $R$ for a trajectory existing for arbitrarily large times. The bound does not assert instantaneous monotonicity or escape of every particle.

= Pairwise moment-of-inertia identity
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$I=\frac1{2M}\sum_{j<k}m_jm_kr_{jk}^2$}

For positive <masses> in their <centre of mass> frame, $\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k|\boldsymbol r_j-\boldsymbol r_k|^2=M\sum_km_kr_k^2$. Thus the scalar inertia $I=\tfrac12\sum_km_kr_k^2$ equals $(2M)^{-1}\sum_{j<k}m_jm_kr_{jk}^2$. The identity compares total spatial extent with pairwise separations.

= Maximum-separation inertia bounds
{parent=Pairwise moment-of-inertia identity}
{title2=$B_1R^2\leq I\leq A_1R^2$}

Let $R$ be the actual largest pairwise separation of positive <masses> in their <centre of mass> frame. The <pairwise moment-of-inertia identity> gives $m_{(1)}m_{(2)}R^2/(2M)\leq I\leq R^2\sum_{j<k}m_jm_k/(2M)$. Consequently the scalar inertia is comparable to $R^2$, independently of configuration.

= Virial temperature
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$T_{\rm vir}$}

The <virial temperature> characterizes the thermal energy of gas supported in a gravitational potential. It is of order $\mu m_pGM/(k_BR)$, with a structural coefficient determined by the density profile. For a uniform self-gravitating gas sphere the <virial theorem> gives $k_BT=GM\mu m_p/(5R)$; a conventional halo circular-speed definition often uses $k_BT\simeq\mu m_pv_c^2/2$.

= Virial mass contours in a cooling diagram
{parent=Virial temperature}
{title2=$n_H\propto T^3/M^2$}

Combining a virial-temperature relation with the density of a uniform self-gravitating sphere gives $M\propto T^{3/2}n_H^{-1/2}$. Thus contours of constant mass in a density-temperature plane satisfy $n_H\propto T^3/M^2$. Structural coefficients, composition and the distinction between baryonic and total gravitating mass matter when giving numerical labels.

= Fixed-energy virial constraint between steady states
{parent=Virial theorem}
{title2=$T=-E,\quad W=2E$}

Two finite isolated steady gravitational systems at the same conserved energy obey $2T+W=0$ at each endpoint, so both have $T=-E$ and $W=2E$. Redistribution can increase central concentration without making the global potential energy more negative. Escaping matter or an external reservoir requires a different energy accounting.

= Gravitational radius
{title2=$R_g$}
{parent=Virial theorem}
{wiki}

For a self-gravitating system of mass $M$ and <Newtonian gravitational potential energy> $W<0$, its gravitational radius is defined by
$$
R_g=-\frac{GM^2}{W}.
$$
It converts the total binding energy into a characteristic size without assuming a particular density profile.

= Gravitational rms escape and collapse speeds
{parent=Gravitational radius}
{title2=$\langle v_{\rm esc}^2\rangle=4\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle,\quad\langle v_{\rm coll}^2\rangle=2\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle$}

For identical point masses of total mass $M$, let $W=-GM^2/R_g$ exclude self-interactions. The <virial theorem> gives $\langle v_{\rm eq}^2\rangle=GM/R_g$. Since the sum of individual potential energies counts each pair twice, the mass-weighted mean frozen-potential <escape velocity> squared is $-4W/M$. A dissipation-free collapse from zero total <energy> instead has kinetic energy $-W$, hence mean squared speed $-2W/M$. These are three-dimensional speeds relative to the <centre of mass>; a coherent collapse speed is not a local random <velocity dispersion>.

\Include[hamiltonian-mechanics]{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Particle dynamics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

Particle dynamics studies motion determined by forces through Newton’s laws.

= Test particle
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

A <test particle> is an idealized body whose own fields and effect on its environment are negligible. Its motion probes an externally prescribed field. A gravitational <test particle> follows a <timelike geodesic> when nongravitational forces are absent; appreciable self-gravity requires a more complete treatment.

= One-dimensional conservative motion
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

For force minus the derivative of a potential, total kinetic plus potential energy is conserved.

= Period near a separatrix
{parent=One-dimensional conservative motion}
{wiki}

A trajectory approaching a <nondegenerate critical point>[nondegenerate] unstable maximum of its <potential energy> has a logarithmically divergent period.

= Simple harmonic motion
{parent=One-dimensional conservative motion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\ddot x+\omega^2x=0$}

= Simple harmonic oscillator
{synonym}

= Harmonic oscillator equation
{synonym}

= Harmonic oscillator
{synonym}

<Simple harmonic motion> is motion in which the <acceleration> is proportional to the <displacement> from <equilibrium> and points back towards it:
$$
\ddot x=-\omega^2x,\qquad \omega>0.
$$
Here $x$ is measured from <equilibrium>, and $\omega$ is the constant <angular frequency>. For a <point mass> attached to an ideal <spring>, <Hooke's law> gives the restoring <force> $F=-kx$, with stiffness $k>0$, and <Newton's second law> yields $\omega=\sqrt{k/m}$. The minus sign matters: for $x>0$ the <force> is negative, and for $x<0$ it is positive. A restoring <force> need not oppose <velocity>; on the return towards <equilibrium> it increases <speed>.

The general solution is
$$
x(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\varphi),\qquad
v(t)=-A\omega\sin(\omega t+\varphi),\qquad
P=\frac{2\pi}{\omega}.
$$
The <amplitude> $A\geq0$ and <oscillation phase> $\varphi$ encode the <initial conditions>. We use $P$ for the <oscillation period> to distinguish it from <kinetic energy> $T$. For $A>0$, the <oscillation period> is independent of <amplitude>, the property of <isochronous harmonic motion>. At $A=0$ the body remains at <equilibrium>. https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/mit8_01scs22_chapter23.pdf[MIT mechanics, Chapter 23, sections 23.2 and 23.3]{ref}

Choose $m=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$, $k=2\,\mathrm{N/m}$, and release the <mass> from rest at <displacement> $A=0.3\,\mathrm m$. Then $\omega=2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $P=\pi\,\mathrm s$, and $\varphi=0$. Writing <time> $t$ in seconds,
$$
x(t)=0.3\cos(2t)\,\mathrm m,\qquad
v(t)=-0.6\sin(2t)\,\mathrm{m/s}.
$$
At $t=0$ the <velocity> vanishes, but the <acceleration> is $-1.2\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$: a <turning point> is not a state of rest that persists. At $t=\pi/4\,\mathrm s$, the body crosses <equilibrium> with <velocity> $-0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and zero <acceleration>. At $t=\pi/2\,\mathrm s$ it reaches the opposite <turning point>, $x=-0.3\,\mathrm m$.

Taking <potential energy> zero at <equilibrium>, the same solution gives
$$
V(t)=\frac12kx(t)^2=0.09\cos^2(2t)\,\mathrm J,\qquad
T(t)=\frac12mv(t)^2=0.09\sin^2(2t)\,\mathrm J,
\qquad E=T+V=0.09\,\mathrm J.
$$
At each <turning point> all <mechanical energy> is <potential energy>; at each <equilibrium> crossing it is <kinetic energy>. Both repeat twice during one complete <position> cycle because squaring removes the sign. At $t=\pi/8\,\mathrm s$, for example, each contributes $0.045\,\mathrm J$.

\Image[mechanical-energy-oscillation.png]
{title=Position, velocity, phase ellipse and energy exchange for a mass of 0.5 kg on a spring of stiffness 2 N/m}

The physical <position> trace covers a line segment, $-0.3\leq x\leq0.3$ in metres. In the <phase plane>, the paired <position> and <velocity> instead trace the <ellipse>
$$
\frac{x^2}{(0.3\,\mathrm m)^2}+\frac{v^2}{(0.6\,\mathrm{m/s})^2}=1.
$$
This is precisely $mv^2/2+kx^2/2=E$, rewritten: the single conserved <mechanical energy> fixes the <amplitude> and the whole <ellipse>. It does not fix the starting <oscillation phase>. At $x=0$ both $v=-0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $v=+0.6\,\mathrm{m/s}$ lie on it; <position> alone cannot distinguish them. Replacing $v$ by <momentum> $p=mv$ gives the equivalent <phase-space trajectory>. The arrows show increasing <time>, clockwise in $(x,v)$ coordinates.

This closed <ellipse> assumes a time-independent <spring> and no <damping> or applied drive. Adding a resisting <force> $-bv$, $b>0$, gives $m\ddot x+b\dot x+kx=0$ and $dE/dt=-bv^2\leq0$. An underdamped <damped harmonic oscillator> spirals inward in the <phase plane>, crossing successively smaller <energy> contours. The ideal <harmonic oscillator> is also useful beyond <springs>: near a smooth <potential energy> minimum with $V''(0)>0$, its <Taylor expansion> begins with $V(0)+V''(0)x^2/2$. For sufficiently small <displacements>, this gives the same restoring <force> to leading order, explaining why <small oscillations> share one solvable pattern.

= Fixed-endpoint harmonic-oscillator action
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}
{title2=$S_{\rm cl}=\frac{\omega[(a^2+b^2)\cos\omega T-2ab]}{2\sin\omega T}$}

For $S=\tfrac12\int_0^T(\dot x^2-\omega^2x^2)dt$ and fixed endpoint values $a,b$, the <Euler-Lagrange equation> is $\ddot x+\omega^2x=0$. When $\sin\omega T\ne0$, solve the endpoint equations for the unique trajectory and use $\dot x^2-\omega^2x^2=(x\dot x)'$ on that trajectory to obtain the displayed <action>. For $\omega\ne0$, at $\omega T=k\pi$ a solution exists only if $b=(-1)^ka$; then there is a family of solutions and their action is zero. At $\omega=0$ the nonresonant formula has the limit $(b-a)^2/(2T)$.

= Quartic oscillator
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}

A quartic oscillator adds an anharmonic term to the quadratic potential, for example $H=(p^2+q^2)/2+\epsilon q^4$.

The quartic term is a specific <anharmonicity> of the oscillator potential.

= Isochronous harmonic motion
{parent=Simple harmonic motion}

Harmonic trajectories are isochronous: all nonconstant oscillations with a fixed frequency have the same period, regardless of amplitude.

= Collision mechanics
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

Collision mechanics uses impulse together with conservation of momentum and, for elastic collisions, kinetic energy.

= Coefficient of restitution
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}
{title2=$e=\frac{\text{normal separation speed}}{\text{normal approach speed}}$}

The restitution law applies to velocities of the actual contact points, including any rotational velocities of rigid bodies. A perfectly <elastic collision> has $e=1$; $e=0$ gives zero immediate normal separation speed, but does not by itself require all parts of colliding bodies to share the same velocity.

// Target: classical-mechanics.bigb

= Geometric bounce sequence
{parent=Coefficient of restitution}
{title2=$h_n=\gamma^{2n}h_0,\quad T_n=2\gamma^n\sqrt{2h_0/g}$}

For repeated vertical collisions with a fixed plate and constant <coefficient of restitution> $0\le\gamma<1$, each collision multiplies the upward speed by $\gamma$, so successive peak heights form the displayed <geometric sequence>. The initial fall plus both legs of every rebound have total distance $h_0(1+\gamma^2)/(1-\gamma^2)$. The idealized impact times accumulate in finite time when $\gamma<1$; real contact mechanics replaces the limiting sequence by rest.

= Collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}

A collision is an interaction between approaching bodies or particles that changes their motion. <Momentum conservation> and <conservation of energy> constrain the outcomes; <inelastic collisions> can transfer mechanical energy into internal degrees of freedom.

= Collision cross-section
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{title2=$\sigma_c$}

A collision cross-section is the effective area that converts a target <number density> and incident particle speed into a <collision> rate. It can depend on particle species, relative speed, and long-range forces, and need not equal a purely <geometric collision cross-section>.

= Impact parameter
{title2=$b$}
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

The impact parameter is the perpendicular distance between an incoming particle's asymptotic straight-line trajectory and the centre of a scattering potential. In central-force scattering it determines the deflection angle whenever the scattering map is single-valued.

= Geometric collision cross-section
{parent=Collision mechanics}

= Geometric cross-section
{synonym}

Two spheres of diameters $D_1,D_2$ collide geometrically when their projected centre separation is at most $(D_1+D_2)/2$, giving cross-section $\sigma=\pi(D_1+D_2)^2/4$.

= Cross-section per unit mass
{parent=Geometric collision cross-section}

For an equal-density sphere of diameter $D$, the ratio of geometric cross-section to mass is
$$
\frac{\pi D^2/4}{\pi\rho D^3/6}=\frac{3}{2\rho D}.
$$
Breaking a fixed mass into smaller pieces therefore increases its total cross-section in inverse proportion to fragment size.

= Gravitational focusing
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

Gravitational focusing increases a physical collision cross-section because mutual gravity bends initially non-colliding trajectories toward one another.

= Elastic collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

An <elastic collision> preserves total <kinetic energy> between the incoming and outgoing states. For isolated colliding bodies, <momentum conservation> also holds. The <kinetic energy> condition supplies information beyond <momentum conservation> alone. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-4-types-of-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.4: Types of Collisions]{ref}

Consider a stipulated one-dimensional example with rightward positive: $m_1=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, $m_2=1\,\mathrm{kg}$, incoming <velocities> $u_1=3\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and $u_2=0$. The total <momentum> is $P=6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$ and total <kinetic energy> is $K=9\,\mathrm J$. In <SI> numerical values, outgoing <velocities> $v_1,v_2$ satisfy
$$
2v_1+v_2=6,\qquad v_1^2+\frac12v_2^2=9.
$$
Substituting $v_2=6-2v_1$ gives $3(v_1-1)(v_1-3)=0$. The root $v_1=3$, $v_2=0$ repeats the incoming state; the outgoing separating state is $v_1=1\,\mathrm{m/s}$, $v_2=4\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Its <momenta> add to $2+4=6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$ and its <kinetic energies> add to $1+8=9\,\mathrm J$. The incoming and outgoing <relative velocities> both have magnitude $3\,\mathrm{m/s}$, consistent with <coefficient of restitution> $e=1$.

\Image[momentum-collision-before-after.png]
{title=The same incoming momentum gives different elastic and sticking collision outcomes}

The <inelastic collision> example below uses exactly these incoming data. Both outcomes preserve total <momentum>, while only the <elastic collision> preserves total <kinetic energy>. These are ideal numerical examples, not experimental measurements.

= Inelastic collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

An <inelastic collision> changes total <kinetic energy>. In the dissipative case it decreases, transferring <energy> to <internal energy>, including <deformation> and microscopic motion. Isolated colliding bodies still obey <momentum conservation>; <conservation of energy> requires accounting for the transferred <energy>. Bodies need not stick in every <inelastic collision>. The decrease in translational <kinetic energy> drives <dissipative spreading of a planetary ring>.

For the incoming <masses> and <velocities> in the <elastic collision> example, stipulate that the bodies stick. This <perfectly inelastic collision> has a shared outgoing <velocity>
$$
v=\frac{m_1u_1+m_2u_2}{m_1+m_2}
=\frac{6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}}{3\,\mathrm{kg}}
=2\,\mathrm{m/s}.
$$
The outgoing <momenta> are $4$ and $2\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$, totaling $6\,\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. The outgoing <kinetic energies> are $4$ and $2\,\mathrm J$, totaling $6\,\mathrm J$. Thus $9-6=3\,\mathrm J$ of <kinetic energy> becomes <internal energy> within the isolated pair. Nothing in <momentum conservation> requires this $3\,\mathrm J$ to remain as <kinetic energy>. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-4-types-of-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.4: elastic and perfectly inelastic outcomes]{ref}

= Perfectly inelastic collision
{parent=Inelastic collision}

A <perfectly inelastic collision> is an <inelastic collision> in which the bodies stick and share one outgoing <velocity>. For two isolated <point masses>, <momentum conservation> gives $v=(m_1u_1+m_2u_2)/(m_1+m_2)$. At fixed total <momentum>, common <velocity> minimizes the outgoing translational <kinetic energy>, so sticking gives the greatest loss of that <kinetic energy> for the specified incoming state.

= Oblique collision
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{wiki}

In a frictionless oblique collision, impulse acts along the common normal and tangential velocity components remain unchanged.

= Impulse
{parent=Collision mechanics}
{title2=$\mathbf J$}
{wiki}

The <impulse> of a <force> over a <time> interval is its <vector> <integral>:
$$
\mathbf J=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\mathbf F(t)\,dt.
$$
Its <International System of Units> unit is $\mathrm{N\,s}=\mathrm{kg\,m/s}$. The <impulse-momentum theorem> equates the net <impulse> to the change in <momentum>. For one fixed-<mass> <point mass>, integrate its net <force>; for a closed collection obeying <Newton's third law>, integrate the sum of external <forces>. In one dimension, the signed area under the <force> versus <time> graph gives the <impulse>; negative <force> contributes negative area. https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/9-2-impulse-and-collisions[OpenStax University Physics Volume 1, section 9.2: Impulse and Collisions]{ref}

Three stipulated rightward net <force> pulses illustrate the same $J=6\,\mathrm{N\,s}$: constant $12\,\mathrm N$ for $0.5\,\mathrm s$, a triangle rising from zero to $24\,\mathrm N$ at $0.25\,\mathrm s$ and falling to zero at $0.5\,\mathrm s$, and constant $3\,\mathrm N$ for $2\,\mathrm s$. Their areas are
$$
(12\,\mathrm N)(0.5\,\mathrm s)
=\frac12(24\,\mathrm N)(0.5\,\mathrm s)
=(3\,\mathrm N)(2\,\mathrm s)
=6\,\mathrm{N\,s}.
$$
Applied separately to the same fixed <mass> $m=2\,\mathrm{kg}$, each gives $\Delta v=J/m=3\,\mathrm{m/s}$. Their peak <forces> differ: $12$, $24$, and $3\,\mathrm N$. Thus <impulse> fixes the final <momentum> change, while the <force> history determines how abruptly it occurs.

\Image[impulse-force-pulses.png]
{title=Three force pulses have the same impulse but different durations and peak forces}

The mean net <force> over the chosen interval is $\overline{\mathbf F}=\mathbf J/(t_f-t_i)$. Extending the interval for a fixed <impulse> reduces this mean <force>. A lower peak <force> additionally depends on the pulse shape: the two short pulses above have the same mean $12\,\mathrm N$ but different peaks. This explains why controlling the duration and shape of a stopping <impulse> matters in cushioning a <collision>.

= Impulse-momentum theorem
{parent=Impulse}
{title2=$\mathbf J_{\mathrm{ext}}=\Delta\mathbf P$}

Integrating the closed-collection balance in <momentum conservation> and applying the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives
$$
\mathbf J_{\mathrm{ext}}
=\int_{t_i}^{t_f}\sum_i\mathbf F_i^{\mathrm{ext}}(t)\,dt
=\mathbf P(t_f)-\mathbf P(t_i).
$$
For one fixed-<mass> <point mass>, this becomes $\mathbf J_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta\mathbf p=m\Delta\mathbf v$. Zero external <impulse> implies equal initial and final total <momenta>; <momentum conservation> throughout the interval requires zero net external <force> throughout it.

= Freely expanding rotating particle ring
{parent=Particle dynamics}

Particles initially on a circle of radius $R$ with common tangential speed $v$ move freely on tangent lines. They remain on a circle of radius
$$
R(t)=\sqrt{R^2+v^2t^2},
$$
rotated through $\theta(t)=\arctan(vt/R)$, while total angular momentum remains $mRv$.

= Relative velocity
{parent=Particle dynamics}
{wiki}

The relative velocity of body 1 with respect to body 2 is $\mathbf v_{12}=\mathbf v_1-\mathbf v_2$.

\Include[celestial-mechanics]{parent=Classical mechanics}
\Include[rigid-body-dynamics]{parent=Classical mechanics}

= Lagrangian mechanics
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{c}
{wiki}

Lagrangian mechanics derives motion from the stationary action of $L=T-V$ through the Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Cartan form of a regular mechanical Lagrangian
{c}
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{title2=$\theta_L=L_{v^i}\,dq^i,\quad\omega_L=-d\theta_L$}

A <regular Lagrangian> $L:TQ\to\mathbb R$ defines a fibre derivative $\mathbb FL:TQ\to T^*Q$ and the Cartan one-form $\theta_L=(\mathbb FL)^*\theta$. Its two-form $\omega_L=-d\theta_L$ is nondegenerate when the velocity Hessian is invertible. The energy is $E_L=v^iL_{v^i}-L$. The equation $\iota_{\Gamma_L}\omega_L=dE_L$ determines the Euler–Lagrange vector field. A <hyperregular Lagrangian> makes the fibre derivative a global diffeomorphism and transports this dynamics to Hamiltonian phase space.

= Helical symmetry of a potential
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

A potential $V(r,\phi,z)=f(r,\phi-kz)$ is invariant under the screw transformation $(\phi,z)\mapsto(\phi+ks,z+s)$. The <Noether conserved quantity for a mechanical point symmetry> is
$$
p_z+kp_\phi,
$$
a linear combination of axial <linear momentum> and axial <angular momentum>.

= Cyclic coordinate
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki}

A generalized coordinate absent from the <Lagrangian> is cyclic. Its conjugate <generalized momentum> is conserved by the <Euler-Lagrange equations>.

= Action
{title2=$S$}
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki=Action_(physics)}

= Action functional
{synonym}

The action of a path is the time integral of its <Lagrangian>. The <principle of stationary action> requires its first variation to vanish for every admissible fixed-endpoint variation.

= Principle of stationary action
{parent=Action}
{wiki}

The principle of stationary action says that the physical path between fixed endpoints is a <stationary point> of the <action>.

= Total-time-derivative invariance of a Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

Replacing a Lagrangian by
$$
L'(q,\dot q,t)=L(q,\dot q,t)+\frac d{dt}F(q,t)
$$
adds only the endpoint term $F(q(t_2),t_2)-F(q(t_1),t_1)$ to the action. Fixed-endpoint variations therefore give the same Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

For electromagnetic scalar and vector potentials $\phi$ and $A$,
$$
L=\frac12m\dot r^2-q\phi+q\dot r\cdot A.
$$
With
$$
E=-\nabla\phi-\partial_tA,
\qquad
B=\nabla\times A,
$$
the Euler--Lagrange equations give the Lorentz force $m\ddot r=q(E+\dot r\times B)$, while the canonical momentum is $p=m\dot r+qA$.

= Hamiltonian of a charged particle in an electromagnetic field
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

The <Legendre transform> of the <charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian> is
$$
H(\mathbf x,\mathbf p,t)=\frac{[\mathbf p-q\mathbf A(\mathbf x,t)]^2}{2m}+q\phi(\mathbf x,t).
$$

= Gauge variation of the charged-particle Lagrangian
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

Under $\phi\mapsto\phi-\partial_tf$ and $A\mapsto A+\nabla f$, the charged-particle Lagrangian changes by
$$
L' - L=q\bigl(\partial_tf+\dot r\cdot\nabla f\bigr)
=q\frac{df(r(t),t)}{dt}.
$$
It therefore has unchanged equations of motion by total-time-derivative invariance.

= Canonical momentum parallel to a uniform magnetic field
{parent=Charged-particle electromagnetic Lagrangian}

For a constant uniform magnetic field $B$, choose the symmetric gauge $A=\tfrac12B\times r$. The coordinate parallel to $B$ is cyclic, so its canonical momentum is conserved. Since $A\cdot B=0$, this component is also the mechanical momentum $m\dot r\cdot\widehat B$.

= Generalized momentum
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

The generalized, or conjugate, momentum associated with a coordinate $q_i$ is
$$
p_i=\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_i}.
$$

In <Lagrangian mechanics>, the coordinate variable describes the configuration; its conjugate momentum need not equal the Cartesian mechanical momentum.

= Ignorable coordinate
{parent=Generalized momentum}

A coordinate $q_i$ is ignorable, or cyclic, when $\partial L/\partial q_i=0$. The Euler--Lagrange equation then gives $\dot p_i=0$, so its generalized momentum is conserved.

= Conserved quantities from cyclic coordinates
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

If a Lagrangian is independent of a coordinate, the conjugate momentum to that coordinate is conserved by the Euler--Lagrange equations.

= Axially symmetric isotropic-mass Lagrangian
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

If $L=|\dot q|^2/2-V(q)$ and $V$ depends only on $|q|^2$ and $(q\cdot n)^2$, then rotations about the axis $n$ leave $L$ invariant. The quantity supplied by <Noether theorem> is the axial angular momentum
$$
J_n=n\cdot(q\times\dot q).
$$
If $L$ also has no explicit time dependence, the energy $|\dot q|^2/2+V(q)$ is conserved independently.

= Double pendulum
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}
{wiki}

A double pendulum consists of two pendula coupled because the second pivot moves with the first mass.

= Double pendulum Lagrangian
{parent=Double pendulum}

The kinetic energy contains the coupling $ml_1l_2\cos(\theta_1-\theta_2)\dot\theta_1\dot\theta_2$, while gravity supplies one cosine potential for each mass height.

= Double pendulum equations of motion
{parent=Double pendulum}

The nonlinear Euler--Lagrange equations couple both angular accelerations and contain centrifugal terms quadratic in angular velocity.

= Kinetic energy from Cartesian position vectors
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

For point masses with generalized coordinates, kinetic energy follows by differentiating their Cartesian position vectors and summing $m_i|\dot r_i|^2/2$.

= Small oscillation
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

Near a stable equilibrium, retaining quadratic Lagrangian terms gives a linear system $\mathsf M\ddot z+\mathsf Kz=0$.

The resulting linearized motion decomposes into <normal modes> near the stable equilibrium.

= Stable equilibrium in Lagrangian mechanics
{parent=Small oscillation}

At an equilibrium, a positive-definite <Hessian matrix> of the potential gives stable <small oscillations>. If a continuous symmetry supplies zero modes, positive definiteness on the directions transverse to the symmetry gives stability of the relative configuration.

= Angular frequency
{parent=Small oscillation}
{wiki}

Angular frequency measures phase advance per unit time. For $x(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\varphi)$ it is $\omega=2\pi/T$, where $T$ is the period.

= Two vertically suspended springs
{parent=Small oscillation}

For two masses hung in series from springs of natural length $\ell$, the lower equilibrium spring tension is $m_2g$ and the upper tension is $(m_1+m_2)g$. Hence their equilibrium lengths are
$$
L_2=\ell+\frac{m_2g}{k_2},
\qquad
L_1=\ell+\frac{(m_1+m_2)g}{k_1}.
$$

= Vertical normal modes of two equal suspended masses
{parent=Two vertically suspended springs}

For equal masses $m$ and spring constants $k$, vertical displacements from equilibrium have stiffness and mass matrices
$$
K=k\begin{pmatrix}2&-1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad M=mI.
$$
The two squared frequencies and displacement ratios are
$$
\omega_\pm^2=\frac{k}{2m}(3\pm\sqrt5),
\qquad
\frac{\eta_2}{\eta_1}=\frac{1\mp\sqrt5}{2}.
$$
Gravity shifts the equilibrium but does not enter these vertical frequencies.

= Small-oscillation mass and stiffness matrices
{parent=Small oscillation}

The Hessians of kinetic and potential energy at equilibrium give the symmetric mass matrix $\mathsf M$ and stiffness matrix $\mathsf K$.

= Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations
{parent=Small oscillation}

Normal frequencies satisfy $\det(\mathsf K-\Omega^2\mathsf M)=0$, and each null vector gives the fixed amplitude ratio of a mode.

= Mass-matrix orthogonality of normal modes
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For symmetric stiffness matrix $K$ and positive-definite mass matrix $M$, eigenvectors $a_i,a_j$ of
$$
Ka=\omega^2Ma
$$
with distinct squared frequencies satisfy
$$
a_i^TMa_j=0.
$$
Thus normal modes are orthogonal in the kinetic-energy inner product.

= Normal modes of a double pendulum
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For equal lengths and mass ratio $\mu=m/M$, the squared frequencies are $\omega_0^2(1+\mu\pm\sqrt{\mu(1+\mu)})$.

= Square-root splitting of nearly degenerate frequencies
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

A weakly coupled degenerate pair can split by order $\sqrt\mu$ when the small parameter enters a singular mass or stiffness matrix.

= Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

If the mass and stiffness matrices commute with a <permutation representation>, each symmetry subspace is invariant under the <generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations>. On an irreducible subspace, <Schur lemma> forces a commuting operator to be scalar, producing a degenerate <normal mode> multiplet.

= Normal modes of three equal masses with a symmetric gap potential
{parent=Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry}

Let three equal masses move on a circle of radius $r$, and let the potential be $U(\alpha)+U(\beta)+U(\gamma)$ with $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2\pi$. At equal spacing $s_0=2\pi/3$, angular displacements have a zero-frequency rigid-rotation mode $(1,1,1)$ and a two-dimensional sum-zero eigenspace with
$$
\omega^2=\frac{3U''(s_0)}{mr^2}.
$$
The two vibrational modes are degenerate by the permutation symmetry.

= Normal modes of three symmetrically coupled trapped masses
{parent=Degenerate normal modes from permutation symmetry}

For three unit masses with unit springs between every pair and one unit spring anchoring each mass to the origin,
$$
M=I,\qquad K=4I-J,
$$
where $J$ is the all-ones matrix. The collective mode $(1,1,1)$ has angular frequency $1$, while the two-dimensional sum-zero subspace has angular frequency $2$.

= Normal modes of two equal masses between three springs
{parent=Generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations}

For equal masses $m$, wall-spring constants $k_1,k_2$, and coupling-spring constant $k_3$, the <small-oscillation mass and stiffness matrices> are
$$
\mathsf M=mI,
\qquad
\mathsf K=
\begin{pmatrix}
k_1+k_3&-k_3\\
-k_3&k_2+k_3
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
If $k_1=k(1+\varepsilon\delta)$, $k_2=k(1-\varepsilon\delta)$, and $k_3=k\varepsilon$, the squared normal frequencies are
$$
\omega_\pm^2=\frac{k}{m}
\left[1+\varepsilon\left(1\pm\sqrt{1+\delta^2}\right)\right].
$$

= Conservation of energy from time-translation invariance
{parent=Lagrangian mechanics}

A Lagrangian with no explicit time dependence conserves $E=\sum_i\dot q_i\,\partial L/\partial\dot q_i-L$.

= Center of mass
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Centres of mass
{synonym}

= Centre of mass
{synonym}

For masses $m_i$ at positions $\mathbf r_i$, the center of mass is $\mathbf R=(\sum_i m_i\mathbf r_i)/(\sum_i m_i)$, with the analogous mass-density integral for a continuous body.

= Barycenter
{disambiguate=astronomy}
{parent=Center of mass}
{wiki}

= Astronomical barycenter
{synonym}

The <centre of mass> of an astronomical system. In an isolated <two-body problem>, both bodies follow <orbits> around their shared astronomical barycenter; the <Kepler orbit> of their relative displacement has a <semi-major axis> equal to the sum of their individual <semi-major axes>.

= Centre of mass after removing material
{parent=Center of mass}
{title2=$\mathbf R=(M_0\mathbf R_0-M_c\mathbf R_c)/(M_0-M_c)$}

Suppose a body of <mass> $M_0$ has <centre of mass> $\mathbf R_0$, and material of <mass> $M_c<M_0$ with <centre of mass> $\mathbf R_c$ is removed. Additivity of the first <mass> moment gives the remaining <centre of mass>
$$
\mathbf R=\frac{M_0\mathbf R_0-M_c\mathbf R_c}{M_0-M_c}.
$$
When the original <centre of mass> is at the origin, the remaining <centre of mass> points opposite the removed material's first <mass> moment. For a constrained <rigid body> rotating about a fixed axis, this displaced <centre of mass> undergoes <centripetal acceleration> even if the applied axial <torque> is zero.

= Center-of-mass motion
{parent=Center of mass}

The center of mass moves as if the total external force acted on the total mass; internal forces cancel in its equation.

= Center-of-mass reduction
{parent=Center-of-mass motion}

Center-of-mass reduction separates translational motion from internal coordinates by using the conserved or externally forced center coordinate.

= Kinetic-energy decomposition about the centre of mass
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}
{title2=$T=\tfrac12M|\dot{\mathbf R}|^2+\tfrac12\sum_i m_i|\dot{\mathbf y}_i|^2$}

For fixed particle <masses> and positive total <mass>, write $\mathbf x_i=\mathbf R+\mathbf y_i$ with $\mathbf R$ the <centre of mass>. The weighted relative <velocities> sum to zero, so expanding the <kinetic energy> eliminates the mixed term and yields the displayed decomposition. For a <rigid body>, the relative contribution is the <rotational kinetic energy> $I\omega^2/2$ about the chosen centre-of-mass axis.

= Jacobi coordinates
{c}
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}
{wiki}

For particles with masses $m_j$ and positions $r_j$, <Jacobi coordinates> consist of the total <center of mass> and successive relative coordinates $\rho_j=r_{j+1}-(\sum_{l=1}^j m_l r_l)/(\sum_{l=1}^j m_l)$. They separate translation from internal motion and diagonalize the <kinetic energy> with the corresponding reduced masses.

= Jacobi coordinates for three particles
{c}
{parent=Jacobi coordinates}

For three particles, choose the relative position of particles one and two, the position of their center of mass relative to particle three, and the total center of mass. The kinetic energy diagonalizes with masses
$$
\frac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2},
\qquad
\frac{(m_1+m_2)m_3}{m_1+m_2+m_3},
\qquad
m_1+m_2+m_3.
$$

= Angular momentum decomposition in three-body Jacobi coordinates
{parent=Jacobi coordinates for three particles}

For Jacobi coordinates $\mathbf a,\mathbf b,\mathbf c$ and their corresponding reduced masses $\alpha,\beta,\gamma$, total angular momentum is
$$
\mathbf L
=\alpha\mathbf a\times\dot{\mathbf a}
+\beta\mathbf b\times\dot{\mathbf b}
+\gamma\mathbf c\times\dot{\mathbf c}.
$$

= Collective rotation and gap coordinates on a circle
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=Center-of-mass reduction}

For three ordered angular positions $q_1,q_2,q_3$, define consecutive gaps
$$
\alpha=q_2-q_1,
\qquad
\beta=q_3-q_2,
\qquad
\gamma=2\pi-q_3+q_1
$$
and the collective angle $\Phi=(q_1+q_2+q_3)/3$. Then $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=2\pi$. For three equal masses on a circle of radius $r$,
$$
T=\frac{3mr^2}{2}\dot\Phi^2
+\frac{mr^2}{3}
\left(\dot\alpha^2+\dot\alpha\dot\beta+\dot\beta^2\right).
$$
Thus the <ignorable coordinate> $\Phi$ decouples from the relative gap dynamics whenever the potential depends only on the gaps.

= Reduced mass
{title2=$\mu$}
{parent=Center of mass}
{wiki}

The <reduced mass> is the effective <mass> for the <two-particle relative coordinate> of two bodies with constant positive <masses> $m_1,m_2$:
$$
\mu=\frac{m_1m_2}{m_1+m_2},\qquad \frac1\mu=\frac1{m_1}+\frac1{m_2}.
$$
Both bodies can respond to their mutual <force>, so their relative <acceleration> is greater than if one were fixed. Consequently $0<\mu<\min(m_1,m_2)$. This explains why using either individual <mass>, or their sum, generally gives the wrong internal <oscillation> rate. The same <reduced mass> describes a <two-body problem>, a <free two-mass spring>, and the approximate <molecular vibration> of a <diatomic molecule>.<Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick>[Reduced Mass, equations 322–328: equal-opposite forces, relative acceleration and reduced mass]{ref}

For equal <masses> $m_1=m_2=m$, $\mu=m/2$. Two $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ bodies therefore have $\mu=0.5\,\mathrm{kg}$: in their stationary <centre of mass> frame a relative <speed> $2\,\mathrm{m/s}$ means individual <speeds> $1\,\mathrm{m/s}$ and total <kinetic energy> $1\,\mathrm J$. With $m_1=1\,\mathrm{kg}$ and $m_2=100\,\mathrm{kg}$, instead $\mu=100/101\,\mathrm{kg}\approx0.990099\,\mathrm{kg}$. At the same relative <speed>, the individual <speeds> are $200/101$ and $2/101\,\mathrm{m/s}$, and the total <kinetic energy> is $200/101\,\mathrm J$. These are calculations for stipulated <masses> and <velocities>.

For fixed $m_1$ and $q=m_2/m_1>0$, $\mu/m_1=q/(1+q)$. The heavy-partner limit $q\to\infty$ gives $\mu\to m_1$ from below; the relative error incurred by replacing $\mu$ with $m_1$ is $(m_1-\mu)/\mu=1/q$. At $q=100$ this is $1\%$. Treating the heavy body as fixed is thus a controlled approximation to internal motion, rather than an exact consequence of its being heavier.

= Two-particle relative coordinate
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1$}

Choose <positions> in one <inertial frame> and let $M=m_1+m_2$. The <centre of mass> and <two-particle relative coordinate> are
$$
\mathbf R=\frac{m_1\mathbf r_1+m_2\mathbf r_2}{M},\qquad \mathbf r=\mathbf r_2-\mathbf r_1,\qquad
\mathbf r_1=\mathbf R-\frac{m_2}{M}\mathbf r,\quad \mathbf r_2=\mathbf R+\frac{m_1}{M}\mathbf r.
$$
The lighter body is farther from the <centre of mass>. Reconstructing both <positions> from these equations is part of solving the <two-body problem>; $\mathbf r$ alone describes their separation, not either body's laboratory <position>.<Newtonian Dynamics by Richard Fitzpatrick>[Reduced Mass, equations 324–326: centre and reconstruction of both particle positions]{ref}

\Image[reduced-mass-center-of-mass-relative-motion.png]
{id=image-reduced-mass-center-of-mass-relative-motion}
{title=<Centre of mass> translation and internal <two-particle relative coordinate> for a pair with mass ratio three}
{description=Original illustrative <free two-mass spring> model, with $m_2=3m_1$, equilibrium separation $\ell$, relative extension $s/\ell=0.3\cos\tau$, $\tau=\omega t$, and $R/\ell=0.08\tau$. The right plot subtracts both the translating <centre of mass> and each body's equilibrium offset. It shows internal <displacements>, not full distances from the <centre of mass>.}

Substitute the reconstructed <velocities> into the <kinetic energy>. The result is the exact two-particle instance of <kinetic-energy decomposition about the centre of mass>:
$$
T=\frac12m_1|\dot{\mathbf r}_1|^2+\frac12m_2|\dot{\mathbf r}_2|^2
=\frac12M|\dot{\mathbf R}|^2+\frac12\mu|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2.
$$
The mixed <dot product> has coefficient $-m_1m_2/M+m_2m_1/M=0$; the coefficient of $|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2$ is $(m_1m_2^2+m_2m_1^2)/M^2=\mu$. This algebra needs constant positive <masses> but no particular <force> law. In the stationary <centre of mass> frame, the fraction of internal <kinetic energy> carried by body 1 is $m_2/M$, and by body 2 is $m_1/M$: the lighter body carries more.

For a closed pair with no external <forces>, write $\mathbf F$ for the <force> on body 2 and $-\mathbf F$ for that on body 1, as required by <Newton's third law>. Adding and subtracting their <Newton's second law> equations yields
$$
M\ddot{\mathbf R}=\mathbf0,\qquad
\ddot{\mathbf r}=\left(\frac1{m_2}+\frac1{m_1}\right)\mathbf F,\qquad
\mu\ddot{\mathbf r}=\mathbf F.
$$
Thus the <centre of mass> moves uniformly while the <two-particle relative coordinate> behaves like one body of <reduced mass>. Equal-opposite internal <forces> suffice for these equations. For an internal <central force>, $\mathbf F=f(r)\widehat{\mathbf r}$ with $r=|\mathbf r|>0$, the internal <torque> vanishes and <conservation of angular momentum> gives constant $\mathbf L_{\rm rel}=\mu\mathbf r\times\dot{\mathbf r}$. If the <central force> comes from a time-independent <potential energy> $U(r)$, then $f(r)=-dU/dr$ and the internal <mechanical energy> $E_{\rm rel}=\mu|\dot{\mathbf r}|^2/2+U(r)$ is conserved. The <kinetic energy> split alone does not establish either conservation law.

= Free two-mass spring
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\omega=\sqrt{k/\mu}$}

Take two <point masses> joined by a massless <spring> of <spring constant> $k>0$ and equilibrium length $\ell>0$. Assume one-dimensional motion along the <spring>, no external <force>, no <damping>, and an extension $s=x_2-x_1-\ell$ satisfying <Hooke's law>; keep $x_2>x_1$. For a real <spring> this is a model within its elastic range. Its <potential energy> is $U=ks^2/2$, so
$$
\mu\ddot s=-ks,\qquad s(t)=A\cos(\omega t+\phi),\qquad
\omega=\sqrt{\frac{k}{\mu}}=\sqrt{k\left(\frac1{m_1}+\frac1{m_2}\right)},\qquad \nu=\frac\omega{2\pi}.
$$
Here $\omega$ is <angular frequency> and $\nu$ is <frequency>. Choose $|A|<\ell$ so the model bodies do not cross. The internal <normal mode> has <displacements> $u_1=-m_2s/M$ and $u_2=m_1s/M$ about equilibrium offsets from the <centre of mass>; its <amplitude> ratio is $|u_1|/|u_2|=m_2/m_1$. The other <normal mode> is collective translation with zero <frequency> and no <spring> extension. This free pair has different boundary conditions from <normal modes of two equal masses between three springs>.

For $k=10\,\mathrm{N/m}$ and two $1\,\mathrm{kg}$ <masses>, $\omega=\sqrt{20}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\approx4.47214\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $\nu\approx0.711763\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Replacing the second <mass> by $100\,\mathrm{kg}$ gives $\omega=\sqrt{10.1}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}\approx3.17805\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ and $\nu\approx0.505802\,\mathrm{Hz}$. Fixing the second endpoint instead would give $\sqrt{10}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$: the free heavy partner still moves. The free pair's <angular frequency> exceeds that fixed-end limit by the factor $\sqrt{1+1/q}$, about $1.004988$ at $q=100$.

\Image[reduced-mass-ratio-spring-frequency.png]
{id=image-reduced-mass-ratio-spring-frequency}
{title=<Reduced mass> and <free two-mass spring> <angular frequency> as the partner becomes heavier}
{description=Original dimensionless model with $m_1$ and $k$ held fixed, $q=m_2/m_1>0$, $\mu/m_1=q/(1+q)$ and $\omega/\sqrt{k/m_1}=\sqrt{1+1/q}$. The marks at $q=1$ and $q=100$ correspond to the computed examples; curves are predictions, not measured data.}

= Isotope scaling of diatomic vibration
{parent=Reduced mass}
{title2=$\nu'/\nu\simeq\sqrt{\mu/\mu'}$}

Near a stable equilibrium <bond length> $r_e$, a <Taylor expansion> of a <diatomic molecule>'s <potential energy> gives $U(r)\simeq U(r_e)+k(r-r_e)^2/2$, with $k=U''(r_e)>0$. Treat the internal stretch as a <harmonic oscillator> of <reduced mass>, initially omitting <molecular rotation>. If replacing an <isotope> leaves this local <potential energy> curvature unchanged, then
$$
\frac{\nu'}\nu\simeq\sqrt{\frac\mu{\mu'}},\qquad
\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}=\frac1{2\pi c}\sqrt{\frac{k}{\mu}}.
$$
Here $c$ is the <speed of light> and $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$ a <spectroscopic wavenumber>; use consistent length units for $c$ and $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$. The <quantum harmonic oscillator> gives adjacent <energy eigenvalues> separated by $h\nu$, with $h$ the <Planck constant>. Consequently a vibrational <photon absorption> can probe the same <frequency> scale, subject to <electric-dipole selection rules>.

For an explicitly approximate <isotope> example, assign the <atomic masses> of $^{1}\mathrm H$, $^{2}\mathrm H$, $^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$, and $^{37}\mathrm{Cl}$ the mass-number values $1u,2u,35u,37u$, respectively, with $u$ the <unified atomic mass unit>. These are stipulated approximations to individual <atomic masses>, not measured <masses> of <molecules> or natural-abundance averages. They give
$$
\mu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}=\frac{35}{36}u,\quad
\mu_{\mathrm D^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}=\frac{70}{37}u,\quad
\frac{\nu_{\mathrm D^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}{\nu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}\simeq\sqrt{\frac{37}{72}}\approx0.716860,
\qquad
\frac{\nu_{\mathrm H^{37}\mathrm{Cl}}}{\nu_{\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}}}\simeq\sqrt{\frac{665}{666}}\approx0.999249.
$$
The symbol $\mathrm D$ means <deuterium>, $^{2}\mathrm H$. Doubling the light partner's approximate <atomic mass> therefore produces a large predicted shift, whereas changing the already heavy <chlorine> partner by two mass units produces a much smaller one. A quantitative comparison with <infrared spectroscopy> requires actual <isotope>-specific <atomic masses>, fitted band parameters, and uncertainties, rather than identifying any arbitrary peak with $\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm}$.

= HCl and DCl vibrational isotope experiment
{c}
{parent=Isotope scaling of diatomic vibration}

An actual <infrared spectroscopy> route is to compare gaseous <hydrogen chloride> containing ordinary <hydrogen> with its <deuterium> counterpart. A documented teaching experiment uses a stopcock-equipped gas cell with potassium bromide windows and a Nicolet Avatar instrument for <Fourier transform spectroscopy>. A nitrogen-purged flask and drying tube supply the mixture; the sample also contains sulfur dioxide. An empty sample-chamber background precedes the filled-cell scan. The <absorbance> trace and peak-picked <spectroscopic wavenumbers> are the readout. The analysis assigns and indexes $^{1}\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ and $^{2}\mathrm H^{35}\mathrm{Cl}$ transitions separately and propagates transition uncertainties. The instructions distinguish resolvable <chlorine> <isotope> peaks from adequate precision for the intended parameter analysis. <Vibrational-Rotational Spectroscopy of HCl by James M. McCormick>[McCormick, Introduction; Experimental, Laboratory Component, Figures 1–2 and background/peak-picking paragraphs; Analysis and Discussion, first three paragraphs]{ref}

A separate published teaching dataset reports a Nicolet Nexus 670 instrument, a $10\,\mathrm{cm}$ gas cell at $20\,\mathrm{Torr}$, and <chlorine> <isotope> peaks resolved to baseline. These apparatus details describe that dataset, not the preceding experiment. <HCl and DCl IR absorption spectra by Jonathan Gutow>[Gutow, HCl and DCl IR absorption spectra, dataset description: instrument, path length, pressure and resolved chlorine isotope peaks]{ref}

The inference connects several models. Fit correctly assigned transitions to separate <molecular rotation> from <molecular vibration>, then compare the inferred vibrational scales using <isotope scaling of diatomic vibration>. Agreement supports the approximate separation between a nearly unchanged <chemical bond> stiffness and a changed nuclear <mass>. It does not make a real <molecule> an exact classical <spring>: <anharmonicity> shifts the fundamental transition away from the local <harmonic oscillator> scale, and coupling to <molecular rotation> shifts individual lines.

For an independent test, calculate the <reduced masses> from the specified <isotopes> and compare the inferred values $k=\mu(2\pi c\widetilde\nu_{\rm harm})^2$. Agreement within uncertainty tests the common-curvature assumption; choosing separate $k$ values just to fit each <frequency> would not test it. The documented background addresses instrument and ambient contributions, but does not by itself subtract window absorption, identify contaminating species, or calibrate the <spectroscopic wavenumber> scale. Those require separate controls. Finite spectral resolution, blended peaks, incorrect <isotope> assignments, and departure from the <harmonic approximation near a potential minimum> limit the comparison. No numerical measured line positions are assumed here.

= Uniform gravitational field
{parent=Classical mechanics}

In a uniform gravitational field, every freely falling center of mass has constant acceleration $-g$.

= Normal coordinate
{parent=Classical mechanics}

A normal coordinate diagonalizes the linearized kinetic and potential forms so that its motion decouples as a harmonic oscillator.

= Antisymmetric molecular vibration
{parent=Normal coordinate}

In an antisymmetric vibration of a symmetric linear triatomic molecule, the outer atoms move oppositely and the central atom does not couple to that mode.

= Virtual work
{parent=Classical mechanics}
{wiki}

= Principle of virtual work
{synonym}

Virtual work is the work of applied forces through an infinitesimal displacement compatible with the system's instantaneous constraints. Equilibrium or overdamped force balance requires the total virtual work to vanish for every admissible virtual displacement.
